      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>SHOP ASSISTANTS' GRIEVANCES</head>
        <p>SHOP ASSISTANTS' GRIEVANCES</p>
        <p>Protest Against Radius Agreements.</p>
        <p>Radius agreements formed the subject of severe criticism and strong protest at a meeting of shop assis- tants and Labour men held in the Bold-street Wesleyan schools on Tuesday evening. Councillor Ball presided over a fair attendance.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN, expressing regret that the attendance was not larger, said they had to recognize that the movement in Warrington, as in other towns., required a good deal of engineering and developing. The meet- ing was called to protest against certain conditions which were often practised and imposed in many public companies that cmployed large numbers of shop assis- tants. He understood they had a number of those shops in Warrington, and unless the shop assistants were pre- pared to organize and combine for their mutual benefit and protection they would not make much headway. The whole tendency seemed to be this: that the law did to day recognise the right of the Employer to impose rather drastic conditions of service upon each employ. It was remarkable how the employing class had come to realize that competition was not the unmixed blessing they would have the workers believe and so they combined to prevent competition. From their point of view competition was only good so long as it reduced the cost of labour. Irdividual liberty and freedom of contract should be as wide as possible, so long as they had a system of competition in commerce and industry like they had to-day. Personally, he did not believe in competition; he believed in co-opera- tion and combination, and if industry to-day was organized on a true, sound, and righteous basis there would be no need for agreements. So long as competi- tion was the law of modern industrial life, the fullest liberty should be given to the assistants. (Applause.) Mr. EDWARDSON moved "That this meeting is strongly of the opinion that the radius clause agree- ments imposed on shop managers and assistants are morally and economically unjust. We therefore call on all shop workers to join the National Union of Shop Assistants, and by organized effort secure the speedy Withdrawal of these restrictions." He contended that the agreements were due to mad competition and the greedy scramble for wealth, and it was an attempt to get the most out of men. (Applause). Under the radius agreements men had no right to obtain the benefit of their experience by taking up better posts under another firm and starting in business for themselves. Mr. E. PLINSTON, in seconding, trusted that the action of that meeting and of other similar meetings would so affect those firms who were forcing those agreements that the time would speedily come when they would be removed for ever, and give people freedom to get a living in a proper manner. (Applause). The Rev. J. F. MATTHEWS supported the resolution. He described the agreements as inhuman. Anything which bound the liberty of intellect, body and heart, whether in regard to black or white people, could not in its essential features be humanitarian. The trouble, he contended, lay in the selfishness of human nature. The agitation which the Shop Assistants' Union had been carrying on had not only been fraught with much success, but with much success ofa character that would highly commend itself to every thoughtful mind. Within the last few days one of the principal boot firms of this country, against which the agitation in the North and South had been directed, had withdrawn unconditionally their written agreements with their managers. He hoped the time was approaching when throughout the country the conditions for which shop assistants were labouring would be attained. (Applause.) Mr. H. L. BARRETT, shop assistants' organiser, delivered a vigorous address and referred with pleasure to the fact that 14 firma had withdrawn the radius Clause, freeing between 2,5000 and 3,000 shop managek from what they believed to be an unreasonable restric- tion upon their liberties. He wished to offer con- gratulations and give full credit to the Public Benefit Boot Company for having withdrawn their agreements. They had met with splendid success ; but they had still company shops in Warrington where radius agreements were in force. What right had any firm to exile a man, on the termination of his contract from a place where perhaps existed ties that bound him to that place? Seven thousand managers were still working under these agreements in valions parts of the country, so that there was still room for protest meetings. The public were not familiar with all the scandalous and unreason- able conditions that attached to shop life. The fight against these things had only just begun, and he urged shop assistants not to rely too much on legislation to put matters right. The system of living-in was bad and must be swept away. How many employers would Eve-in their assistants if they did not make a profit out of the board and lodgings of their assistants.? The atmosphere of such a house, he contended, was demoral- y3ing, and should be done away with as soon as possible. The system deprived the assistant of his liberty, made him a politically negligible, socially powerless, and industrially helpless quantity. Any system which r educed men and women to that automatism should be swept away. (Applause). The resolution was passed unanimously.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>EVERY HOME NEEDS ZAM-BUK."</head>
        <p>EVERY HOME NEEDS ZAM-BUK."</p>
        <p>Daily mishaps make Zam-Buk a daily need in every home; and those who haven't a box bandy would do well to consiier the vast number of everyday emergen- oies in which this unique healing halm saves time, pain, and money. For cuts, bruises, burns, scalds, and sprains, Zam-Buk is an excellent ",first-aid." It soothes smarting pain, counteracts poison and kills microbes, ensures heal- •ing by first intention," and grows new skin. Zam-Buk is specially useful for the common skin -troubles. Are the housewife's hands rough and chapped? —she makes them soft and smooth again with Zam-Buk. Are her daughter's cheeks and lips chafed by the cold March winds ?—Zacn-Buk again is the soothing and ,smoothing healer. The children's chilblains, cold-sores, .and chaps all yield to the same natural and never-failing Temedy, and Zam-Buk's wonderful purity makes it just the right thing for even baby's tormenting rashes and chafings. Rubbed well into back and chest, Zam-Buk is a pleasant remedy for cold, sore throat, or tight chest, and it drives out deep-seatod rheumatic pains in back or limbs. Spring rashes and pimples, boils, abscesses, and other seasonable eruptions also call for the application of Zam-Buk; and so do acute or chronic cases of eczema, psoriasis, scalp disease, pois,oned wounds, ulcers, bad legs and the like. _ _ _</p>
        <p>-Zam-link is the ideal skin-cure because it allays irra- tation and reduces inflammation, expels disease, tones up the tissues, and renews the skin in clearness and health.</p>
