      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>Warrington Man's Death.</head>
        <p>Warrington Man's Death.</p>
        <p>The body of Pat—rick Ward (50), a labourer, of 8, Philip-street, Warrington, who had been missing since November 24th, 1900, was recovered from the Randle Sluices of the canal at Norton on Sunday afternoon by Joseph Kirkham. At the inquest on Tuesday William Ward, 8, Phihip-street, Warrington, labourer at Wood's wire works, said he last saw his father alive in September on a Saturday evening. He had been missing since that evening. Joseph Kirkham, sluice-man at Randles Sluices, said he saw the body of the deceased lying on the Marshes on Sunday after- noon at four o'clock. The body was practically naked and much decomposed. It appeared to have been bro ught up by the tide that day. P.C. Hulse testified to having examined the body, which was badly decom- posed. The flesh was eaten from the skull, and from witness's opinion the body had been in the water for three or four months. The jury returned a verdict of 4' Found dead."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>WORKMEN'S COMPENSATION ACT.</head>
        <p>WORKMEN'S COMPENSATION ACT.</p>
        <p>The Finance Committee had requested the Law Clerk to the Committee to report upon the operation of the Workment's Compensation Act, 1906, so far as it affects those in the service of the committee.</p>
        <p>The minutes were approved without comment.</p>
        <p>GRATIFYING EXAMINATION RESULTS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the School Management Committee the result of part-1 of the preliminary examination for the certificate. 1907, was submitted, and it was noted that out of 22 pupil teachers presented for examination, 21 had been successful.</p>
        <p>Canon STEVENSON, in moving the adoption of the minutes, said Warrington had done about as well as any other place in the kingdom in the examination. (Hear, hear.) ACCOMMODATION AT THE SECONDARY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The School Management Committee considered and approved the report of a sub-committee appointed to consider the proposals made in regard to the suggested enlargement of the Secondary School and Technical Institute. The recommendations, of the sub-committee were t—(1) That the Gymnasium Committee be approached with a view to arrangements being made for the use of the Gymnasium as an assembly and examina- tion room for the purposes of the Seconday School, (2) that authority be given for a preliminary estimate to be obtained of the cost of building a woodwork room, a mechanical laboratory, and a drawing office; and (3) that the whole of the land belonging to the Education Committee bounded by Winmarleigh-street and Museum-street be enclosed.</p>
        <p>Canon STEVENSON expressed the opinion that the scheme as suggested ought to be economical and satis- factory. There was a want at the Secondary School of a large room in which the whole school could be assembled at once. At the present time there was no room in the building large enough to accommodate all the scholars. Another disadvantage was that examinations for admission to the school Bcc., could only be conducted by utilizing the class rooms, to the great inconvenience of the scholars. With regard to the 'rechnical Institute, the accommodation for certain classes was not by any means sufficient. He believed it was originally intended that the Gymnasium should be connected with the Technical School, and there would be no difficulty in connecting the two by a passage or corridor. If the Gymnasium Committee were willing to meet them in the matter, he did not see why some reasonable arrangement should not be made for the use of that room during the day school hours, leaving it quite free for gymnasium purposes at night. That would place at their disposal a large room capable of holding the whole of the scholars and one which would be very useful for the conduct of examinations. There was also a piece of land belonging to the Techni- cal Institute on whichcould be erected a good building of two storeys, providing four additional class rooms which were sorely needed. The improvements pro- posed would be a very great economy and would save a large building having to be put up, while at the same time he saw no maw% why the scheme should not work satisfastorily from the educational pnint of view. Councillor FULTON-SMITH. seconded the adoption of the minutes.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. T. FAIRCLOUGH moved that the report be not approved. He Itgreed as to the urgency of the need, but contended that the committee should have placed before them a complete scheme which would provide all the buildings required for the next twenty years. The ex- tension should be undertaken on the lines of the original plan Councillor EtTANp seeonded.</p>
        <p>Canon STEVENSON said that what was proposed would not in any way interfere with the original plan pro- posed some years ago for the extension of the Technical School. The scheme would, however, secure the accom- modation required for some years. The alternative would he to carry out the original plan who would be very much more expensive. Mr. H. ROBERTS thought the passing of the resolu- tion only committed the meeting to approval and adop- tion of the report. When the actual plans came up for consideration, they could deal with them as they thought fit. The CHAIRMAN: If there is any idea of going on with the larger scheme, now is the time to refer it back. Mr. ROBERTS said they were faced with the practical impossibility of expending any very considerable sum at the present time. What the committee wanted to do was to comply with the requirementa of the Education Department and the needs of the district at the least possible expenditure, in a practicable way. The scheme was a tentative one to carry them over a year or two till the re'quirements and the finances of the town were such that they could go In for a bolder and more definite scheme.</p>
        <p>Mr. 121esos said the question had been before the School Management Committee for two years. They had been continually pressed by the Board of Education to do something serious in the matter of enlargement, ever since the school was crowded at its opening. They met representatives of the Board a short time ago and those gentlemen came with proposals of a very extensive character. The present scheme seemed to be the irreducible minimum they would accept. They told the Committee that the Government support of the school was imperilled by their refusing to do something to provide the necessary accommodation. There was the ratepayer to be considered and it would be absurd to build additional buildings when they had in such close proximity to the school the Gymnasium and the School of Art.</p>
        <p>Councillor FULTON SMITH said the real effect of the adoption of the report would be that they would be able to find out the cost. When the question came before the committee they could decide whether the scheme was feasible or whether they should carry out the original plan. If it appeared to be a moderate schame as regarded expense and yet to supply the immediate need, it would be for the committee to decide finally that it should go on. Only the proposer and seconder voted for the amend- ment, and the report was approved. MUSIC HAUT CHARMS.</p>
        <p>The Rev. JAMES DAvrEs called attention to the resolution of the School Management Committee that the sum of 10s. per annum be paid to the Rev. B. Percival in respect of the use of the piano belonging to him at the St. Peter's school for public elementary school purposes; the committee to be responsible for the tuning of the instrument, and the payment to be made as from the Ist April, 1907. He did not think that the sum was sufficient, in view of the fact that the committee charged 2s. 6d. per occasion for the use of pianos supplied by. the committee for concerts and ss. for dances. 42 was being paid in respect of a harmonium at Silver-street. He moved that the minute be not approved. Mr. JOLLEY seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. ROBERTS thought the whole principle was at fault. The more dignified course would be for the committee to furnish any instrument required in the schools and not to allow these various charges to he obtained. What guided the committee in the decision to offer 10s. was that they thought the punishment would about fit the crime—(laughter), or that 10s. was about the commercial value, taking it at a high figure, of the 'use of the piano. He was told it was one of the vilest instruments, one of the most abominable crocks to be found in Warrington. (Laughter.) The owners might be forgiven for forming a somewhat liberal estimate of its value, but they said it was worth £5 and the Com- mittee thought 10s. was a pretty fair percentage. The Rev. J. DAVIES said the piano cost £l5 and was guaranteed for five years. The DIRECTOR said the piano was certified by three of the school managers to be worth ZS. Canon STEVENSON said the plan the committee adopted had been to pay so much per cent. on the value of the instrument as declared by the managers. Councillor LORD said the committee were given to understand that the instrument was in every way satis- factoty and they did not see why the committee should be put to the expense of buying a new piano. Councillor STEEL said the piano was not the crock described by Mr. Roberts, and Mr. Percival considered 10s. was an altogether inadequate payment. The return as to its value was made by the headmistress and signed by the managers in the absence of the Vicar. On a vote being taken the amendment Ives defeated by 11 votes to 9.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>LOCAL APPOINTMENT,</head>
        <p>A LOCAL APPOINTMENT,</p>
        <p>A letter baying been read from the Managers of the -'airfteld school, in reference to the proposed appoint- ment of a Mistress-in-Charge of the Upper Girls' department of that school, .it was resolved that a circular be sent to all the qualified mistresses (head and assistant) serving in the schools in the Borough calling attention to, and inviting applications for the vacancy, the salary to be offered to assistant teachers to beat the rate of £lOO per annum, and to bead teachers according to the scale, the appointment to terminate on or before the 31st December next ; it being understood that, in the event of it being ultimately decided not to appoint a head teacher for the departnlenf, tllB Corntuittee will</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>FOR SATURDAY MORNING.</head>
        <p>FOR SATURDAY MORNING.</p>
        <p>Many a merry school-girl spends Saturday morning helping mother to cook, and quite frequently the little woman of twelve is almost as good a hand as mother at pudding and cake-making. With cakes and puddings as well as with that most palatable and sustaining food—currant bread—the house- mother's rule should be exactly the same. Cakes, puddings and bread should contain a generous supply of currants—the food that is practically all nutriment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>TAXATION OF LAND VALUES. Public Meeting at Warrington.</head>
        <p>TAXATION OF LAND VALUES. Public Meeting at Warrington.</p>
        <p>and co-ordinated, and doors were open, now, for all children who were capable to pass right on into the Secondary School, the Grammar School, or the Tech- nical Institute. The Secondary School and the Technical Institute had shown such signs of progress that they were casting longing eyes on the Gymnasium, and had both become so popular and so successful that nothing save the imperative demand for economy pre- vented them from immediately embarking on a large scheme of extension. It would always be a source of pride to him that during his two yeaas of office, the long negotiations with the governors of the Grammar School had resulted in a complete agreement, and that, henceforth, the oldest local educational institution, enlarged, re-organized, and carefully adapted to present needs, would take its proper place in the educationat system of the town. He would not be satisfied until the school so long and honourably associated with the name of old Sir Thomas Boteler was supplemented by the provision of a high school for girls, nor must they rest until the educational ladder, reached right up to the universities, But in the meantime they had taken steps to provide for the increasing needs of Elementary Education and he was glad, indeed, to think that his last official act would be to take part in the formal opening of the Beamont School. Everything is now in readiness for the build- ing of Bolton School, and, although he personally dis- approved of some of the "economies" which had so long delayed it, and doubted if they were " economies " at all, in any true or real sense, he could, at any rate, congratulate their advocates upon having saved above .22,000. Other schools were cootemplated and one of them, indeed, to meet the requirements of Bank Quay, wad rapidly becoming urgent; but every district will no doubt be properly provided for in turn. What was the net effect of it all upon the children ? There had been a marked improvement everywhere in health, intelligence and general tone, and, although the Education Act had cost something like a shilling in the 2 they were getting value for our money. He was glad that increased attention had been paid to physical training; that with the assistance of the Gymnasium Committee, the teachers were now fully competent to give the necessary physical instruction ' • and that,thanks to the assistance of the Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee, many of their boys had learnt to swim. He would not be satisfied until at least equal facilities had been accorded to the girls; and although be was not able to induce the committee to provide a swimming bath at each of the two new schools, he trusted that the provision would be ultimately made. It had been a source of particular pleasure to have been able to assist, in specially directing the attention of our tiny future citizens to the ideals of local, national and Imperial duty, and to feel that Empire Day, which was celebrated last year for the first. time in our local schools, seemed likely to become a permanent institution, and would be this year cele-</p>
        <p>CURE A COLD IN ONE DAY</p>
        <p>Take Laxative Bromo-Quinine Tablets. All Chemists refund the money if it fails. E. W. Grove's name on each boz, is. lid</p>
        <p>A meeting under the —auspices of the Warrington Branch of the English League for the Taxation of Land Values was held in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street on Friday of last week. Mr. W. H. Sherburn presided over a small attendance.</p>
        <p>Mr. C. H. SMITHSON, Halifax,delivered an interesting address. There was, he said, a distinct connection between the taxation of land values and Liberalism, because Liberalism always stood for the liberty and freedom of the individual. We never could have the freedom of production which Cobden desired so long as the land monopoly was allowed to exist, and the taxa- tion of land values was, above all others, the measure which would break down the monoply. A small but important measure was foreshadowed in the King's speech—the valuation of land throughout the country. That was necessarily the first step towards the taxation of land values and it was quite probable that the House of Lords would attempt to mutilate the measure because they knew perfectly well that once it was passed the power</p>
        <p>of the Commons was absolutely supreme and taxation could he levied on the land without the Lords being able to interfere. (Hear, hear.) But, he ventured to say, if the House of Lords did mutilate that maasure it would be an opportunity of appealing to the country on the question of the right of the Lords to interfere with the legislation of the representatives of the people. Ethically they were perfectly justified in the taxation of land values as every child born into the world had an equal right to the use of the bountiful gifts of the Creator. Land monopoly infringed those rights. The brute creation had free access to the land and it was only in the case of man that someone could stand between him and the Creator's gifts. (Hear, hear.) Land was taxed in the old feudal days for the purpose of raising the imperial revenue but in 1600 a measure was passed for the substitution of excise duties for the taxation of land—a measure which was the greatest economic fraud in the history of England. Since 1660 the total income from this source had been £5,000,000,000. They heard a great deal about com- pensation, but if they were going to talk about com- pensation he could imagine some of them asking that some of that enormous sum. which should have been raised from a tax on land, ought to be paid back. If they were prepared to waive their claim to the return of that enormous sum he thought they had gone as far as justice demanded. Economically a tax on land values was absolutely sound. Land holders, generally speak- ing, put every obstacle in the way of the acquisition of small holdings by the agricultural labourer because they knew that if the labourer got a small holding they would lose their power over him. It was only the withholding of the land which made labour subservient throughout the whole industrial activities of the country. Recently an experiment was made by the provision of small holdings and some 1,100 applications were received for 35 holdings. These were allocated and a farm which formerly only supported three families now sup- ported thirty-five. That of course meant that the immigration of labourers horn the land to the cities was reduced to that extent. It was this immigration which produced the unemployment to the cities, and it, it was not difficult to realize that the universal applica- tion of the principle of small holdings could quickly absorb the percentage of unemployment which existed. The result of this would be that instead of labour wanting work, work could be waiting for labour and, subsequently labour would be able to demand a higher wage, (Hear, hear.) The power of the capitalist was entirely due to the presence of unemployed. ( Applause.) A few questions were asked, and votes of thanks to the speaker and chairman concluded the meeting.</p>
        <p>ing wide to Jenkins the latter handed on to Isherwood who ran in at the corner,no goal resulting. Half-time: ‘Varrington, 13 points ; Batley, 2 points. On resuming the home men went in for running and passing almost exclusively and prevented Batley having much advantage from the wind. The visitors had not time to get going before brilliant passing ended in Isherwood making an opening for Fish who ran round the posts. After this there was only one team in in it, the locals quite outclassing their old foes. Batley rallied slightly, anda good run by will Davies ended in Wattle Davies dropping a goal. Isherwood and Hocken- hall added tries, and Thomas plAced a goal from the last. Batley then got Dangerous, and a long pass from Edwards gave Will Davies a chance to score, Wattle Davies adding the goal as the result of clever play by Jenkins. In the last few minutes Hockenhall scored twice. Thomas on each occasion adding goals. Result: —Warrington 5 goals and 8 tries (34 points), Batley 3 goals and 1 try (9 points).</p>
      </div>
