      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>OLDHAM v. RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>OLDHAM v. RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The wet and stormy weather had a prejudicial effect on the attendance at Watersheddings, but there were some 8,000 spectators present. There was a strong wind blowing, of which Oldham had the advantage in the first half. The visitors soon gained a footing in the Oldham half, and after neat passing Deakin was grassed by Cash close to the home line. Ferguson and Avery led their team to Runcorn's quarter, but W. Dixon passed for- ward. The home attack was met with a grand defence, but Ferguson at length made a mark at mid-field, tiknd from here kicked a splendid goal. On resuming, Oldham pressed the visitors hard, but they kept their line intact until half-time came with the score 2 points in Oldham's favour.</p>
        <p>In the second half the home team played with more success, and Runcorn fell away somewhat. Their play was mainly of th,) kick and rush order. On the defence the visitors were excellent, and at three-quarter time the issue of the gome was still in doubt. At last the Oldham attack was rewarded, and five minutes before time Tyson scored. Ferguson failed at goal. Runcorn could not retrieve the game, and the final score was— Oldham 1 goal and 1 try (5 points), Runcorn nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>SWINTON v. ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>SWINTON v. ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Played at Swinton, before 3,000 spectators. Swinton pressed at the outset, but the " Saints " defence pre- vailed for a long period. until Valentine managed to work his way over the line, and Wallwork kicked a goal. The last named player added a try to Swinton's score before the change of ends. Half-time score :—Swinton 8 points, St. Helens nil, _ _</p>
        <p>The visitors attacked after the interval, and Hillen scored two tries, while J. Creevey also crossed the line successfully. C. Creevey's attempts at goal all failed. Swinton added to their score a try by G. H. Jones, and a penalty goal, and the result was :—Swinton 2 goals and 4 tries (16 points), St. Helens 3 tries (9 points).</p>
        <p>R Fe-PLAYED TIES. Dewsbury 2 416 Birrow . 0 1 3 Wakefield 2 4 16 Workington 1 1 5</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, March 23, 1907</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 23, 1907</p>
        <p>ECZEMA &amp; POISONED BLOOD Treated in Three Hospitals. Cured at last by VENO'S SEAWEED TONIC.</p>
        <p>The following sworn declaration as to the wonderful efficacy of Veno's Seaweed Tonic in a severe case of eczema and poisoned blood is interesting : "I, George Albert Gough, of the Prince of Wales Inn, Middle Row, Wolverhampton, make oath and say that for many years I suffered intense agony from eczema and poisoned blood. I went over to America, and whilst there suffered so badly that my uncle had to strap me down in bed. I was like a madman, and used to scratch pieces out of my flesh. At last I got a little better and returned to my home at Wolverhampton, but I had not been back two weeks when it came on worse than ever.</p>
        <p>"I have been treated since then in three different hospitals, and in each case turned out uncured. Doctor could do me no good, and my hands and face were a mass of sores and blotches.</p>
        <p>"About four years ago I got a sample of Veno's Sea- weed Tonic, and found that the first two or three doses relieved me a little. I began then to take it regularly, and soon the sores and blotches on my hands, face, and body disappeared entirely, and since I stopped taking it (four years ago) I have had no return of the eczema.</p>
        <p>"G. A. GOUGH." Sworn at Wolverhampton, this 14 1/06, before me, H. WOOD ROFFE HILL.</p>
        <p>(Cominissioner for Oaths).</p>
        <p>For all skin and blood diseases,Veno's Seaweed Tonic is safest and most powerful remedy known. It cures the worst cases of indigestion, torpid liver, flatulence, pains in the back, headache, kidney trouble, had blood, and female weakness, and is especially good for chronic constipation. Price is. 13d., and 2s. 9d. of all chemists. For all-round purity and potency there is not its equal any where.</p>
      </div>
