      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Confirmation Service.</p>
        <p>On Saturday afternoon the Bishop of Liverpool (Dr. Chavasse) conducted a confirmation service at the Parish Church. One hundred and twenty-seven candi- dates were presented and of these 109 were connected with the Parish Church, 17 were from Padgate, and one from Haigh. The clergy present, in addition to the Bishop, were the Rev. Canon Willis; the Rev. W. Pollock-Hill, the Rev. T. A. J. Ridpath, and the Rev. T. W. Barber. A fairly large congregation witnessed the ceremony, and listened with interest to an address by the Bishop.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>Wycliffe P.S.A.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A.</p>
        <p>There was a good attendance at Sunday's meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. The Hon. Secretary pre- sided, and referred to the visit of Canon Stott Holland to the Parish Church and his address on "I can and I can't," and said personally he should have been pleased if as a body they could have gone to hear him, and shown the Catholic sympathy and undenominational character of their Brotherhood. What a grand thing it would be if all Churches could unite all their force in fighting evil. Mr. Dance, of Manchester, and Mr. Evans took part in the meeting, Mr. Wm. Rutter gave a cornet solo Dream of Paradise," and received much applause. Mr. Payton presided at the organ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>Canon Scott Holland's Visit. Socialistic Visions.</head>
        <p>Canon Scott Holland's Visit. Socialistic Visions.</p>
        <p>Canon Scott Holland is one of the leading figures in the religious and social life of to-day. He can sway great multitudes by his eloquence and he never hesitates to declare his convictions on questions affecting the advancement of the community, be they palatable or otherwise. It was only to be expected that great interest should centre in the famous preacher's visit to Warrington on Sunday, but it was indeed a remarkable and impressive scene which the Parish Church pre- sented, filled, as it was, in every part with men. The service was held under the auspices of the Union of Church of England Bible Classes and members of those organiz itions were present in full force. But repre- sentatives of other religious communions helped to swell the congregation. In a front pew was to be seen a well-known Baptist pastor,while a cursory survey of the assembly revealed the presence of many other Noncon- formists. The hymn " Onward Christian Soldiers " was sung right lustily to Sullivan's stirring tune, Mr. F. AT. Darby being at the organ. A brief form of ser- vice preceded the sermon, the Rector intoning the prayers and Canon Morley Stevenson reading the lesson. Canon Scott Holland immediately grasped the attention of his hearers,a.nd held it throughout a discourse lasting three-quarters of an hour. He is an unusually rapid but very distinct speaker, and is gifted with a wonder- ful command of words. His subject was the simple one : " I can and 1 cant," and he took for his text the words: "I know both how to be abased and I know how to abound: everywhere and in all things I am instructed both to be full and to be hungry, both to abound and to suffer need. I can do all things through Christ which strengtheneth me." He pointed out that "I can " was the verdict which St. Paul, as an old man, was able to pronounce upon his life—and what a life it had been! The preacher in a series of beautiful word pictures brought vividly before his hearers some of the most momentous scenes in the life of the Apostle, showing how he became master of every situation. He then passed to the other extreme—the despairing cry which came from many a human heart " I can't "—and sketched the posi- tion of the man who determines to combat evil and live a pure and upright life but is driven to the conclusion that he cannot. He showed, however, that this record of failure was actually the previous experience of St. Paul who sank into the deepest abyss of man's con- fession "I cant." These two 'cries were not contra- dictions. They were the same thing " I can do all things through Christ." The power of "I can "entered into life through the very door opened by" I can't." "Because you can't" said the preacher "God can. He cannot enter into your soul until you have said 'I can't.' 'I can because I can't.' That is the paradox of life." There were so many men in England who were still saying" I can," who could not utter this great confession. It was the English nation, the Saxon in us, he supposed, that was always saying "Oh, but I can." This idea, asserted the Canon, was 2,000 years behind the times and he showed how the Jewish nation, a race very strong in the moral conscience and powerful in religion, failed utterly in the endeavour to work out the life of righteousness themselves and came to the conclusion" I can't." Christ came to answer that con- clusion. He stood on earth claiming that the verdict must be given that man could not do these things in his own strength. The Incarnation rested on that verdict. There were to-day great industrial movements in the world, full of hope and promise—movements of those who had seen the true vision, great socialistic dreams of brotherhood and of the city that could be built here on earth. Full of hope, promise and strength, it was the vision towards which we should all be moving. They had seen it, but it was not enough to see the vision. On the contrary to see was to be smitten to the dust: they had never seen the vifion till they had been put to shame by it. The higher it rose the harder the task. How could they arrive at the brotherhood that was to be so sweet and precious and kill all the egotism and selfishness in their souls, the competitive selfishness that still ruined and tyrannized over the world? How</p>
        <p>were they to become unselfish, siintiv and holy an love their brethren? There was the vision. Look at it, dream of it, work for it, and as they saw it let them fall down in the dust before the light that was shining and say "Woe is me, for I am a man of unclean lips."' Those who saw the true prophetic s &gt;cial vision had to learn the lesson of Isaah. The vision would come and they would build the new city, but it would not be built until they hact been purged of their iniquity and until they said 'Here am I, send me." They would do all things but not by their own resolution or power, or imaginings but by Jesus Christ their Lord. That did not mean that Christianity only appealed to the broken fallen prodigals. They too often heard that Christianity had nothing to do with the strong men fighting in the van for humanity, that it had become a sort of ambul- ance taking away the wounded in the rear. The servants of Jesus Christ were the strongest and best men in the world, but they nevertheless had to say "For all my goodness, for all my soundness and glorious gifts I cannot do it. I have to go down in the dust with any sinner, any prodigal or harlot and say 'Depart from me, for lam sinful."' Christ came to save not only the sinners in their weakness, but the strong men from themselves. Their own strength beguiled them, their own goodness enclosed them in the prison. Therefore it was. to them in their strength that the Gospel was sent. There was a self enthroned in every one of them with which they were pleased but which must be expelled, and Christ was here on earth to break, crush and crucify it. _ _ _</p>
        <p>A collection was taken in aid of the Infirmary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>WHAT IS SIN?</head>
        <p>WHAT IS SIN?</p>
        <p>Rev. John Yonge and the New Theology.</p>
        <p>Definition.</p>
        <p>The Congo Horrors</p>
        <p>"What is sin ?" was the subject of the Rev.. John Yonge's sermon at Wycliffe Church on Sunday evening. He said this was one of the most momentous questions, and it was impossible to over-estimate the necessity of its accurate and adequate answer. It was being asked afresh just now and it was being aswered with consider- able eloquence and confidence by the teachers of the New Theology. He had already endeavoured to show ' that the New Theology presented in the doctrine of the immanence of God in man only a partial and inadequate idea which needed to be carefully supplemented and counter-balanced by another idea of that relation—that of God's transcendence over man without which even what was true about the immanence might act upon the mind as prejudically as though it were not true. He wanted that evening to show that, as it seemed at least to his judgment, the New Theology was deficient in a very similar fashion in its teaching concerning sin. The Bible made sin appear, before the thoughtful mind, as unspeakably dreadful, disastrous, and destructive. It employed every conceivable image to set forth its evil nature, its evil influence, and its evil effects and consequences. It showed how sin darkened the mind, polluted the heart, seared the conscience, and destroyed the soul. in created universal mischief and ruin amongst mankind— misery, suffering, cruelty, tyranny, injustice, oppression, degradation, death. It transformed human nature into a nature akin to the devilish. It was the prolific parent of all the woes of hsmanity through all the ages. They must be careful to remember that always in Scripture sin was spoken of, forbidden and denounced as an offence against God. He would dwell the more upon that, because, as it seemed to him, the ' New Theology obscured that teaching, and in so far as it did that, the New 'Theology was unscriptural. A sinner in the sight of heaven was a lawless person : a person who was a breaker and violater of the laws of I truth and and righteousness, a person who ignored and disobeyed the Word and Will of God. Such a life was I labelled by the Bible as an ungodly and evil life, and lit was expressly and repeatedly declared that God would bring it into judgment. It was to be feared that ! the tendency of some teaching, at any rate, which claimed to be the New Theology was to minimise the evit and the guilt of sin and also to lessen the import- ance and force of Scripture doctrine about human responsibility for sin. Any teaching and any teacher who would do that was to be regarded with distrust. When a New Theologian told them that " evil is negative, not positive ; it is the , shadow where the light ought to be" and when he boldly declared that sin is a blundering quest for God,' they felt justified in replying that they found no warrant for any such idea ire all Scripture. It might be, indeed, and no doubt it was true, that the sinner who lived in sin was a man who was "missing the mark" of his creation, but he was not doing it blindly. He waS doing it against the witnsss of conscience and in mans cases—in all who possessed a knowledge of Divine Revelation—he was doing it against his better know- ledge. A sinner who went in for a sinful life took no heed of God. He ignored God; turned a deaf ear to Him ; would not so much as look at Him. He was o transgressor, a mutineer, against the Eternal King, rebel ! Human sin was the most awful and terrible and disastrous fact in human nature,and any doctrine which tended to lessea its awfulness added a new peril to life. That was what, as far as he understood it. the New Theology did—it extenuated sin, it minimized it. And perhaps in none of its suggestions did it do Ws' more completely than when it defined sin as selfishneSS; Having referred at some length to the Rev. R. J• Campbell's sermon to which he listened the previouo Tuesday, Mr. Yonge said that to say that "sin is selfish- ness" might be philosophically accurate, but it wat not "a language that is understanded of the people.' It would be absolutely true to turn that sentence round and say that " selfishness is sin," and it was the prolifio parent of all kinds of sin. Might he suggest that there was in this very day, among otherS which could be named, one most glaring public instance of sin. This nation was gradually awakening to the monstrosity of evil which was being I inflicted upon the natives of the Congo province iss • Africa by the atrocious villainies which were now being perpetrated there by the agents of the King of L the Belgians. Many of his hearers would be by thiS • time aware of the cruelties and infamies which had been going on. It had all been done to force labour out of ignorant and helpless natives in order to create wealth ?or that utterly heartless monarch. As one read the evidence which was coming to light of the tortures, and maimings, and deaths that had been suffered among these people in order to furnish rubber for that con- scienceless man to sell to all the world at an enormous L profit, from which he was said to have become the wealthiest monarch in the world, one wondered at the l self-repression of Heaven ! Would that gigantic sin be ' covered and explained by the term " Selfishness " ? Would any of the desds of lust and hate and cruelty find adequate covering under that phrase? Did not the definition break down as unequal to the facts of sin ? " Sin is lawlessness," said St. ' John. It was rebellion against God ; it was offence against heaven, and could only merit the dread con- demnation and penalty of guilt. Sin was a life which was offensive to the holy Majesty of God, which was a rebellion against His Nature and His Will, which created a separation from God and goodness that must be eternal and ruinous unless it be brought, now and here, to repentant end. The evil and the guilt of sin found its Divinest expression and measure in the Cross of our Lord Jesus Christ. As that Crucifixion was the most incalculable crime against both man and God, so did it proclaim the awfulness of the guilt of human sin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>REDUCED TO SKIN AND BONE.</head>
