      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</head>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>SPRING NOVELTIES.</p>
        <p>Copper and bronze coloured shoes are to en- tirely replace tan this year, and must, of course, have stockings of the same colour, says the They are smartest with dead-gold buckles or buckles covered with kid to match embroidered in soft coloured silks. Earrings are growing longer and longer, and the old-fash- ioned cameo or jade pendants are being eagerly sought after to be worn with the curious neck- lets of cameos or little chains that were worn by our great-grandmothers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>AN AFTERNOON GOWN.</head>
        <p>AN AFTERNOON GOWN.</p>
        <p>The subject of our sketch is a modish and be- ooming afternoon gown of soft face cloth. The original, which was a delightfully, smart and pretty affair, was carried out in a delicate and lovely shade of grey, quite dainty enough to make the gown wearable throughout the sum- mer. Very simple in shape, the bodice was yet elegant in line and invested with a chic all its own. A wide band of imitation Venetian point, 'lx a very heavy make and soft ivory tone, out- I Jined the V-shaped opening of the pinafore," and was continued down the front to the waist- I band Similar lace edged the Ehort kimono</p>
        <p>FIRST AN/iMIA- THEN INDIGESTION-' THEN RHEUMATISM. What a Hull Lady Suffered. Bad Blood the Single Cause.</p>
        <p>eleeves, from beneath which came soft inner sleeves of embroidered ivory net finished with wee bands of the Venetian point. Of net, also, were the dainty wee gauged chemisette and neckband. The skirt, which was cut very long all round, was perfectly plain, and fitted closely over the hips.</p>
        <p>As a general thing, when men undertake the occupations usually pursued by women, they excel in them. The best cooks, the best cleaners, house servants, washers and ironers, the best nurses, are, declares a San Francisco newspaper, of the stronger sex. The House of Commons has shewn a keen if somewhat acidulated sense of humour, remarks 4, ladies' paper. The Women's Suffrage Bill was talked out! The Canterbury College governors have de- cided to 'accept Mr. John Studholme's offer to "What I endured year after year would have wrecked contribute £2OO a-year for three years towards the strongest constitution." The words were uttered the establishment of a Chair of Domestic Eco- by Mrs. Hatch, of 8, Victoria-avenue, Hull, during her I nomy. The appointment will be offered to recital of a long record of agony resulting from weak, Miss Gilchrist, of the Tennessee University. impure blood. Yet after these many years of misery The Queen of Spain has a passion for open Mrs. Hatch recovered sound health, because in Dr. windows. Upon entering the Palace at Madrid Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People she found a remedy the other day she ordered all the windows to be that actually made Rich Red Blood and drove out of apened at once, and, in consequence, _sixteen her system all the disorders arising from bad blood. ladies became incapable of attending upon her, "in the first place said Mrs. Hatch, "I was sickly, is they all caught chills. pale and ana3mic. Th'is was when I was approaching the The ingenuity of the modern woman has dis- age of twenty, and as time went on 'I grew so feeble covered a new method of earning a competence, that often I could hardly keep on I says' " Marmaduke " in Truth. There are THESE WERE my feet. When I rose in the morn- horse-breakers; it has occurred to an ingenious HER SYMPTOMS. ing fits of dizziness made me stagger i woman that there should be husband-breakers. helplessly; I could not touch any For a moderate fee she is porepared to mak