        <p>Gossip.</p>
        <p>Not an Ache or Pain To-day. Made Well by</p>
        <p>ing illustrated pamphlet describing many cures will be ent post free on application.</p>
        <p>THE CASTLE OF THE KING.</p>
        <p>We have received a copy of an interesting and fully illustrated book on Windsor Castle and the famous College at Eton ; the articles are written by, Sir Richard Holmes' the ex-Librarian to his Majesty at Windsor Castle, and by the well-known author, Mr. A.C. Benson, whose intimate association with their respective sub- jects, fit them so ably to deal with the history of these two national buildings. The legends and story of the great park surrounding the royal demesne are told by Mr. Menzies. The book is issued by the Windsor and Eton Chamber of Commerce and will be forwarded to intending visitors by the Hon. Secretary on request. Messrs. Watts and Co. are issuing a second and revised edition of "The Churches and Modern Thought." This work is attracting considerable attention. The first edition, published last year, consisted of one thousand copies—the present edition comprises five thousand copies ; but the publishers confidently expect that another and still larger edition will be required at no distant date. The book, both in its style and in its subject-matter, appeals to the widest possible range of readers, ant to render its purchase within the means of a large circle, it has been arranged to issue a popular edition concurrently with the new library edition. The publishers believe that a work dealing exhaustively, temperately, and lucidly with the arguments for and ' against belief, and also stimulating to thought in vital and healthy ways, is a want much felt at this present jtime of religious unrest, and that the appearance of such ! a book at the psychical moment could hardly have been Lmore opportune.</p>
        <p>High-crowned, and with a wide brim sharply bent down to one side, the hat is made of Tegal straw, and is very becoming in shape. Round the crown is swathed a wide, very soft ribbon of a deep bronze-green shade" the ends of the rib- bon being tied in a smart bow at the back. On either side of the crown is arranged a huge clus- ter of lovely tea roses, shading from the palest soft yellow to an almost orange shade. The hat is completed by a cache-Kigne of roses and bronze-green tulle.</p>
