      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>GIANT HAT-PIN,</head>
        <p>THE GIANT HAT-PIN,</p>
        <p>The hat-pins of the spring season are growing in size and ugliness. I saw one last week, writes "Lorna" in the British Weekly, which was very Like an egg-cup. It was made of Dresden china. Another familiar style is the over-blown pearl, about the size of' a tangerine orange. Two of these, well fixed in on each side, may be trusted to spoil the appearance of the most modish hat ever contrived by woman's genius.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>TO-DAY'S RECIPE</head>
        <p>TO-DAY'S RECIPE</p>
        <p>OYSTERS IN SCALLOP SHELLS. - Allow half-a- knen oysters to each shell. Let them remain in</p>
        <p>ir own liquor. Put into a saucepan loz. of butter, three skinned mushrooms, a bunch of parsley, and three skinned shallots, finely minced, and a dash of nutmeg. Dust in a little flour. • Stir this over the fire until it is brown. then add the liquor from the oysters. When it is the consistency of thick sauce remove it from the fire and put in the oysters, and give it a toss or two, adding the juice of a lemon and a dash of cayenne. Put the mixture into the shells, which have been well buttered. Screen them with nne bread crumbs, place small pieces of butter here and there. Now put the shells upon a tin and set it in a good oven. When the scallops are of a fine colour send them to table in the shells. Arrange them on a dish upon g croyley... Gan* them with partilex,</p>
      </div>
