      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>UNITED ALKALI COMPANY, LIMITED.</head>
        <p>UNITED ALKALI COMPANY, LIMITED.</p>
        <p>DIRECTORS' REPORT. The directors in their report on the company's opera- tions for the year ending 3 lst December last, state that the certified balance-sheet and profit and loss account show a net profit of £393,293, and amount brought for- ward from 1905 £41,706, making £434,999, less transfer to debenture redemption fund account, making a total of £75,000, £15,000—£419,999. After transferring the following amounts :—To reserve fund ,making a total of £175,000) £75,000, to reserve fund (depreciation por- tion making a total of £850,004), .£50,000 the directors propose to apportion the balance as follows :—lnterim dividend on preference shares at 7s. per share, for six months to 30th June last, paid 31st August, 1906, less income tax, £93,939; final dividend on preference shares at 7s. per share, for six months to 31st December, 1906, payable 26th inst., less income tax, .293,939 ; divided on ' ordinary shares at 4s. per share for twelve months, pay- , able 26th inst., less income-tax,256,875 ; amount carried forward, £50,246 4s. Bd. The buildings, plant, and machinery have been well maintained, ' and all necessary repairs have been effected. In common with most industries, the company hal; participated in the general improvement in the trade of the country. During the year a con- siderable quantity of pyrites from the company's Spanish mines was consumed in the works, and inreased quantities will be consumed in 1907. A further pur- chase of mining properties in Spain, including a 3fl. 61n. gauge railway, 43 miles in length for goods and passengers, had been effected during 1906 on advantag- eous terms. Development in several directions will somewhat delay the moment when full advantage can be taken of these purchases. The Alkali and Copper Works of Messrs. William Henderson and Co., Ltd., situated at Irvine (Ayrshire), were offered for sale by Im auction in April last, and re acquired by the com- pany on favourable terms. The directors much regret to announce the death of Sir Sir Charles Tennant, Bart., president, Sir David Gamble, Bart., K.C.8., and Mr. J. K. Huntley, vice-presidents; and Mr. William Gamble, director. Mr. Holbrook Gaskell has accepted the office of president, and the directors have appointed Mr. G. Christopher Clayton, of Rtonfield, Woolton-road, Liver- pool, a director in the place of William Gamble. The directors who retire by rotation are Messrs. Holbrook Gaskell, jun., Duncan M'Kechnie, Max 'Muspratt, and , James Tennant, all of whom are eligible and offer them- elves for re-election.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>RETIREMENT OF MR. J. C. PARR.</head>
        <p>RETIREMENT OF MR. J. C. PARR.</p>
        <p>Twenty Years' Growth of the Institution.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Governors of the Warring- ton Infirmary was held at the Town Hall on Wednesday, Mr. J. C. Parr presided and those present included: The Revs. Father ,Chittle, E. C. E. Carleton, H. V. Pigot, W. Bracecamp, B. Percival, E. Peters, and J. F. Matthews, the Mayor, Colonel Buckton, Captain Withington, Messrs. 0. Dakin, F. Monks, John White, W. Long, W. P. Rylands, R. C. Parr, C. White, G. Thompson, J. Hesketh, L. W. Reynolds, F. Pendlebury, J. Rymer Young, J. Pairclough, C. Jackson Holmes, J. Broadhurst, W. Bailey, L. Weir, Dr. Hodgkinson, Dr. Bowden and Mr. J. H. J. Hampson (secretary). Letters of apslogy for non-attendance were read from Rev. John Yonge and Miss Massey.</p>
        <p>ANNUAL REPORTS.</p>
        <p>In their annual report the Board of Management pointed out that the total number of patients treated at the institution was 9,469, as compared with 8,993 in 1905. Financially the income had increased from £3,134 to £3,241; and the expenditures from £3,188 to £3,251. , Referring to the extension scheme the Board earnestly I appeal for further snbscriptions in order that the new premises might be free from debt when opened. The Board also place on record their rearet at the death of Mr. Robert Dobson, a former trustee and of Alderman John Roberts, Life Governor and Chairman for 26 years of the Workingmen's Committee. The Medical Board had to report that the work of the Infirmary is still increasing, this being especi- ally noticeable in the accidents and emergencies, which in 1905 numbered 3,101 and 3,769 in 1906; The Medical Board again call attention to the abuse of the Institution which undoubtedly exists, and suggest that a thorough review of the system of recommendation is necessary. The completion of the extension will convert the present hospital into one of considerable size, requiring a larger staff and many alterations in details of manage- ment which will need the careful consideration of the Board of Management. The medical staff look forward with satisfaction to the fact that, on the completion of the extension, they will have at their disposal more beds for the treatmett of medical cases, which hitherto have had to be tteated in their own homes.</p>
        <p>Mr. PARR said he was reminded that it was just 20 years since he was first chosen for the presidential chair and it might be of interest if he compared the position in 1887 with that of to-day. In 1887,25 operations were performed whereas in 1906 the number had increased to 307. The in-patients numbered 162, as against 607, accidents and emergencies 977, as against 3,669, income £1,629, as against £3,241, and expenses £1,509, as against £3,251. Having referred to the extension scheme, Mr. Parr said it was hoped to have the building completed this year. He wished he could say that their responsi- bility ended there, but it was only right to remember that with increased accommodation additional funds would be required, as a larger hospital would entail a larger staff, and the admission of more patients would involve a larger outlay, and he wished to stimulate them to increased exertion. (Hear, hear.) Hitherto the hospital had been well supported and unlike many institutions of a similar character, kept practically free from debt, and it %odd be little short of a misfortune if the increased expenditure was not met by a corresponding inerease in income. He hoped that the extension would lead to a larger and more representative subscription list. (Applause). Mr. W. P. RYLANDS seconded. It was a curious fact that their income almost balanced their expeniture.That was not to be accounted for by coincidence and he ventured to suggest that the explanation was that when money was required it was forthcoming and that encour- aged him to believe that when the extention was open and the needs of the hospital were much greater the increased subscrsption would be forthcoming. He thought that institutions which were slightly in debt, prospered as to be a little behind was a wonderful stimulous. (Hear, hear.) Hospital accommodation was a form of charity which should have first claim upon the generosity of everyone in a district and if the people of Warrington took a proper view of their responsibilities in the matter the subscription list ought without the slightest difEculty to be doubled. (Hear. hear.) In conclusion Mr. Rylands appealed for increased subsoriptions on behalf of the extension fund in order that the building when opened might be free from debt. (Applause.) Captain WITHLNGTON supported the motion which was approved. ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>MR. PARR RESIGNS THE PRESIDENCY.</head>
        <p>MR. PARR RESIGNS THE PRESIDENCY.</p>
        <p>Mr.JoHN WHITE moved the re-election of Mr. George Dakin as hon. treasurer, including in the motion a vote of thanks for past services. They must bear in mind that in the erection of the new wing to the Infirmary they had had to destroy .property which was bringing in a certain amount of Income and he thought they should endeavour to raise sufficient money to cover that less. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Mr. GEoriaz THOMPSON seconded the motion, which was heartily carried. On the motion of the Rev. H. V. PIGOT, seconded by Mr. WILLIAM LONG, Mr. C. Jackson Holmes was re-elected hon. auditor, and thanked for his past ser- vices.</p>
        <p>Rev. Father WHITTLE moved the re-election of the hon. medical officers—Drs. E. A. Fox, G. W. Joseph, C. E. Richmond, and E. Bowden—together with thanks for their past services. There were none who did so much for the Infirmary as the hon. medical officers, and without them he failed to see how they could raise the £l,OOO which the services of those gentlemen represented. (Hear, hear.) Mr. L. WEnt seconded, and the resolution was heartily approved. Dr. BOWDRN briefly returned thanks. The Board of Management : The President and Vice- Presidents, Rev. Harry Vernon Pigot, M.A., Mr. Henry Thornton, Lieutenant-Colonel J. Douglas Buckton,Rev. Father J. P. Whittle, Rey. E. C. F. Carleton, M.A., Mr. W. J. Forshaw, Roy. William Bracecamp, Mr. C.Jackson Holmes, Captain George R. Crosfield, Rev. C. Harvey Cook, Mr. Thomas Burton, Mr. C. M. Watkins, Mr. T. A. Wilkinson, Mr. F. A. Frost, Mr. J. H. Smethurst, and Mr. George Walker, the House Visitors' and *trinance Committee: Messrs. George Dakin, John Fairclough, Charleu White, and rhomns Burton ; and the Ladies' Committee :—Mesdames Ziba Armitage, John Richard Pickmere, Joseph Charlton Pars, John White, Frederic William Willis, Harry Vernon Pigot, Frederick William Monks (hon. secretary), Charles Broadbent, George Dakin and Lady Greenall were unanimously re-elected. Replying to the vote of thanks, the Rev. H. V. PIGOT referred to the loss which the Board of Management had sostained by the death of Alderman Roberts, and the retirement of Mr. George Thompson. Mr. J. C. PARR moved a special vote of thanks to the Workingmen's Committee. He pointed out. that in 20 years the annual subscription from that Committee had risen from £368 to £945, and he believed than it was an open secret that last year the Committee had raised over £l,OOO. (Applause.) He hoped that success would stimulate the Committee to further endeavour and that with the increasing needs of the Infirmary they would pa their shoulders to the wheel and find ever a larger sum. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>Mr. j'OTYN FAIRCLOLIGH seconded and the resolution was carried amidst applause.</p>
        <p>RETIREMENT OF MR. PARR.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. RYMER YOUNG moved the re-electiod of Mr. J. C. Parr as president and the re-election of the vice- presidents, with the addition of the names of Mr. Williara Long and the Rev. H. V. Pigot.</p>
        <p>The MAvOit seconded, Mr. PARR asked to be allowed to withdraw his name. Having held the position for 20 years he felt that it was time he handed over the responsibilities to a younger man.</p>
        <p>Mr. P. Moncs said that Mr. Parr had previously intimated his intention of resigning and under the circumstances he submitted the mrn- of Sir Gilbert Greenall. Sir Gilbert had been approached and was most willing to take over the duties of the office. Mr. CHARLES WHITE seconded and the motion was approved, Mr. J. C. Parr's name being placed on the list of vice presidents. Mr. RYLANDS said they ought not to allow the occa- sion to pass without registering a very hearty vote of thanks to Mr. Parr for his valuable services during the past 20 years. The motion was seconded and carried with acclama- tion.</p>
        <p>Replying to a vote of thanks to the Board of Manage- ment. Mr. Thompson said that his leaving Warrington involved no severance which he felt more deeply 'than the retirement from the Board. Votes of thanks to the Mayor for the use of the Council Chamber and to Mr. Parr for Presiding, con- cluded the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>Diastroas Fire at Moore.</head>
        <p>Diastroas Fire at Moore.</p>
        <p>A. disastrous fire occurred on Monday at Moore, a row of four thatched cottages being totally destroyed. The cottages were situated on the banks of the Bridge- water Canal, and it is supposed that a spark from a passing tug set one of the roofs on fire. Fanned by the gale the flames spread rapidly until all the build- ings were a burning mass. The cottagers, assisted by many villagers, removed their furniture into the roadway. A young man named Maddock was in one of the rooms when the roof crashed in. He was pinned down and burned about the face, but was soon rescued.</p>
        <p>LUCKY STOWAWAYS.</p>
        <p>There were two stowaways on the wrecked Suevic, who were to have been prosecuted when the vessel reached her destination, but since landing on the Cornich coast they have disappeared.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>VOICE OF LABOUR.</head>
        <p>THE VOICE OF LABOUR.</p>
        <p>Edited by John Workman. Readers of this column are invited to assist in</p>
        <p>making it interesting and helpful to social workers. 1 by contributing themselves. Correspondents d —itr</p>
        <p>dponaen . must write on one side of the paper only, and endeavour to make their letters and notes short and to the point. A nom-de-plume may be used, but the real name of the writer should be forwarded in confidence to the Editor of the column. M.S.S. must be addressed to John Workman, " Examiner " Office, Warrington and should be sent in not later than Wednesday in each week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>NEW THEOLOGY.</head>
        <p>THE NEW THEOLOGY.</p>
        <p>I We were told the other evening that if John Wesley i had been li ring in the 20th Century he would have I been a Socialist ; that unemployment was caused to a I large extent by the unnecessary luxury of the rich; I that a lord is but a sinner and he ought to go to hell; I that many gain riches but lose their own souls. All I those remarks were pqssed in a Wesleyan Chapel in the healthiest surburb of Warrington. I am concerned to know how many follov(ers of John Wesley there are in Warrington; and how many more would there be if the fear of social ostracism,— and the stoppage of the food supply--were not in the hearts of the folk.—A WESLEYAN. SHOP ASSISTANTS AND RADIUS AGREEMENTS. With regard to the vexed question of radius agreements, the Editor of this column has receved a letter from Mr. J. Hazelip, (secretary of the Public Benefit Boot C 0.,) in which he says:. "You will be glad to know that my firm, who have had a little difference with the Shop Assistants on this matter, have withdrawn their agreement altogether and that henceforth the managers will not be required to sign any agreement. As a certain amount of unenviable notoriety has been given to my company owing to the dispute with the Shop Assistants, perhaps you will be kind enough to inform your readers that the agreement has now been withdrawn, and that the company are amongst those who pay the beet wages and observe the best conditions in the retail boot trade„ and as manufacturers are included in the best of the i fair shops in the town." ARE TRADE UNIONISTS SHORT-SIGHTED? _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>The resolution submitted by the Union of Carpenters and:Joiners at the last meeting of the Trades Council is vet, regrettable as it lends colour to the somewhat general opinion that Trade Unionists are a,short- sighted and narrow-minded lot. A little enquiry and consideration would have shown those responsible for the resolution that the Beamont School Managers have not the slightest intention of teaching carpentery or joinering. The manual instruction and practice in simple woodwork that is intended to be given is similar to that already followed in our local Grammar School, and other institutions, but nobody would seriously contend that the boys in the Grammar School are being taught a trade. Shall we abolish lessons in needlework, cookery, penmanship, drawing and painting? Would anybody advise that we do so ? Yet all these branches of education are taught, but not with the intention of providing cheap dressmakers, cooks, clerks, draughtsmen and artists. The fact is that the training of hand and eye entailed in manual instruction is part and parcel of any true system of education which seeks to develop harmoniously and concurrently the powers of body, heart and brain. A visit of inspection to the Beamont School would be very useful to the Trades Council, and there are no difficulties in the way. Labour men, above all others, should welcome and encourage the fullest education of the children. The future is on the knees of the gods, but an intelligent educated democracy will go far in fashioning that future to its own desire. The children are the coming citizens. Educate them to the full. We shall need all the knowledge we can get hold of in the coming time. _ . .</p>
        <p>" LAZARUS." MYSTERIOUS INITIALS.</p>
        <p>It is surprising to find what confusion there is in the mind of the man in the street" regarding the various labour organisations in Warrington, especially those known by the initials T. &amp; L.C., 1.L.P., and L.R.C. In all probability reference will be made from time to time in this column to these organisa- , tions, and in order that those interested in the Voice of Labour" may better understand the difference I between them I purpose giving a few facts concerning their constitution and history. This week we will take the Trades and Labour Council, which is the oldest labour amalgamation in Warrington, having been formed in the year 1875. For many years the Council was struggling on with a membership of some six or seven branches only. In its early days the Council gave financial assistance during strikes, etc., funds being raised for this purpose by enforcing levies of sixpence and threepence upon the affiliated mem- bers. Frequently when the delegates had assembled it was found "that there being no business to trans- act the meeting do stand adjourned." The difficulty now seems to be how to get through the business in the allotted time. The Council was represented at the Trades Union Congress on several occasions. Several attempts were made to attain representation on the Town Council and Board of Guardians, and for some time the C•mncil had two representatives on the latter body. During recent years the Council has taken no direct part in election matters. It is expected, however, that the result of the vote which is now being taken in the affiliated branches, will 'be a revision of the rules, and that probably next November, we shall find the Council putting forward independent Trades Union candidates. There are now 26 separate trades affiliated to the Council. The Council is ccnnected with the National Labour Party, the Lancashire and Cheshire Federation of Trades Councils (of wkich Councillor Stott is presi- dent) and the Warrington Labour Representation Committee. I ought to add that the Trades Council is open for membership to trades union branches only. Next week I will deal with the Warrington Labour Representation Committee. THE SCOUT. THE DIGNITY OF MANUAL LABOUR.</p>
        <p>Dear John,—l have read with interest your initial column in the" Examiner " of Saturday last, and I trust that the weekly contributions will be, in no sense, unworthy of the title "The Voice of Labour."</p>
        <p>Let me here say, John, that a great deal of nonsense is often written and talked about the dignity of manual labour by sentimental novelists,middle-class politicians and journalists, and members of the clerical, profes- sional and commercial classes. There is nothing necessarily dignified about manual labour; whilst much of it is absolutely degrading. It is morally injurious to a man to do anything in which he does not find a pleasure, and the tasks of colliers,chemical workers, scavengers, stokers, &amp;c., are pleasureless activities. To sweep a slushy crossing, drive a tram-car or even act as a policeman for eight or more hours on a wet day with an east wind blowing, are rather unpleasant situations, but to perform these services with mental, moral, or even physical dignity, appears to me an impossibility, whilst to fulfil these duties with " joy " is entirely out of the question. I also desire, John, to , impress upon you the fact that the majority of those men who are employed on the disagreeable and demoralising tasks are actually paid comparatively low wages and are more subject to periodical spells of compulsory idleness and consequent loss of wages than are those hand and brain workers who receive a higher remuneration, and whose occupations are of a more 'congenial nature Further than this, John, the , wealthy "superior" classes continually recommend the practice of thrift to the poverty-stricken wage•earners who are also told to order themselves lowly and reverently to all their " betters," and to do their duty in that state of life into which they are "called." To recommend thrift to the poor,John,is grossly insulting, and equivalent to advising a man who is starving to eat less. For a labouring man to practise thrift would be most immoral, and no human being should be willing to live like a badly fed animal, whilst a poor man who is really grateful and contented must be exceptionally stupid. In conclusion, John, I wish to di-ect your atten- tion to the following extract from the preacher of "Village Politics " "The modern Christ would be a politician. His aim would be to raise the whole platform of modern society. He would not try to make the poor contented with a lot in which they cannot be much better than savages or brutes.. He would Work at the destruction of caste which is the vice at the root of all our creeds and institu- tions. He would not content himself with denouncing sin as merely spiritual evil.He would go into its economic causes, and destroy the flower by cutting at the roots—poverty and ignor- ance. He would accept the truths of science and He would teach that a man saves his soul best by helping his neighbour."—Yours, etc., DEMOS.</p>
        <p>Communication from STANLEY LORD is held over until nest Week.—BLUE-BELL—We are afraid your philosophizings are too much for us.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
