      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>TEIE EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>TEIE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>MARCH 16th. 1907</p>
        <p>Further steps were taken ROME RULE. on Wednesday with the object of making Home Rule the supreme question of the moment. The EARL or DENBIGH had given notice of a question 'which was designed on the principle of those Japanese or Chinese boxes which contain almost Innumerable other boxes fitting into each other, 8° as to form what we believe is called " a nest." In the House of Lords a question may be Preceded by a speech, and Lord DENBIGH Prefaced his interrogatories by a discourse 'which extended over half an hour or so. It was merely an expansion of his question, the Object of which was to associate the Government 'ith declarations made by Mr. REDMOND or 4rs contrariwise," to entangle it in contradictions. The Opposition goes on believing that it can quite easily catch the Government in a trap, and it also Pea on demonstrating its complete inability to do anYtiling of the kind. The situation is certainly not withont its els-</p>
        <p>113,611,t ef Comedy. We are far from complaining toe debate which was raised on Wednesday. It 7" not only interesting, as Lord LANSDOwEE ueelared, but it was also most useful and 17alueb'e• It defined the present attitude of the two parties on the Irish question, and it gave the LORD CHANCELLOR the opportunty of making cne of the most frank speeches ever delivered in the *House of Lords. Lord LOREBURN'S extreme candour will be all the most conspicuous if it is kept, in mind that the allegation of the Opposition leaders is that the Government is engaged in a eenepiracy to hoodwink the country. Mr. BALFOUR "'erred two days since that it is committing a fraud r)11 the electorate. It would doubtless be cutting I If the Governrnent,as the EARL of CREWE remarked, /vere not somewhat indifferent to mock heroics and ahnulated indignation. The pretence that is expected to do great ser- l'iee for the moment is that the Government is afraid to give expression to its real views. It lA 141180 pretended, according to convenience, that it °lda views of a totally opposite kind. For Instance, it is said to have run away from Home Rillop and it is also said to have been engaged in PreParina legislation which will be the prelude to 4.tti_new Rome Rule Bill. The Opposition runs with i'ue hare and hunts with the hounds. In the culleet possible sense its acts up to Lord RANDOLFII ,IltritetrlLL's dictum, that the business of an Pris fosition is to oppose. shA ter the LORD CHANCF,LLOR's speech there ab°,llldi in all decency, be no more talking strn )fllng away from Home Rule at leaat C"ue PRIME MINISTER is, as I am," said Lord BeuRN, "a Home Ruler." The Liberal party had repudiated nothing. He alluded specifically to prominent members of the Government. Mr "ASQUITH, Mr. HALDANE, and some others had not abandoned their Home Rule views, and had no thought of abandoning them. Their speeches during the General Election could almost be con- as a declaration that Home Rule could not be dealt with during the lifetime of the pre.. sent Parliament, hut they had not gone hack on Mr. GLADSTONE's work, which, said the LORD CHANCELLOR, was "one from which this country will reap benefit and blessing Or ages to come." "Undoubtedly" he said " any Irish measure brought forward by the Government 13:111E4 tend in the direction of Home Rule. It was ePeesible to touch Irish questions without doing ;cleething tending in this direction. The measure ra° be brought forward would not be a Home Rule 10:arlre, hut it would be a measure extending eelf-g.,,overnment ; it would bring Home Rule to-ter.' "Whatever step you take," said Lord 13trini. "inevitably tends in that direction." human forces, great natural forces, were beat human in behalf of Home Rule. No other settle- of the Irish question was possible. For his Part he hoped that this settlement might soon ue attained.</p>
        <p>Doubtless many of our C4IIi3rAIcoPHoBIA. readers perused last week Ma the report of Mr. Len ae's address to the National Service Lague ao air of wonderment. If Mr. Maxse is an allthority on the delicate questions noon which he ,13:3a,ka so freely then "the mailed fist" is sus- a oVet our heads like the sword of Damocles ..nd ready to fall at any moment. Mr. Maxse pre- the German nation in a most evil light. 11.1ging by wnat he said the Kaiser and his Millions of subjects are literally thirsting for the ra of Uncle Edward and his peaceful. self- satisfied people. The be-all and end-all of German licY is, according to the Maxse theory, a overthrow of British national and commercial 03raroaesi. "The German Government," said 6r. Maxse, "'seems to be gradually convincing the ~,,,er,men People that England is the obstruction "'eh must be swept aside before Germany can ',lope to obtain that place in the world which she i?eards as to the one to which she is entitled." , "0 question arises as to what authority Mr. Maxse '"'°aaesses for speaking in this alarmist vein. Wel ws„Lalrely doubt the malicious intent which he zibutes to Germany, Gnd WA are very pleased to iecnive this week the letter, published on page 8, ;ram Mr. Ockel who has resided thirty-two years In Germany and made a study of the aims and aspirations of her people. Mr. Ockel says -Vainly and bluntly that the German hatred of „ngland does not exist, and that en the contrary ie People of Germany are better disposed towards V re English than to almost any other race. We pre- ?: to take Mr. Ockel as an authority before Mr j'IL arse. At any rate his views 'are those which are those accepted as correct in preference to of Gerrnanophobe agitators. It is possible that official Germany does not love England, but the creation of a huge fleet is no proof that it is</p>
        <p>REVISED LIST. PROPERTIES &amp; CHIEF RENTS. FOR SALE. POST FREE. abows at a glance the address, rental. outgoings. net income. and price asked. The properties are for absolute "ale. and any reasonable offer will be accepted. The following are abort particulars of lot Ilq out of list : RIGHT HOUSH9 £ e. d. Gross Renta1... All payments</p>
        <p>--- Net Incsotnq</p>
        <p>PRIOR £3511 17 PBR CBNT. INTICRUST. It Is Impossible to do better with absolute safety. Don't lakrable in Stooks and Shares or Mining Speculations, but 'Mit Your nanne7 to BRICKS AND MORTAR. 111431URBSTIIR PROPHRTIBS, FIAPBST INVBSTMIINT IN COUNTRY. MR. CLAYTON, 189 Albert Square, Manchester.</p>
        <p>aimed at our own safety. Kaiser William's vanity alone is sufficient to account for the desire to have a fleet second to none. We are sorry to see the National Service League descending to what say lurs very much of terrorist tactics, With the Channel Tunnel an accomplished fact they might have had a chance of frightening us into compulsory national service, but now that that schema has been " dished " we shall continue to dream on in that treasured island security which is so discomforting to the apprehensive Leaguers.</p>
        <p>There is a pregnant criticism UNREGENERATE of Mr. Balfour in the pages MR. BALFOUR. of this month's " Dawn " which is obviously penned by the Editor. As an official Conservative he ought to known better than to belabour his leader, but the spectacle of his out-Hunting Mr. Rowland Hunt is an entertaining one. We wonder if the Party Whip will fall in this case. This is what our friend says in " Dawn" : Mr. Balfour is as unregenerate as ever; that, in the continued and most lamentable absence of Mr. Chamberlain, he is still bewildering everybody by his fiscal jugleries and discouraging every true Imperialist by diluting the true imperial doctrine down to milk and water. It is bad enough to find him acidly repudiating either an Imperial Zollverein or an Imperial Council—the last of which his own Colonial Secretary (Mr. Lyttleton) proposed—and worse to find him taking all the body out of the idea of Imperial Preference (especially in view Of the impending conference with the colonies); but my anger fairly blazed when I read, the other day, that Mr. Rowland Hunt, the unionist member for the Ludlow Division of Shropshire, who had the courage, in the House, to tell his leader boldly what nine out of every ten Conservatives are thinking. had been struck off the the official list of Unionist members, or "excommunicated," as a leading daily aptly called it. This is the kind of thing which " ab-solutely " makes one sick, as I "Duff Winterden " would have said, and almost loathe the nauseating tyranny of party politics. If Toryism hopes to thrive by crushing out all individuality, and rigorously stifling every inde- n3ndent mind and tongue, and moulding all its followers to one dead level of enforced obedience to a leader who persists in flouting its declared opinion, Toryism is a doomed and dying force, and every man who loves his country and desires to serve it will begin to look elsewhere."</p>
        <p>The latest raid of the THE WO3IEN'S suffragettes on the House RAID. of Commons, though it was</p>
        <p>the biggest thing yet attempted, presented no really new feature. The women came in crowds, but the police were pre- pared for them, and at no time had they any difficulty in repulsing them. As a matter of fact, the difficulty of dealing with the women would never have been so great had it not been for the vast crowds of spectators who collected in the vicinity of Parliament-square. The point which strikes the public mind in connection with this latest affair is that the women get no nearer to attaining their object, if that object is to enter the House of Commons itself. What good pur- pose would be served by an invasion of the Chamber one fails to see. The women would not be allowed to stay, nor would their chance of obtaining the vote be increased. On their side it must he granted that they are acting strictly in accordance with the precedent of all great popular movements. The Reform agitation, the Repeal of the Corn Laws, the emancipation of Labour, were all accompanied by scenes no less disorderly and by struggles no less ridiculed by the class against which they were directed than those in which the women have engaged. One thin(' the public and the House of Commons cannot fail to recognise, and that is that the movement has taken too strong a hold to be easily checked. It has considerable monetary support behind it, and its recruits are increasing as the demonstrations increase. The proceedings at Westminster are certainly deplorable, but their object has been to bring the cause advocated by the women before the public, and in that they have certainly snc- ceeded. Parliament up to the present has not had the courage to acknowledge the claims of the women : hut on the other hand, it has not resisted them. The issue may be decided on Wednesday next if the advocates of Woman's Suffrage can manage to keep their friends in the House for the occasion.</p>
        <p>The Beamont School—thA OUR NEW SCITOOL. first institution of its kind</p>
        <p>provided by the Warrington ratepayers—was opened on Thursday with great eclat. With the unlimited resources of the rate payers at their service, the committee have suc- ceeded in erectine, a handsome building which is an ornament architecturally and provides the most modern educational facilities. Considering that the cost is over £l4 per head (including land) the townspeople were entitled to expect something out of the ordinary and they have got it. The school was the admiration of all who insplcted it; in- fact the only drawback of the luxury is the bill that has to he met. Moreover,the Beamont School is only the first of our Council schools and as the demands of the popula- tion increase, more of these buildings will of neces- sity have to be provided. The prospect, is not a very pleasant one for the long-suffering ratepayer, but all he can hope is that the Education Committee will exercise the utmost economy consistent with that efficiency which is increas- ingly necessary. The only cause for die- anpointment on Thursday was that Lord Stanley of Alderley, one of our most prominent education- was too unwell to fulfil his engagement. At the last moment Alderman Bennett was invited to open the school and he did so in one of the most effective and eloquent speeches we have heard him deliver. As everyone knows, tlse alder- man is an enthusiast in educational matters and in his address he placed before teachers and scholars the high ideals of which he is such a con- sistent advocate. We hone the Beamont School will take a worthy place amongst our educational institutions—in fact we have no doubt that it will when the inhabitants of the district main the exceptional facilities placed at their command.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
