      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>"It is so much easier to rail at Socialism than to try to understand it." * * *</p>
        <p>The new Beamont School is a very fine structure and we should imagine that after viewing it, parents will have no qualms at sending their children to the only free school in the district. Coroner Brighouse has a poor opinion of the efficacy of inquests in getting at the true facts of cases. At Warrington on Monday he said that a great deal more information could be obtained from witnesses by talking the matter over in a public house, with pipes and ale, than by questioning them in the box.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>117,</head>
        <p>117,</p>
        <p>" One of the vilest instrn' ments, one of the most abominable crocks to be found in Warrington " was the criticism hurled at the piano in a certain Warring- ton day school by a member of the Education Com- mittee on Monday. The long-suffering instrument, however, was not without its defenders, but after a, debate the committee decided that 10s. per annum was sufficient to pay for*its*eus* .</p>
        <p>The place for spinsters with an eye to matrimony is Fort Dodge, lowa, for the wedding bells are likely to be ringing one continuous peal in the near future. The local Council have passed a by-law requiring all able-bodied parsons between the ages of 25 and 45 who are not now married to obtain a licence to wed within GO days or be subjected to a fine of -5,2 to * * *</p>
        <p>Miss Annie Kenney, the militant suffragette, evidently aspires to emulate Cromwell. She has designs on the Mace, and speaking after the bloodless battle on Wednesday which resulted in the rout of the suffragist forces, she said "it would have been a beautiful thing if we could have got to the mace in the House." It would indeed. The spectacle of Miss Kenney occupying the floor and saying " away with that bauble" as Cromwell did in 1053 would be endowed with a singular beauty. * * * Amon, the private bills Parliament will be asked this session to consider is one backed by Mr. W. H. Lever, Mr. Vivian and others, for providing pensions for workmen over sixty-five years of age. The sug- gested method of raising the money is by a grant, equivalent to nine-tenths of the amount required, from the Imperial Exchequer, and one-tenth from the Local Taxation Fund (the "whisky money "). Registrars of births, marriages, and deaths, as being the people in possession of statistical information as to ages of the recipients, are to carry out the provisions of the bill if it becomes law. 41. _ .</p>
        <p>The Heathens' grumble at the recent gale is not directed against the buffetings to which they were subjected. Many of them were devoutly hoping that the unsightly hoardings on the village side of the Swing Bridge would not be able to withstand the fury of the elements, and would be laid low. Not so, how- ever. The wind hardly shook them and the Heathens are now hoping that their firmness is not symbolical of the Manchester Ship Canal Company, but that that august Corporation will be more easy to move and will sacrifice a very insignificant annual monetary return from the hoardings at the unanimous wish of the villagers, * * * There was a laughable incident at the meeting held in support of Mr. A. J. Tolley's candidature on Thurs- day evening. " Heckling " was pretty severe and at one stage feeling ran high. Matters had almost reached a climax when a solemn-visaged individual rose with great deliberation and said he wished to ask a question to which he hoped the candidate would give a straightforward answer. The audience waited breathlessly, expscting something serious, when the questioner calmly asked: " Who's going to get in on Monday ?" Needless to say the meeting was con- vulsed and Mr. Jolley did not venture on a prophecy.</p>
        <p>* • Men are not wise in openly proclaiming that they are outnumbered by women, for possibly women will not be content with founding on this a claim to the vote. They may go one step further and disfranchise Man. Members are always making jokes about what would happen if lady members sat with them in the House. Woman has an obvious answer. "We out- number you by nearly a million—so out you go and in we come." Thus Man would not be inconvenience d by the presence of women on the benches of the House as Man would not be there. He would be peepin.g coyly down from behind the bars of that cage in which Woman has had to languish, or you would see him being shown round the premises by ladies who would stand him tea and strawberries.—S. L. FICGIIM in "London Opinion."</p>
        <p>We are glad to see that the humble shop assistant is fighting for the rights which have been withheld from him so long by avaricious employers. The system of radius agreements, and living in, are both inhuman and unjust,a,nd it is only by combining their forces that the shop assistants can free themselves from the white slavery which has too long been their unhappy lot. The heavy fetters of radius. agreements —which tend to destroy tine individuality of the shop- man —are being gradually broken, but there are seven thousand managers still in the toils. Progress has been made rapidly of late, and 14 firms have between them freed from 2,000 to 3,000 shop managers from ' these cruel restrictions upon liberty. The Public Benefit Boot Company is one of the big firms which has most recently followed suit, and their public- spirited act is to be commended. * Few men in the Socialist ranks are so eloquent of as active as Mr. Victor Grayson who addressed a meeting at Warrington on Thursday. A former university student, he is a scholarly 'speaker with a masterly command of language. Not the least of the claims which he advances in support of the tenets of the faith he possesses is that of personal experience, as for the purpose of seeing how the sub- merged tenth exist he does not hesitate to sacrifice personal comfort in order to live in the lowest of the slums. Trying as his experiences have been they serve his purpose as he is thereby enabled to picture more graphically the horrors of slumdom. He burns with the zeal of a true social reformer and on Thurs- day night he commanded the undivided attention of a large audience for well . over an hour whilst he expounded his beliefs. 0 0 0 Canadians have long been seeking a national flag besides the Union flag which they share with the rest of the Empire. All suggestions hitherto brought 'forward have failed owing to the introduction of racial or religions symbols. Recently the question has been opened again hy a proposal of Mr. Ernest Girarlot, who was the Dominion Commissioner to the Milan Exhibition of last year. Mr. -Girardot's design has ! been reproduced during the past few weeks in the leading Canadian newspapers. It contains in the left-hand top quarter a Union Jack, with a beaver- stamped maple leaf in the quarter 14eneath. The rest of the flag is covered with nine vertical stripes, one for each of the nine Domipion provinces. To the objection that his proposed flag is suspiciously like the United States flag Mr. Girardot replies that no nation has a monopoly of bars or stripes. A similar objec- tion, he says, was made to the Australian national flag, on which there is a star for each province. * * * The provision of hospitals and organizations for the alleviation of the suffering of the sick and injured poor is a form of charity which has the first claim upon the community, and the apnreciation of this is shown in Warrington by the fact that the Infirmary is practically free from debt—a state of affairs, unfortunately, not to be reported in connection with many similar institutions. Locally for twenty years the annual income has almost balanced the expenditure, and with Mr. W. Peter Rvlands we are inclined to think this cannot be accounted for by mere coincidence, but that it is proof that iThen the money is wanted it is forthcoming. It is to be hoped that this is so, and that the extension and consequent increasing needs of the institution will be met by a aenerous response, and that the future will not see so deserving a charity burdened by debt. *_*_* _ _</p>
        <p>After over twelve month;;' existence the Warrington Distress Committee has at last realized a very impor- tant fact—that the Unemployed Act of 1905- does not grant sufficient powers to enable them, to any real extent, to grapple with the unemployed question. Under the Act the funds necessary for the establish- ment of relief works have to be raised by voluntary</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>Coming Events. Performances in aid of the Warrington Catholic Amateur Dramatic Society, will be given in the Parr Hell from April 4th to Gth (inclusive) in aid of the funds of the Warrington Children's Summer Camp. The Society is the St. Mary's Anaatuer Dramatic Society under a new name and is to be congratulated upon the selection of the play—" The ticket of leave</p>
        <p>The children attending the Warrington Gymnasium will give their annual display on Mond ty evening, and will also receive prizes from the hands of the Mayoress. Opportunity will also be taken of making a presentation to Mr. J. Kinsley upon his retirement from the office of instructor.</p>
        <p>Address to Men. Bishop. Welldon, the recently-appointed Dean of Manchester, delivered the last of the series of addresses to men at the Parish Church last evening. His subject was " The Atonement" which he dealt with most ably and eloquently.</p>
        <p>PREMIER IN NAME, Premier in qualit*-:1 Premier Cycles from seven guineas.—Sole agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt. The Lawson Centract. The Town Clerk has been served with a writ for .06,425 at the suit of lfr. George Lawson who claims the balance of sums due to him under the contract for work at Delph-lane. Education Committee The annual meeting of the Education Oommitte will be held at the Town -Hall on Monday evening when the business will include the appointment of chairman and deputy-chairman and committees.</p>
        <p>The Medical Officer of Health. The Health C )mmittee have considerd the appointment of a successor to the late Dr. Gornall as MediCal Officer of Health and recommend that a salary of :C450 be offered. The late Dr.Cloraall was receiving £5OO per annum.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constableship. The Watch Committee have met this week to con- sider the appointment of a successor to the late Chief Constable and it was decided to advertise for appli- cations for the plst. Candidates will be restricted to persons who have had exoerience in police ditties. The commencing salary is 5,350—f,100 less than the late Chief Constable was receiving.</p>
        <p>PREMIER CYCLES with Sturmey Archer three speed gear, nine guineas. Two speed gear eight guineas.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge-street Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Madame Butt's Illness.</head>
        <p>Madame Butt's Illness.</p>
        <p>It was a keen disappointment to local music-lovers that owing to the illness of Madame Clara Butt and Mr. Kennerley Rumford, the concert which should have taken place at the Parr Hall on Wednesday evening had to be cancelled. The distinguished vocalists were staying at Brighton and the doctor forebade them tu travel North. A concert arranged at St. Helens for Thursday evening had also to be abandoned. Madame Butt is hoping to fulfil an engagement at Leeds on Monday evening and to visit Warrington in May.</p>
        <p>Progressive Labour League. The annual meeting of the above League was held at the Liberal Club on Tuesday evening, Mr. J. Elwell jun., presiding in the absence of Mr. T. Barnes (retiring president)—Mr. J. -thrton presented the annual report which reviewed the work of the League and alluded to the public meetings addressed by Mr. F. Maddison, M.P., Mr. F. Neilson, and Mr. Havelock Wilson, M.P. —A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the retiring officers for their services. The follow- ing officers were elected : —President, Mr. Joseph Elwell, jun. ; vice-presidents, Messrs. J. Howes, Wilkinson Haigh, G. Walker, S. Wright, James Powell, F. Atkinson, Thomas Barnes,and F. Hitching; hon. treasurer,Mr. S. ViTright,j.P. ; hon.secretaries, Mr. J. Morton and Mr. J. Howes, jun.</p>
        <p>UNDER THE CLOCK for Good BOOTS. Advt.— Oddfellowship. The twenty-second anniversary of the Loyal Friend- ship Lodge of the Warrington District of Orlifellows was celebrated by a" smoker" at the Blue Lion Inn, Market-place, on Monday evening, when there was a good attendance of members and visiting brethren. N.G. George Wood was in the chair.—After the usual business of the Lodge had been transacted, the Chair- man called upon P.P.G.M. Bramhall to present a framed emblem of the Order to P.G. W. H. Percival for services rendered to his Lodge. Bro. Bramhall said it gave him great pleasure to make the presenta- tion to so worthy a brother, who, though quite a young member, had set an example which other members might follow with benefit. (Applause). Bro. Percival briefly responded. Songs were given by Bro. Daniels, Meachem, Perry, Burgess, Ravenscroft, and recitations by Bros. Knight and Appleby. Mr. Lake entertained the brethren with selections from his gramaphone.</p>
        <p>BABY CARRIAGES.—NOW and exclusive designs now showing.—!. S. GARLAND, 51, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Musical Evening at Crosfields'.</head>
        <p>Musical Evening at Crosfields'.</p>
        <p>The last and, in some respects, best 'concert of the season was given on Monday evening last in the Men's Dining Hall by the Crosfields' Choir and Orchestra before a very good audience of employees and friends. Both societies combined in the opening item Watson's " Gallant Troubadour." Thereafter the programme was alternated, the. orchestra giving Rollinson's Spanish Dance " Sobre la Plaza," an " Orchestral Sketch " and " Valse Lente "by Mr. F. 11. Crossley. A selection" Emerald Isle "by Sullivan and German —this the gem of tho evening —was most enthusiasti- cally encored. Waldteufel's familiar Waltz " Jeunesse Doree " and lastly Holzrnann's two step, "Blaze away" rendered with a verve and brilliancy which accorded with its title and kindled a responsive fire in the audience. The contributions by the choir, which alter- nated with ther,cornprised Wagner's Spinning. Chorus from" The Flying Dutchman" —very nicely rendered by 1 the female section with the support of the orchestra Pinsuti's " Tell me Flora " and " Tell me not in ' mournful numbers" The former of these was very daintily done, but in the latter there was a noticeable loss of pitch, probably owing to the' heat of the room. " Forth to the Meadows," 1 by Schubert (choir and orchestra) ; an old English , part song " Early one Morning" and the bridal chorus from " Lohengrin," a combined performance which elicited fruitless demands for an encore. In addition there were two instrumental solos. Mt. S. ! Pyatt gave with great acceptance "0 Dry those Tears "(Teresa del Riego) on the cornet and Mr. G. F. Bedford as a euphonium solo " Thou'rt Passing Hence " (Sullivan), this latter a performance which only failed of real distinction by a certain lack of inspiration which was felt as a hindrance even by those who most admire Mr. Bedforth's perfect mastery, of tone. Miss Clarke Nevall accompanied and Mr. F. H. Crossley filled his usual place as conductor.</p>
