      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <p>Local Will. Mr. John Clarke, of 270, Manchester-road, War- rington, who died on the 7th of last month, left estate valued by the widow, Mrs. Ellen Clarke, the sole executrix, at £5,661 4s. 101. gross, and £3,903 3s. 10d. net.</p>
        <p>LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDGE-STREET.—Adtv. The Author of "In His Steps " Warrington people will have the high privilege of listening, on Monday night next, to one of America's boldest, bravest and ablest ministers. The Rev. Dr. Sheldon of Tofeka, Kansas is announced to deliever a special address in Wycliffe Congregational Church and we besreak for our eminent visitor a hearty welcome and an appreciative audience. Mrs. Cherry of the B.W.T A. and the Rev. John Yonge will also speak. The chair will be taken at 8 o'clock by Mr. Charles E. Parker.</p>
        <p>Passive Resistance. At the Borough Police Court, on Tuesday, four pissive resisters were summoned for the non-payment of the education portion of the poor rate. The defendants, and the amounts owing, were : —Messrs. Thomas Dale, Priestley-street, is. 4d. ; Evan E. Hughes, Crosfield-street, 93. 6d. ; William Clatworthy, Lovely-lane, Ss. ; and Miss E. J. Field- house, Bridge-street, 12s. 9d. Mr. Dale told the magistrates that he refused to pay rates that were appropriated for denominational purposes. The usual orders were made.</p>
        <p>The New Post Office. On Saturday, the members of the Building Students' Association paid a visit to the new post office buildings in course of erection at Springfield-street. They were met on the site by Mr. Bowman, the Government engineer,in charge of the work. The engineer's office was first visited, where the plans, sections and details of the more important constructions and appliances were clearly explained. A tour round the building was commenced, the granolithic flooring. concrete par- tition walls, and natural asphalt roofs and damp-proof courses, being the subject of much interesting discus- sion. A vote of thanks heartily accorded to Mr. Bowman brought an instructive visit to a close.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT, UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASFIIRE.-Ailvt.</p>
        <p>Motor Accident at Warrington.</p>
        <p>A serious motor car accident occurred at Warring- ton on Saturday afternoon. A chauffeur named Charles Catbrill, employed by the Wrexham Motor Company, was driving a car to Chester from Haydock and when passing the Lord Rodney Inn ran over Thomas Smith, aged 50, a forgernan residing at 12, Amelia-street, who stepped off the footpath in front of the vehicle. The driver informed the police that he sounded his horn, but Smith appeared to be con- fused. Cathrill immediately pulled up, placed Smith on the car and conveyed him to the Infirmary. Smith is lying in the institution suffering from concussion and although his condition was at first reported to he dangerous, he is now making progress towards recovery. Kleptomania,</p>
        <p>On Wednesday the Frodsham magistrates deal with a somewhat unusual case The defendant was an elderly widow named Jane Simon, who has no settled address, and she was charged with stealing a variety of articles, ranging from a Prayer, book to a gridiron. She was engaged by a Mrs. Dickson, a grocer's wife, to assist in the removal of her furniture from Ashley Cottages to Durfell Cottages, and afterwards the following things were missed —A pair of window curtains, a tapestry table cloth, a blackled brush, a grid fireiron, a pair of sugar tongs, a blanket, a sheet, an apron, a Prwr-book, two table cloths, three metal table prongs, a spoon,and several plates. Prisoner was also charged with stealing a damask bed valiance and a bed quilt at a local auction. She was sent to gaol for a month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>PUBLIC BENEFTT.—UNDERITHE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN L ANC A SKIRE.—Advt. Handball. ,</head>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFTT.—UNDERITHE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN L ANC A SKIRE.—Advt. Handball. ,</p>
        <p>In connection with the Liverpool and District Handball League, a very exciting struggle took plate last Tuesday evening at the Borough Gymnasium, between Warrington and Egremont St. John's. As both these teams are in the running for the champion- ship, a keen game was anticipated. Throughout the game the throwing on both sides was uncertain in consequence of the rough tackling, which caused the referee to stop the play several times, and penalise the offenders The Home team had a penalty throw in the second half but failed at goal. The game finished with the score, Warrington 0, Egremont St Johns, 0. The next league Match takes place at the Borough Gymnasium, when Birkenhead fulfil their return engagement with Warrington, next Wednesday evening, March 27th.</p>
        <p>Death of Mr. F. Fearneley. Many Warrington people will receive with regret the news of the sudden death on Friday night of Mr. Frank Fearneley, of Salford, a gentleman widely known in political circles and particularly for his years of work in the Conservative cause. Mr. Fearneley, who was about sixty years of age, was suddenly taken ill at an hotel in Chapel-street, Salford, shortly after seven o'clock, and though quickly removed in a cab to the Salford Royal Hospital, ex- pired before medical aid could be obtained. Mr. Fearneley was a man of genial humour, and where politics were concerned had a reputation for enjoying a keen contest. He was one of the originators of the Convervative L-Lbour Party and as the leader of the Party conducted a vigorous campaign in Warrington immediately prior to the last general election. He was well known in our district, and was much respected, even by those who could not agree with his views.</p>
        <p>G" HERALD" KITCHEN RANGES &amp; Peveril " Fire rates are unequalled. Inspection invited. Large stock of Wood Chimney Pieces, etc.—J. S. GARLAND, 31 to 55, Bridge-st.,.Warrington.--Advt. Concert at Bold-street. Success attended the first annual concert arranged by Mr. E. H. Pearce, which was held in the Bold- street schools on Monday evening. Alderman Tinnion presided over a large attendance. The principal vocalist was Miss Marie Houghton, who was in excel- lent voice,her treatment of "Star of the twilight" being particularly artistic and effective, calling for a very hearty encore. With Miss L. Fairhurst she took part in the duet "The Elfin's call," for which the artistes were re-called. A sympathetic sendering of "The Raft" earned for Miss Fairhurst an encore. Mr. E. H. Pearce (baritone) delighted tile audience with his selections, his robust voice being heard to great advantage, whilst Mr. W. Ireland's tenor songs were much appreciated. Mr. W. Pearce and Miss Houghton acted as accompanists, the former also contributing a well-executed pianoforte solo. Mr. W. Parkinson gays an entertaining display of animated pictures. Mr. Alf. Grounds maintained his reputation as one of the leading humorists.</p>
        <p>Spiritualist Anniversary. In connection with the tenth anniversary of the Spiritualist Society, a public tea meeting was held on Monday evening, when a goodly number partook of tea. The after-proceedings took the part of an enter- tainment The chair was occupied by the President, Mr. Stout. Songs were rendered by Miss Armstrong, Miss Lester, Mrs. Genders, Mr.. J. E. Hannan, and Mr. R. Bailey, each artiste being encored. Mrs Jones (piano) and Misses Jones and Wilkinson (mandolines) gave some capital selections, and )Ir. Sidney Dark, a violin solo. Miss Mold was enthusiastically encored for her recitations, and Sybil Worthington's recitation was also loudly applauded. Lily Llewellyn, a tiny dot, delighted the audience with her rendering of the song " Only tired," and in respopse to an encore recited " Only a baby small." Migs E. Chapman, of Stretford, played a brilliant pianoforte solo, " The Hugenots,' and accompanied most efficiently the various vocalists, &amp;c. Junior Liberal Association.</p>
        <p>At the weekly meeting of the WarrinFton Junior Liberal Association at Gregory's Cafe on lionday evening a paper was read by Mr. L. Williams on the principal event in the life of George Washington. Mr. Williams gave a very interesting sketch of Washington's life, emphasizing his doggedness, his coolness in danger, Ins personal disinterestedness, and, above all, his faith in his cause. At the con- clusion of the paper, Jr. R. Lloyd Jones gMie a short resume of the difficulties with which Ifazzini (the great Italian Political Reformer) had to contend in the middle of the nineteenth century, and showed how the success he achieved was due to the singleness of purpose and the devotion to and faith in his causepome discussion on both subjects, led by the Chairman (Councillor Hawthorn) followed, and the meeting closed with hearty thanks to the principal speakers for a most interesting evening. Next Monday's meet- ing —the last of the session—is to take the form of a social, and an interesting programme had been I orringed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
