      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>CHILDREN'S INSANE DESIRES.</p>
        <p>I Mr. Brighouse, county coroner, on Tuesday held the inquiry into the death of William Pennington, aged three of 11, Peter-street, who was killed the previous day by being run over in Peter-street by a shandry. Isaac Twist, of 123, Peasley Cross-lane, a youth, who was driving the baker's shandry for Mr. T. Forster, provision dealer, Liverpool-road, said the child and two small companions were playing on the foothpath. Just as he turned out of Edward-street into Peter-street the children ran across the road. Two of them went behind the cart, but deceased ran straight into the pony, was knocked down, and the wheel passed over it.—The Coroner said it was an extraordinary fact that there appeared to be an insane desire in the minds of children playing in the public street to run across in front of a, trap, a cyclist, or a motor-car, to the very great danger of themselves and the travelling public.—The jury returned a verdict of "Accident," and exonerated the river from blame. IRISH M.P. AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>THE FRIENDSHIP OF THE LIBERAL PARTY. Mr. J. P. Farrell, M.P. for North Longford, on Sun- day afternoon addressed a crowded meeting of the members of the Wilfred S. Blunt Branch of the United Irish League, in their new clubrooms in the St. Helens Itarket place. Mr. P. M'Cormick presided. Mr. L. O'Clearey moved a resolution expressing entire coati- lance in the Irish party, and welcoming Mr. Farrell to the town.—Mr. J. Finch seconded, and congratulated the Irish party on the noble stand they had made for the defence of the rights of English and Irish Catholic children.— The resolution was unanimously carried.—Nir. Farrell replied in an eloquent and entertaining speech, and said he could assure the Catholics and the Nationalists and the Irishmen of St. Helens that in the hands of Mr. Redmond and the Irish party their cause was absolutely safe. No matter what party in Parliament might falter or fail, the eighty men who sat eternally in the cold shades of opposition would never be found either desert- ing their religion or their country. They had defended their religion against the present Government, and they were ready at all times to strike a blow for liberty and freedom. St. Patrick's Day was a most effective answer to those people who said that Ireland was not a nation. It was very fitting that on that day Irishmen should meet in all parts of the world to proclaim their fealty and adherence to the great principles which had guided their people so far, and from which they would never depart. He reviewed the great historial events of the past hundred years, and said that Irishmen would never cease to protest against the misgovernment and tyranny which had driven Irishmen from their homes into all parts of the world. In 1841 the population of Ireland was 8,500,000, but sixty years later, in 1901, it had fallen to 4,600,000. airing that time forty Coercion Acts had been passed by the House of Commons, and England had been robbing Ireland of from .C 3,000,000 to £3,500,000 a year in extra taxation. In the work of. remedying the grievances of the land of their fathers the Irishmen of Great Britain could without any reproach of selfishness take a foremost part. It was encouraging to review the progress they had made in the lifetime of the present generation. Since the time of Henry If. the land of Ireland had been confiscated piece by piece until the people of the country possessed nothing, but by means of the Parliamentary work of the past twenty- five years they had succeeded in handing back at least one-third of the land to the old stock, who were back in possession of the fee simple of the land of their fathers. (Cheers.) Then in 1898 the Tories thought to kill Home Rule by kindness, and gave them the County Councils Act. That had made no difference in their attitude to Home Rule, but it had given Irishmen an opportunity of showing that they could perform public work in the most satisfactory manner. Then the Labourers Act had made a tremendous difference in the housing of the labouring class. He was glad to know that St. Helens Irishmen supported their party so loyally. He believed sincerely that when the Irish people succeeded in getting possession of the land the government of the land was bound to be placed in their hands. The Irish party had good friends to-day in the House of Commons. They were no longer laughed and jeered at by the Tories, who mismanaged the Government for twenty years. The tide was not changed, and whilst there were people of whom they must be doubtful, still in the main and in the mass the great bulk of the Liberal and the whole of the Labour party were solid with the Irish people. (Applause.) One of the last votes he gave was in the division when by 295 to 58 Parliament declared that the Coercion Act was a shame, and that it should be wiped off the Statute Book; and on every possible occasion they had found warm-hearted, sincere, and earnest friends amongst the new Liberal party. They had differed with them on the religious question, but they had comraanded their respect. The Liberals knew their position, and realised the sacredness with which they as Catholics regarded that great question of religious 'education in schools. They knew that no political benefit whatever would induce the Irish party or the Irish people to betray the faith of the people of Ireland or England for any political mess of pottage, and they knew that they would never be a party to any policy that would work injustice to the children of any denomination. The Liberals gave them credit for all this, and, judging by the attitude of the bulk of the Liberal party, the Irishmen looked forward very hope- fully to the future.—A vote of thanks to Mr. Farrell was enthusiastically passed, on the motion or Mr Forshaw, seconded by Mr. Kilgannon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON</p>
        <p>SELF-DENIAL</p>
        <p>The annual self-denial week of the local corps of the Salvation Army resulted in a collection of over £3l— more than last year. OCCASTONAL COURT.</p>
        <p>At the Occasional Court, on Monday, before Mr. Ormand, Mary Baker was fined 10s. and costs, or 14 days, for being drunk and disorderly.—On Tuesday, be-fore Mr. Ormand, Michael Coyne and a man named Kelly were each fined 2. Gd. and costs for being drunk and disorderly. CONSERVATIVE SMOKING CONCERT.</p>
        <p>A smoking concert was held in the Conservative Club on Saturday evening. Mr. C. R. Pilkington was in the chair. Songs were given by Mr. Sidney Smith, Mr. Lea, Mr. Davies, Mr. Nicholls Mr. Mather, Molynenx and Mr. Harrison, the Earlestown Prize Quartette, and Lero Male Voice Party. WP:LSH SUNDAY SCHOOL TEA. _ _</p>
        <p>The annual Sunday school tea was held on Saturday in the Welsh Congregational schoolroom, Houghton- street, when about 50 sat down to a well served repast. The programme of the evening was composed of songs, recitations, violin solos; &amp;c. Mr. Maurice Williams spoke on the importance of young people of their Sunday schools making them- selves useful in any work they undertook to do, and helping one another, as the younger ones must be pre- pared to take on the work of their older sisters and brothers in the coarse of time. An impromtu dialogue was given, which was very amusing. IRISH DEMONSTRATION AT EARLESTOWN. _ _ _</p>
        <p>On Saturday night there was a great assembly of the members of the Davitt Pranch of the United Irish League and their friends in the Earlestown Town Hall. Dr. O'Keefe (St. Helens) presided,and was supported by Colonel Nolan, MT., Mr. \V. .1. Duffy, M.P. 'for South Galway, &amp;c.—Colonel Nolan, replying te a vote of con- fidence in the Irish party, pointed out that the spirit of coercion Irid, in the words of the Chief Secretary for Ireland, died, and was buried. He argued that the Irish party had set an example to the British .democracy in sending men from their own ranks to represent them, and submitted that, whilst the British democracy could by their votes strike a blow in favour of self-govern- ment for Ireland, they would at the same time be doing something for their own benefit, as self-government meant the finding of employmeat for Irishmen in their own country, and avoiding their coming over here to compete with British labour. Mr. Duffy submitted that there was no possible solution of the Irish question but by the concession of Home Rule.—The Rev. R. P. Farley (Bt. Helens), as a Protestant supporter of the National cause, believed that the old spirit of antagonism between Green and Orange was fast dying out, and that Irish people were coming to recognise that only in unify of purpose could they improve the conditions of their country.—A musical programme of considerable merit was also gone through.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>ROYAL SEA-BATHING HOSPITAL. PREVENTION OF CRIPPLES.</head>
        <p>ROYAL SEA-BATHING HOSPITAL. PREVENTION OF CRIPPLES.</p>
        <p>An admirable institution which is doing a noble work is the Royal Sea-Bathing Hospital founded at Margate in 1791. •The hospital possesses exceptional advantages for the treatment of early tuberculous disease of the hip, bones, joints and glands, but in their annual report the lion, visiting surgeons point out, that though a con- siderable amount of work has been done during the year 1006, as shown by the list of cases, they feel bound to state that the institution has not been utilized to any- thing like the full extent of its possibilities. The number of gland and joint cases admitted in an early stage of disease still forms an exceedingly small propor- tion of the whole. The great bulk of cases admitted had already undergone more or less prolonged treatment in other institutions, especially in Metropolitan hospitals, and it was probable that had they been trans- ferred to this seaside hospital in the beginning, the expanditure of both time and funds would have been lessened.Thehon.visiting surgeons think the possibilities of the Royal Sea Bathing Hospital as a place for the pre- vention of cripples by surgical treatment under the best possible conditions are insufficiently realized both by the pablic arri the profession, and should be brought more prominently before their consideration. Speaking at the annual Court of Governors of the Royal Sea Bathing Hospital the Earl of Derby said the necessity of early treatment of surgical tuberculosis in suitable surroundings did not vet appear to be suffi- ciently realised by the public and doubtless many cripples could have been prevented from becoming so had they applied for treatment at the Royal Sea • Bathing Hospital when first attacked by the disease. It Methodist Free Church. was his earnest hope and feeling that a good many very The annual choir sermons in connection with the charitable persons would come forward to help a work Buckley-street Methodist Free Church, were-preached of that kind. Lord Biddulph, treasurer, and Sir Henry on Sunday by Mr. G. Truman, of Liverpool, to large Lennard, Bart, chairman of the Committee of Manage- congregations. Special anthems were rendered by the ment spoke of the need for better day-rooms,dispensary choir, under the conductorship of Mr. N. Cook. Miss and a larger Electrical department. Mr. Stuart Sankey M. Plinston presided at the organ. The collections L.C.C., seconded the adoption of the report and referred were in aid of the choir fund a satisfactory sum being to the fact that £3,476 was received as patients' pay- realized. ments and trusted that this sum might be provided by increased public support and the patients treated free</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A GALLANT P.C.T.</p>
        <p>On Monday morning, the Police Court Missionary at Widnes (Mr. J. Fleming) performed a very plucky action. Mr. Fleming was in Dock-street, when he observed a woman named Guest rush out of one of the houses carrying a child whose clothing was ablaze. The woman seemed to have quite lost her presence of mind. Mr. Fleming at once seized the youngster and wrapped his overcoat around it, extinguishing the flames. In the process of so doing his hands were severely burned. The child was fortunate enough to escape very lightly ndeed. How its clothing caught fire is unknown. A WO ',PINT CRIPPI,E AT RUNTJORN.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn, on Monday, James Kershaw, a cripple who walks by the aid of a crutch, pleaded guilty to being drunk and disorderly and to assaulting Police- constable Wynne. At ten o'clock on Saturday night the constable received f;omplaints of the prisoner's con- duct, and he requested him to go to his boat. He replied that it was a lock-up job, and he might as well have six months as three, and he swung his crutch and struck the constable several times. Robbed of his crutch, the prisoner struck the constable with his fists, and tried to bite his face. Eventually Superintendent Ennion and Police-sergeant Allonby brought the ambu- lance and conveyed him to the police station.—Superin- tendent Ennion said the prisoner acted like a madman, and his language was the most obscene he had ever heard.—The prisoner had a record, and he was sentenced to a month's hard labour ori• each charge, tke sentences to run consecutively. PASSIVE RESISTANCE SALE AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>The sale of the goods of local passive resisters took place in the parade ground attached to the Widnes Police Station last Friday. The articles put up for auction were a gold watch seized from Mr. W. Knowles of Harrison-street, Halewood ; a silver cream jug seized from Mr. William J. Bell, Wood-lane, Halewood •, a case of silver knives and forks seized from Mr. Alexadder C. Mitchell, and two-electro-plated teapots, a coffee pot, sugar basin, milk jug, travelling bag and other articles seized from Mr. S. Robinson, of Widnes. The remainder of the Widnes passive resisters paid the "education portion "of the rate, under protest. The auctioneer was Mr. George Owens,of the firm of Messrs Alfred Grounds and Company. Mr. S. Robinson, Mr. Tuson, Mrs. Knowles and one other lady,with the police officials comprised the bidders. The articles were put up in one lot and the first bid by Mr. Robinson, was sufficiently high to cover the amount required. The articles were accordingly knocked down to him.—Mr. Robinson in a few words, expressed the thanks of the possive resisters for the courteous way in which the police had carried out the unpleasant duties of levying the distraints. He expressed the hope that that would be the last occasion upon which a gathering of that kind would take place.—Superintendent Strickland thanked Mr. Rohinson for his remarks and said that the police tried to carry our all their duties in a manner satisfactory to the general public.--The proceedings then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held at Dutton on Tuesday, when there were present :—Alderman Norman (presiding), Rev. C. R. Nunn, Messrs. A. H. Talbot, T. Baxter, S. Wood- ward, T. Wright, J. B. Chadwick, C. E. Linaker, W. IL Morris, T. Wood, J. Douglas, P. Collins, J. Timper- ery, J. Hallows, C. Roylance, J. Spruce. H. Eyes, J. Whitley, P. Mounfield, H. M. Davies, W. Weir, A. 3. Guest, J. H. Davies, G. Pilling, E. Bretherton, G. E. Warburton, R. Billington, T. Guest, H. Mawdsley. T. Antwile, C. Horton, A. Baoome, T. Rutter, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant clerk), J. Cunnah (master), T. Cook,J. Pearson, and W. R. de Prez (reliev- ing officers).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>TENDERS ACCEPTED.</head>
        <p>TENDERS ACCEPTED.</p>
        <p>The following tenders were accepted :—Groceries, Mr. E. S. Collinson, Frodsham ; butcher's meat, Mr. S. Hancock, Frodsham ; drapery, 14r. P. R. Wright, Halton ; clothing, Mr. T. J. Lee, Warrington ; drysalt- ing, Mr. H. Gibson, Runcorn; milk. Mr. J. Richardson, Dutton ; coal, Aiessrs.R. Evans and C 0.,; coke, Runcorn Gas Company ; coffins, Mr. J. B. Jones, Frodsham ; boots, Mr. J. W. Beardsley, Runcorn: fuaerals, Mr. E. Scragg and Messrs. T. Booth and Sons; labour, Mr. Robert Collins, Mr. T. Pollard, and Mr. J. E. Jones.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>INC RH ASE IN IN M ITES.</head>
        <p>INC RH ASE IN IN M ITES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Spruce reported that there were in the house 167 persons, as compared with 143 twelve months ago.</p>
        <p>EST I ITES.</p>
        <p>The Clerk submitted the following estimate of pro- bable expenditure-including county rate of £BO6B and deducting balance of £7247-.C11,323. The assessable value of the district was £227,219, so that a rate of is. in the £ would be necessary. The call upon the over- seers of the several parishes would be:-Acton Grange £ll3. Alvanley £76, Antrobus £127, Appleton £326, Aston £25, Aston Grange £25, Bartington £3O, Budwortn £lO7, Clifton £175, Crowley £6B, Daresbury £64, Dutton £378, Frodsham £630, Frodsham Lordship £4lO, Grappenhall £425, Halton £B4, Helsby £4BO, Keckwick £163, Kingsley £265. Kingswood £95, Latch- ord £l7O, Hanley £BB, Moore £230, Newton by Dares- bury £63, Newton by Frodsham £4l, Morley £l7O, Norton £295, Preston-o'th-Hill 180, Runcorn £2,900, Seven Oaks £47, Stockham £36, Rtockton Heath £66o' Stretton £96, Sutton £3ll, Phelwall £240, Walton Superior 2106, NVeston £650, Whitley Inferior £73, Whitley Superior £95. Mr. Ashton explained that tlfe call was exactly the same as for the last half year. Mr. Wood said he was surprised to find that the call upon Runcorn had gone up whilst for Frodsham and Helsby it had gone down. Mr. Ashton explained that this often occurred as in some parishes the balance decreased and an increased call had to be made to keep the balance up. RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL._</p>
        <p>The fortnightly meeting of the Runcorn Rural Dis- trict Council was held at Dutton on Tuesday when there were present :—Mr. Pilling (presiding), Rev. C. R. Nunn, Messrs. A. H. Talbot, T. Baxter, S. Woodward, T. Wright, J. B.Chadwick. C. E. Linaker, J. Hallows, C. Roylance, J. Spruce, H. Eyes, J. Whitley, P. Mounfield, H. M. Davies, W. Weir, A. G. Guest, J. H. Davies, G. Pilling, E. Bretherton, G. E. Warburton, R. Billington, T. Guest, H. Ilawdsley, C. Antwis, C. Horton, A. Broome, T. Rutter, G. F. Ashton (clerk), W. E. Hough (assistant clerk), J. Swinton and W.Diggle (surveyors), J. Farrington (inspector).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>DISTRICT COUNCIL POWERS.</head>
        <p>DISTRICT COUNCIL POWERS.</p>
        <p>The Clerk to the Cannock Rural Council for- warded a resolution upon the proposed transference to County Councils of the powers of Rural District Coun- cils, as to public health and housing laws. The Council considered that an unjust and unfair condemnation had been made of the whole of the Rural Councils in the Kingdom. The proposals would not result in any increase of economy or efficiency and would result in a weakening of the present control of the ratepayers over the expenditure of moneys raised out of the local rates. The Clerk further stated that the Reigate Council had forwarded a similar resolution. 11r. Woodward thought it would be be one of the greatest calamities which could befal the country. Districts should have self-government as far as possible. He moved the referring of the question to the Paalia- montary Committee. Mr. C. E. Linaker seconded. They could not have a better illustration of the mistake of vesting powers in the County Council than the Education Act. The resolution was approved. ESTIMATES.</p>
        <p>The Clerk submitted the following estimates: -probable expenditure £l,OBO, requiring a rate of 6d. in the R. Proposed call, Acton Grange £B7, Alvanley £42, Antrohus £5B, Appleton £l5O, Aston £lOB, Aston Grange £l2, Bartington £l4, 13udworth 251, Clifton £73, Crowley £3O, Daresbury £2B, Dutton'. £lB2, Frodsbam £331, Frodsham Lordship .£203, Grappenhall £2OO, Halton £ll2, Hatton £36, Helsby £244, Keckwick £72, Kingsley £l3O, Kingswood , £6O, Latchford £96, Manley £5O, Moore £lO5, Newton-by-Daresbnry £27, Newton-by- Frodsham £2O, Norley £B4, Norton £l4l, Preston-o'th'- Hill £B3, Seven Oaks £24, Stockham £lB, Stockton Heath £327, Stretton £4B, Sutton £l4l, Thelwall £ll5, Walton Inferior £l4O, Walton Superior £4B, Weston £306, Whitley Inferior £36, Whitley Superior £42.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>NORLFS WATER SUPPLY.</head>
        <p>NORLFS WATER SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>_ The Clerk estimated that the Local Government Board had consented to the borrowing by the Council of £2,315 for the construction of a water supply at Morley. The Clerk suggested that the engineer should get out estimates for the larger and the smaller scheme for Morley before the Council decided what sum to borrow. Mr. Linaker moved in accordance with the Clerk's suggestion, and the motion was approved. Speaking to the motion the Rev. C. R. Nunn said the fault lay with the Parish of Morley. When the matter was first introlkuced the Local Government Board would have accepteclany proposal from the Parish Council hut when that .:Jody declined to move the scheme was forced upon them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>MEDICAL OFFICER RE-APPOINTED.</head>
        <p>MEDICAL OFFICER RE-APPOINTED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Woodward moved the re-appointment of Dr. Adams as medical officer of health for the district. In Dr. Adams at the present time they had a most diligent and efficient officer. The motion was approved.</p>
        <p>ISOLATION HOSPITAL. The Isolation Hospital Committee reported that the hospital would be free from patients that week for the first time since its erection. Mr. Woodward said he was sorry that that did not mean that the district was free from infectious disease. At Norley there was quite an epidemic of diptheria but the people could not be prevailed upon to send the patients to the hospital. The Council had the power to enforce the sending of patients to the hospital but they did not desire to have to do this if it could be avoided.</p>
        <p>T</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>;1%7-</p>
      </div>
