      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>VICTIMS OF THE GALE.</head>
        <p>VICTIMS OF THE GALE.</p>
        <p>DROWNING FATALITIES AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A parcel of provisions was found on the Ship Canal wall at Runcorn Docks early on Sunday morning, and two Salford seamen, named Walter Williams and John Ward, were missed from the crew of the flat Atlantic (owners, the Manchester Oil Company), which was lying in the dock. It was surmised that the men were returning to the boat during the height of the storm, and that they were blown into the canal by the gale. The tide at the time was running very strongly. Grappling operations were conducted under the direc- tion of Dock inspector Moores, but without success, and Captain Byfield, of the local grappling corps, was summoned, and about ten o'clock recovered the body of Williams. The body of Ward has not yet been recovered.—Mr. R. Dobson. district coroner, conducted an inquest at the Runcorn Police Station on Tuesday.— Emma Williams, 67, Harris-street, Salford, said the deceased was her husband and was 5$ years of age. He was captain of the canal barge Atlantic. He was a man who drank very little beer.— Walter Alltoft, licensee of the Bridgewater Arms, said the deceased called at his house on Saturday evening and had a pint of beer with another man. They left shortly before eleven o'clock.—Thomas Shelton, canal constable, Handley-street, said that shortly after mid- night on Saturday he found a parcel of groceries on the canal side. He promptly reported the matt3r to his night inspector, and dragging operations were corn- menced.—ln reply to the Coroner, witness Alltoft said Williams was carrying a parcel when he left his house.—Mr. William Byfleld, Runcorn Grappling Corps, deposed to having recovered the body at the North Pier of the Manchester Ship Canal at 10 o'clock on Sunday morning.—P.C. Wynne said he examined the body, and found some bruises on the face and a deep cut on the left temple. He thought these had been made by the grappling irons. He raderstood that deceased and his mate were the best of friends. Deceased's watch was stenped at 11.22 o'clock.—ln reviewing the evidence, the Coroner commented upon the fact that deceased's companion was missing. The marks upon the face bad in all probability been caused after death. —The jury returred a verdict of "Found drowned."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>NOW TO KEEP FIT,</head>
        <p>NOW TO KEEP FIT,</p>
        <p>A WORD ABOUT MOTHER SEICiEL'S SYRUP, THE GREAT RESTORER OF WEAK STONIACHS.</p>
        <p>How to keep fit is a problem which becomes daily more difficult of solution; it is hard even to keep moderately healthy. And yet we must be fit to compete successfully in the rush, the struggle for existence. Why are we not fit? Why that weak, weary, run-down feeling, that half-life which so many struggle under? There are two causes. The first cause is the rush we have spoken of. The second cause is that the excessive activity of the nervous system—in short, the worry— orains the strength of the body; and the digestive drgans, robbed of their fair share of the great life stream which should course freely through every part of your frame, and weaken 34 perhaps by hastily-eaten meals, break down under the strain. That can be altered.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>STRENGTHEN YOUR DIGESTION.</head>
        <p>STRENGTHEN YOUR DIGESTION.</p>
        <p>These digestive organs, the stomach, liver and bowels must be assisted. More nourishment must be drawn from the food you eat, so that your whole body may have enough and to spare. Strengthen your digestive apparatus by taking Mother Seigel's Syrup, and all the nutriment your food contains will then be absorbed into your system and go to make rich, pure, vitalising blood. Thus every part of your body will be fully nourished, and health must result. That is the way to be fit. Says Mr. Albert Till, blacksmith, Royal Oak, near Marsden, Kent :—" Mother Seigel's Syrup has been our family medicine for years, and we have all benefited by its use. My wife used to suffer terribly from bilious- ness and indigestion, with pains in her back and stomach, wind and headache; but the Syrup quite cured her. I too have taken it end so have all my family, and as I have said, it does us all good."</p>
        <p>Take Mother Seirel's Syrup after every meal and you will soon be as well and fit as ever in your life.</p>
        <p>THE 2/6 BOTTLE CONTAINS THREE TIMES AS MUCH AS THE 1/i1 SIZE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>GREAT GALE.</head>
        <p>THE GREAT GALE.</p>
        <p>HOARDINGS BLOWN DOWN AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Passer-by Killed.</p>
        <p>A gale of extraordinary severity swept over•Warrington during Saturday night and wrought considerable damage. Trees were blown down, slates and chimney pots dislodged, while so heavy was the rain that the Mersey overflowed its banks, and Arpley meadows were flooded. One of the most disastrous effects of the storm was witnessed at Atherton's Quay. At about three o'clock on Sunday morning, when the wind was at its height, a wall running alongside the river opposite the tube works of Messrs. Monks, Hall and Co., col- lapsed and part of it fell into the water, the other part being left in a slanting position. The whole len1„.1 of the wall which has thus been damaged is about 1. yards. A great quantity of the embankment was also rried away, and a potion of the footpath rraning betwen the works and the river is very narrow ;n con- taluence. It had been noted for same time that there were signs of the wall giving way, owing to tides and the action af the water generally, aril atrangements were in progress for its repft;,.. Such tremendors damage, however, had never been dreamt of, and the cost tray be anything up to £3,000. MAX CRUSHED TO DEATH.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>JURY AND DANGEROUS HOARDINGS.</head>
        <p>JURY AND DANGEROUS HOARDINGS.</p>
        <p>The terriii3 gale was the cause of one shocking fatality at Warrington. Part of a bill-posting hoarding at the corner of Sankey-street and Parker-street collapsed and fell upon Benjamin Alfred Cheesborough, aged 22, an automatic turner, of Rose Cottage, Crow lane, Newton-le-Willows, who was walking to the station. He was pinned beneath the boards and when extricated was found to be so terribly injured that he died before reaching the Infirmary. The inquest was held at the Police Station on Tuesday afternoon by Mr. Coroner Brighouse. Afr. A Smith, solicitor, of Wigan, sppeared for the deceased's relatives, and Mr. Chapman (Messrs Chapman and Broots, solicitors, Manchester), represented the Warrington and District Billposting and Advertising Co,. Ltd., owners of the Jqoarding. The Borough Surveyor (gr. Longdin) was also present. The evidence showed that deceasc I came to Warring- ton by the 7.45 train from Earlestown, along with a friend named Arthur Tate, and attended a dance at the Workmen's Club, Market Place. Both left the dance at 10..10 but outside the club they parted and promised to meet again at Bank Quay Station in time for the 11.30 train to Earlestown. At 11.15 Daniel David Pepperell, 13, Nicholson-street, was proceeding home along Sankey-street, accompanied by a friend. When they got to Parker-street, they saw that the hoarding had been blown down, and on looking underneath they found Cheesborough. A number of men came on the scene and assisted to lift up the hoarding. Cheesborough was unconscious. A cab was sent for, and he wes conveyed to the Infirmary. He was there examined by Dr. Brown, the junior house surgeor, who said he was dead. In answer to the Cononer, Tate said it was blowing very hard at the time. Tn his opinion Gneesborough was dead when placed in the cab._</p>
        <p>The Coroner to the Borout.th Surveyor) : Have you heard any complaints about the instability of the hoarding, The Batough Surveyor: None whatever. He added that the hoarding was put up before the Corporation's Act of 1899, and the Billpesting Company bad to main- tain it and kcal) it in order. The Coroner: The Corporation have not found fault with the owners of the hoarding on the ground that it was insecure?</p>
        <p>The Borough Surveyor: I have nothing at all to say about it.</p>
        <p>At this stage Mr. Chapman expressed the deepest sympathy of the Billposting Company with the parents of the deceased in the sad loss they had sustained. They all knew that Saturday's gale was an exceptionally severe one.</p>
        <p>The Coroner said the jury knew what the structure was, and what the gale was, and they knew what the gale had done all over England. It was very unfor- tunate for Mr. Cheesborough to lose his son, but the only question was, "Was it an accident ?" The jury, after a short retirement, returned a verdiet of" k idental death," and added a rider that the three main supports of the structure were completely rotten, and that they were of the opinion that the hoarding was too high. The Coroner observed that they had never heard half the story at those inquiries. If they were to go into a public-house and have a gill of ale and a smoke they would get more out of a witness than they would in the box. He was told that most of the hoardings in War- rington were rotten at the bottom of their supports,and that they were too high. It was bad ,enough for Mr. Caeesborough to lose his son, but if one of those hoardings came down while children were coming out of school there would be some trouble. Mr. Smith said if anyone examined the posts they would come to the conclusion that they were rotten. They could stick their fingers into the wood. Mr. Chapman remarked that so recently as February 20th the hoarding in question was examined, and so far as any outward and visible signs were concerned it was all right. Saturday's gale was an excoptional one, and had it not occurred the hoarding would have been up that day. Hoardings bad come dowv all over the country. Mr. Smith said if the hoarding in question was examined in February, it must have been an examina- tion with the eye, and not the kind of inspection the public were entitled to. The Coroner said the jury thought the hoardings were too high, and were very dangerous. The higher they were the more stable they ought to be made in founda- tion. The Act of Parliament stated that "The owner or other person using any hoarding, wall or similar structure for advertising purposes, whether erected before or after the passing of this Act, shall at all time.; hereafter keep and maintain the same in proper and safe repair and condition." There was a clear obligation ' on the owners. It was decided that the Coroner should, on behalf of the jury, write to the Corporation respectfully request- ing them to see that the hoadings in the borough were , maintained in accordance with the provisions of the Act, and to say that the jury were of the opinion that hoardings ought not to be more than 12ft high. The jury expressed their sympathy with the bereaved family.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>Ragged School and Mission.</head>
        <p>Ragged School and Mission.</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening the parables of," The talents," "The tares," and "The ten virgins," illustrated with lantern slides were very well described by Mr. F. J. Owen and the lessons derived from them were most instructive. The collections for the day were for the Infirmary. On Monday evening an excellent pictorial concert was given by Messrs. Albert G-. Odell,Frank Owen And the Bros.Wrikht. The conjuring tricks of Master Lloyd were greatly enjoyed and Pro- fessor Vox ventriloquist, with his two dolls " Eliza and Jack " created roars af. laughter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH AFFAIRS.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH AFFAIRS.</p>
        <p>THE HEALTH OF THE DISTRICT</p>
        <p>In his annual report to the Runcorn Rural District Council, and referring to Stockton Heath, Dr. Adams says :—The growth of this township continues as a residential district. An extensive outbreak of scarlet fever occurred during the year, no less than 54 cases being notified ; also 1 case of diphtheria, and 3 of typhoid fever occured ; only 1 death however took place—from typhoid fever.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>ANTI-CIGARETTE SOCIETY.</head>
        <p>ANTI-CIGARETTE SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>• The first meeting of the Anti-cigaratte Smoking Seciety was held on Wednesday evening.—The Secretary Master Starkey said they had now about 40 members ranging from 10 to 17 years of age.—Mr. Hallows said that though their society had been working quietly it had been none the less successful and as far as he knew every membeli had been true to _his pledge. He believed the society bad been beneficial to the village. Sugges' ions were made whereby the pennies so spent might be saved, collected, and used towards a drive out in the summer, believing that to deal thoroughly with this and like questions you must offer some inducement for what you take away and to this end Mr. Hallows would be glad of any hints or financial help from any one interested in this matter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>SPRING BULB SHOW.</head>
        <p>SPRING BULB SHOW.</p>
        <p>The second annual show of spring bulbs, in connec- tion with the Stockton Heath Garden Society, was held in the Church Institute, Victoria-square, on Saturday. It was to be reeretted that the exhibits in several of the classes were not so numerous as was to be desired but numbers only were lacking, as the quality of the plants reached a high standard. The President of the Society, Mr. Vero B. Davies presided over the opening ceremony being supported by the Mayor and Mayoress of Warrington and Miss Smethurst, the Rev. J. Collins, Mrs. Vera B. Davies, and Messrs. R. I. Wynne-Jones, F. Pearson, W. Hickman, and E. W. Bindloss. The Chairman remarked that the membership of the Society had greatly increased and at the end of 1906 there were 485 names on the list. (Applause.) They were also able to give more money in prizes. Whereas at the last show only ,C 6 was given, there were that day prizes amounting to £l3. The society had taken two new departures, one was the extending of the radius to Latchford, and the other was the formation of a juvenile section. Prizes were being given to the school children for essays on the Garden Society, and tree planting. He hoped the trees and shrubs planted in the village mid on the canal bank would prosper, and that the members would keep their gardens full of flowers. (Applause.) Mrs. Wynne-Jones gracefully declared the show open. Mr. Bindloss, in proposing a vote of thanks to Mrs. Wynne-Jones, said they had been doing all they could t:-) beautify the village, and he hoped that in a few years the suburb would be one of the most charming places in England. (Hear, hear.) They were doing all they could to inculcate into all classes of the community a greater love for God's own beautiful gifts. He hoped they would all surround their homes with flowers and make them beautiful. (Applause). Mr. F. Pearson seconded, and the vote was heartily accorded. Mr. Wynne-Jones briefly returning thanks on behalf of Mrs. Wynne-Jones. On the proposition of the Rev. J. Collins, seconded by Mr. W. Hickman, a hearty vote of thanks was accor- ded to the Chairman. THE PRIZE LIST.</p>
        <p>The following is a list of the prize winners : Six hyacinths in pots: 1, Vernon ; 2, E. W. White- house. Three hyacinths in pots: 1, E. Verndn ; 2, J Astall. One hyacinth in a pot: 1, E. Velnon ; 2. P. Cliffe : 3, G. Whitfield. Three t.yacinths in glasses: 1, J. Mynekyme. One hyacinth in a glass: 1, P. Cliffe ; 2. J. Mynekyrne. Thrc 3 pots of single tulips 2, E. Vernon. One pot of single tulips: 1, P. Cliffe ; 2, J. Owen; 3, J. Mottram. Three pots of sinele narcissi: 1, J. Owen; 2, E. W. Whitehouse; 3, E. Vernon. One pot of single narcissi : 1, A. J. Mottram ; 2. E. W. Whitbouse ,• 3. J. Owen. Three pots of double daffodils: 1, E. W. Wnitehouse. Three pots of single daffodils : 1, A. J. Mottram. Onerot of double daffodils: 1, P. Cliffe •, 2, J. Astall; 3, A. J. Mottram. One pot of single daffodils: 1, Mrs. Har- gnaws ; 2,J. Astall. Miscellaneors collection of bulbs grown in one bowl in anything other than soil : 1, E. %V. Wnitehouse. One pot each of hyacinths, tulips, and narcissi, grown from bulbs supplied by Mr. G. Starkey, Golden-square, Warrington: I, E. Vernon; 2, E. W Whitehouce. One pot each of hyacinths, tulips and narcissi grown from bulbs supplied by Messrs. Thomas. Pierpoint and Co., Horsemarket-street, Warrington; 1, E. Vernon. .</p>
        <p>• Much of the success of the proceedings was due to efforts of a committee of which Mr. A. Fawcett was the hon. secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>HOUSE OF LORDS. MR, A. J. KING, M.P., FAVOURS THE REFERENDUM.</head>
        <p>THE HOUSE OF LORDS. MR, A. J. KING, M.P., FAVOURS THE REFERENDUM.</p>
        <p>The series of debates in connection with the Stockton Heath Liberal Association was continued on Tuesday evening in the Providence Schoolroom, Mr. W. H. Sherburn presiding. The subject for discussion was the House of Lords and at the outset the following letter from Mr. A. J. Kihg, M.P., for the Knutsford Parliamentary Division, was read :—" I am obliged by yours of the Bth instond am glad to hear that your association is so active. Dis- cussions of the kind you name are most useful for main- taining interest in public questions, and for helping the members to form clear and definite opinions upon them. With regard to the House of Lords, I regard this as one of the most lucent questions with which the Government have to deal. and in my opinion it should be tsckled without delay, and before the forces at the back of the present Government have been dissipated by the introduction of important reforms, which, whether they pass into law or are throu n out by the Lords, inevit- ably arouse the hostility of some vested interest. In reply to your definite questions, I think undoubtedly the power of the Upper House must first be limited. Wnen that is done it will be time enough to consider the basis of its reconstruction. As to the best means of limiting its power,l am personally inclined to favour the referendum. Let the House of Commons pass a strong resolution to the effect that the Upper House shall not have power to thwart indefinitely the declared wishes of the people as expressed in the House of Commons. In case the House of Lords disagree with an important Bill, then the Government shall have power to take a poll of the electors on that issue alone. An adverse result of such a poll must not, however' necessarily lead to a change of government. Once establish by this or some ' other means the supremacy of the House of Commons, then I should be in favour of reforming the Upper House in such a way as to restore its political complexion to something like equality as between the parties, and make it into a really useful working assembly. The hereditary principle should be itbolished, and Qn elected lelement introduced either from the County and Borough Councils, or in such other way as may be considered advisable. I should also abolish the Bishops as such. Universities and learned societies, such as the Royal Society, College of Surgeons, and even the various Churches might be given the power of nomination of a limited number of members. (Applause.) You may make what use you like of this letter, which, however, contains only hasty suggestions subject to modification as the result of further discussion.—Yours faithfully, A. J. KING." Mr. J. Battersby introduced the debate. Social reform, he said, should not be blocked by en irrespon- sible body of legislators throwing out or mutilating beyond recognition measures which had been passed by the House of Commons in fulfilment of a mandate given Ito them by the people. (Hear, hear.) Such a state of things was diametrically opposed to the welfare of the people. In the year 1906 alone the House of Lords had mutilated "The Agrieultural Holdings Act," "The Town Tenants (Ireland) Act," " The Education (Pro- vision of Meals) Act," . and bad rejected "The Plural Voting Bill," and had wrecked "The Education Bill." What should be done? Some said the House of Lords should be ended, which was easy to say. Others sugges- ted mending it, and that was also easy to say, but not so easy to achieve. There was no doubt that something</p>
        <p>would have to be done, and done quickly, otherwise legislation would be practically at a standstill, and the forces of the democracy reduced to impotence. (Applause). He moved: "That this meeting, while noting with satisfsction that it is the intention of the present Government to deal with the question of the House of Lords, considers that immediate action should be taken by the Government to adopt means whereby the will of the people as expressed through their elected i representatives n the House of Commons shall be made effective." (Applause). Mr. E. LLOYD JONES seconded. Having expressed his belief in the necessity of a second chamber he said he favoured the suggestion that Bills upon which the two Houses could not agree should be referred to a combined committee of six Liberal peers, six Conserva- tive peers, six members of the Government and six representatives of the opposition with a member nomin- ated by the Premier as chairman. An interesting discussion followed and ultimately the resolution was unanimousls approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. The Increase of Expenditure.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. The Increase of Expenditure.</p>
        <p>LIVELY DISCUSSION.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardiars was held at the Workhouse on Wednesday, under the presidency of Mr. J. Billingo. There were also pre- sent --Mrs. Wilkinson, Miss Broadbent, Messrs. W. Hickman A. J. Jolley, R. Forshaw, S. Davies, J. Pay- ton, T. Ashcroft, G. Thompson, L. Bent, W. C. Brooks, W. Flannagan, T. Ritchie, G. Mather, P. Long- ton, W. Houghton, A. Brooks, M. W. Seton, J. C. Sutton (clerk), J. F. Awbery (master), and A. Wright (superin- tendent of Padgate schools).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>TENDERS ACCEPTED.</head>
        <p>TENDERS ACCEPTED.</p>
        <p>The following contracts were accepted :—House: Fish and ice, Mr. W. Whittaker; meat, Mr. H. Singleton; groceries and necessaries, Messrs. Edwin Richardson and Son, Ltd.; wines and spirits, Mr. James Scotson ; flour and oatmeal, Mr. Joseph Mason; brushes, ac. Mr. George Ratcliffe ; clogs, 'Mr. William Jones; coffins, Mr. John Massey; coal, Wigan Coal and Iron Co. ; drapery, Mr. T. J. Lee; tailors' goods, Mr. James Critchley ; male officer's uniform, Mr. James Critchley; boots, shoes and slippers, Public Benefit Boot Co.; leather, Mr. 'Frederick Morley; drugs, Mr. H. M. Ashton; winding clocks, Mr. G. D. Southerden.—Schools: Fuel, Wigan Coal and Iron Co.; meat, Mr. T. Baldwin; drapery, Mr. E. A. Jones ; tailors' goods, Mr. J. Critcbley ; bread. Mr. J. Hallwood : brushes, Mr. G. Ratcliffe; groceries, Mr. W. Monks. FATHER RYAN'S RESIGNATION.</p>
        <p>Father RYAN having tendered to the clerk on March 6th, his immediate resignation of the position of Roman Catholic instructor, the House Committee resolved to recommend to the Board that Father Crotty be appointed to the vacancy at a salary of per annum. Mr. FORSHAW moved the adoption of the minutes. Mr. THOMPSON, in seconding, said Father Ryan was appointed religious instructor by the Board and he (Mr. Thompson) thought his resignation should have been accepted by the Board. It appeared, however, that Father Ryan went to the clerk and tendered his resigna- tion, and it was rather out of the ordinary course for any officer of the Union to be able to resign in that way. He did not wish to say a word to prejudice Father Ryan's candidature—he had too much respect for him— but he was merely speaking about officials generally. Was it right that any official should be able to go to the clerk and tender his resignation, unless it had been accepted by the Board, before his successor had been appointed? The CLERK said the act of resignation was purely the act of an officer. The Board might call upon him, if he gave an immediate resignation, to forfeits month's pay. The law was that the Clerk of the Union represented the Guardians between the periods of their meetings and no other officer could accept a resignation. Mr. THOMPSON: Could not Father Ryan have been nominated for the Board without tendering his resignation? The CLERK: I would have said he was disqualified if he had done. Mr. THOMPSON considered it was not right for the House Committee to recommend another person for the office without the Board having first accepted Father Ryan's resignation. The CLERK: If you dr 3ide that it should be not accepted, it falls to the ground. It must have the con- firmation of the Board. In answer to Mr. Bent the CLERK said it was open to the Board to suggest any other person for the chaplaincy. Mr. HICKMAN thought the Committee had taken the only course open to them as there must.be some spiritual adviser temporarily appointed. The minutes were approved. THE NURSING STAFF.</p>
        <p>The RESIDENT MEDICAL OFFICER submitted a report to the House Committee calling attention to the inadequacy of the nursing staff and to the hours of duty Sr.c., and it was decided to hold a special meeting to consider the whole question. COST OF VACCINATION.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from the Leigh Union forwarding a copy of certain minutes passed by the Board on the subject of vaccination. The minutes contained a letter from the Local Government Board stating that the Board had given directions for the preparation of an order dealin* with the matter of the fees to be paid to public vaccinators. The Gateshead Union had also considered the question and had decided that each of their public vaccinators be given 28 days notice ter- minating their present contracts, and at the expiration of that time they should hand over the books and papers in their possession relating to vaccination. Boards of Guardians throughout the kingdom were asked to take similar action. The CLERK advised the Board to defer action until the Order was issued. Mr. HOUGHTON: I would like to act At once if possible. I feel so strongly on the matter. The thanks of the Union are due to Mr. Jolley for his work on behalf of the Union in regard to this question. I don't consider the Local Government Board's proposal goes far enough. It is only an instalment of the modifica- tion of the scale of fees. Mr. BENT thought they ought to support the Gates- head Union.</p>
        <p>The CLERK : It is simply an anticipation of the Order and you cannot rely on what the Government or any branch of it may do. They tell you they are going to do something. Mr. JOLLEY : There is no reason to doubt that the Order will be speedily issued. THE EXPENDITURE OF THE UNION. ALLEGED "ALARMING INCREASE."</p>
        <p>The CLERK submitted the estimates for the ensuing ball year, showing that the actual expenditure in the corresponding half of last year was £15,731 Bs. 2d., and the estimate for the coming half year totalled £15,461. The amount put down for in-maintenance at the Work- house was £2,600 as against £2,636 is. 2d., and at the schools £1,050 as against £1,052 14s. Cd. Out-relief (including non-resident poor) was estimated to amount to £2,500 as against £2,473 4s. 9d. Mr. FORSHAW said they had managed to come before the ratepayers with a call of 6d. in the £, and yet they were called an extravagant Board. The fact that the rate was so low gave a perfect answer to such criti- cisms. The Board was in a far stronger position as regarded the ownership of property than it was when he first became connected with it. Its position continued to improve,and yet they were not asking the ratepsyers for more than 6d. He thought when it was stated that the Board was a spending Board it was only fair that some one should say those statements were incorrect and had no foundation in fact. Mr. HICKMAN : Witt you sive the name of the person who has made the statement that it is a spending Board? _ _</p>
        <p>Mr, FORSHAW : I did not say any person. Mr. HICKMA.N But you have made a charge against</p>
        <p>• someone. Mr. FORSHAW : It has been stated publicly that the Board is a spending Board. Mr. HICKMAN : When and where? Mr. FORSHAW : Can you say it is incorrect? Mr. AsHcrtorr : Mr. Forshaw should have said the majority of the Board is not a spending Board Mr. FORSHAW : T shouldn't have said anything of the sort. Mr. HICKMAN : lam sorry you have raised the question, especially at this time. Mr. FORSHAW : You need not have any sorrow. Mr. THOMPSON said that at a meeting held in Warring- ton a few days ago certain statements were made. The speaker, however, should have gone a year or two further back.</p>
        <p>Mr. BEnT : I don't think this question ought to be brought forward. The CHAIRMAN : The observations have something to do with the estimates now before you. Mr. THOMPSON : I have a perfect right to deal with the expenditure of the Union. Mr. 13zYr : These are electioneering addrasses. Mr. THOMPSON said that in the year 1902 the expendi- ture of the Union was £6,900 and now it was £7,893. That was a very alarming increase and a great deal of it was due to the salaries of officers. That they could not avoid, but still, taking the whole position, there was a slight decrease in the demands for the last one or two half-years, especially the last half-year. The reason for that, however, was the decrease in the nuMber of inmates.He must say that for some years the expendi- ture had gone up, as Mr. Mather had stated, by leaps and bounds and it was quite time something was done 1 to check it. _</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN said that in the good old days 25 or 30 years ago, the call for Union purposes used to be about ls.lod. per annum. The fact that the Board was now calling upon the ratepayers for 6d. in the £ for the next half year indicated to intelligent minds and sensible business people that they were getting value for the money spent. During the last twenty five years they had built schools, and R new hospital, had paid close upon £l,OOO more for vaccina- tion purposes, re-organized the nursing system, and improved the whole administration. Where expenditure could be out down, the greatest economy was exercised. The Board had always been strong believers in out- relief, where it was conducive to the good of the recipient and when the law would allow it. Mr. ASHCROFT said that if it had not been for the opposition of some of the Guardians the expenditure would have been frightful. They had prevented large buildings being erected at great cost. Mr. HICKMAN, referring to the Chairman's statement that the call had been reduced from is. 10d. to 6d. in the last twenty-five years, asked what the rateable value of the Union was at the beginning of that period. The CLERK said the whole proceedings were dis- orderly. (Laughter.) The only question before the Board was the estimates for the coming half-year. He had no reason to believe that the call would be more than 6d. in the 2.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. MATHER, seconded by ir. A. BROOKS the estimates were passed.</p>
        <p>THE LUNACY WARDS.</p>
        <p>A letter was read from Dr. Irving, Commissioner in Lunacy, referring to a recent visit to the House. He said that with regard to the female inmates of the lunacy wards,they were well and kindly looked after,the dormitories were clean, and the beds properly made. With regard to the male wards, however, he offered certain severe criticisms.</p>
        <p>The report was referred to the House Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>WESLEYAN QUARTERLY MEETING.</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN QUARTERLY MEETING.</p>
        <p>New Ministers Invited.</p>
        <p>The quarterly meeting of the Warrington Wesleyan Methodist Circuit was held at Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church on Thursday evening. There was a very large attendance. The Rev. Sidney Pitt presided and was supported by the Revs. Willism Mellor, J. Manning, E.J. Gawne, John Gunnell and E. J. Smith. The last- named, who is the newly-appointed minister for Lymm, was introduced td the meeting and suitably replied. The SUPERINTENDENT reported the largest member- ship of the Circuit for many years, the present numbers being 914 members with 90 on trial—an increase on the year of 105. Reference was made to the death at Padgate on Tuesday last of Mr. Henry Ward who had been Sunday school superintendent for twenty-five years. A vote of condolence was passed with the family. A satisfactory financial statement was submitted by the stewards, the income meeting the expenditure. Mr. R. J. MARSH, of Stockton Heath, was unani- mously recommended as a candidate for the ministry. Mr. F. A. HARRIS presented the chapel report, show- ing the total value of the properties in the circuit to he £45,000. Ten of the trusts reported a balance in hand and seven a debt.</p>
        <p>The Revs. Sydney Pitt, William Mellor, and E. J. Gawne were heartily thanked for their services during the year, testimony being borne to the value of their work, and they were invited to remain in the circuit another year. Special reference was also made to the work done at Bewsey-road by Mrs. and Miss Pitt.</p>
        <p>The Rev. THOMAS MILLER, of Nottingham, was invited to succeed the Rev. J. Manning who leaves for Preston in September next. The Rev. E. B. KEELING, of Middlesborough, was invited to become superintendent of the circuit at the conference of 1908. It was decided that June 23rd should be observed throughout the circuit as the Local Preachers' Mutual Aid Association Sunday. ' The circuit stewards (Messrs. John Upson and J. H. Meldrum), together with Messrs. A. Taylor and F. Johnson were elected representatives at the synod to be held at Bootle in May. Messrs. Mitchell and Corbett were elected substitutes in case of any of the repre- sentatives cannot attend.</p>
        <p>Temperance returns were presentc,d by Mr. J. Monks and showed an increase in adult total abstainers, but a slight decrease of members of Bands of Hope. __</p>
        <p>The next meeting will be held on Janne 13th at Penketh.</p>
      </div>
