      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Mr DEAR NIECES AND NEPHEWS,--.</head>
        <p>Mr DEAR NIECES AND NEPHEWS,--.</p>
        <p>I hope you have not been counting too much this week ont seeing a long list of easy puzzles, etc., as I promised you I would re- vive; because, to tell you the truth, I have not yet been able to get a nice lot together, but hope to do so either the next week or the one after; up till then I will give A weekly prize to the boy and girl who sends in the best con- tribution during the week. Upon looking over my membership book I could not fail to notice what a difference between the number of Nieces and Nephews; to every Nephew I think I must have got quite five Nieces. Now this will never do. Whatever would happen were I to have you all at my house to a party, with five girls to every boy; I am afraid the boys would have rather a rough time of it, wouldn't theyt so we must alter it, and this is how we'll dolt: I will give another weekly prize to the girl who gets the most boys to become mem- bers and contributors to our Corner. I don't think you will find it very difficult, with your feminine persuasive powers. At any rate, you'll try, my dears, won't you? With this week's post I have received one or two more cards for the competition which has just closed, otherwise they would certainly have stood a good chance.</p>
        <p>Now I must conclude, with best wishes.</p>
        <p>UNCLE RED.</p>
        <p>WELCOME TO Hilda Hencheliffe, Hilda Rutter.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l should very much like to become one of your Nieces; also one of my sahool friends, Hilda Rutter, who lives at Hill-street. I am sending a piece of poetry, called:—</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>FROGS AT SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>FROGS AT SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>Twenty froggies went to school. Down beside a rushy pool; Twenty little coats of green, Twenty vests, all white and clean. "We must be in time," they say, "First we learn, and then we play. That is tow we keep the rule When we froggies go to school." Master Bull-frog, grave and stern, Called the classes each in turn; Taught them how to learn and strive, Showed them how to leap and dive. From his seat upon a log, He taught them how to say, Also how to dodge a blow From the stones the bad boys throw. Twenty froggies grew up fast: Big frogs they became at last; Not one dunce among the lot, Not one lesson they forgot. Each can say, "Ker-shog," and "Shoe," As a froggie ought to do. Now they sit upon a log, Teaching other little frogs.</p>
        <p>"Ker-shog,"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>LITTLE PUSSY.</head>
        <p>LITTLE PUSSY.</p>
        <p>I love little pussy, Her ooat is so warm; And if I don't hurt her, She will do me no harm. I will not pull her tail, Nor drive her away, But pussy and I Very gentiv will play. She will sit by my side, And I'll give her some food, And pussy will love me, Because I am good.</p>
        <p>HILDA HENCHCLIFFE.</p>
        <p>HER NEXT HUSBAND.</p>
        <p>The widow of a small tenant farmer was re- turning from her husband's funeral, weeping bitterly, when she was joined by a neighbour, who tried to console her.</p>
        <p>"Cheer up, woman," said he, get another husband." "Oh, no, no!" she replied, "Who would take me?"</p>
        <p>"You'll soon</p>
        <p>"Why, I would take you myself (loOking at his feet), if only I had a new pair of boots." The widow, lifting her faee, and wiping her eyes, said, earnestly, "I wonder if John's would fit you."</p>
        <p>CONSTANCE MARGARET BRAYLEY.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncl• Ned,—l am writing to ask you if I may become a member of your "Children's Corner"? If I may, I will send a story and a riddle next week. Hoping you are quite well, • I remain, yours sincerely, CONSTANCE MARGARET BRAYLEY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <p>V.,..,</p>
        <p>Dear .Uncle Ned,—l do like your "Children's Corner." It is so nice to read the, interesting storiee 'hand letters from your Nephews and Nieces. 'I feel as though I should like to be a Niece of yours. Will you have me—l am only nine years old? Remaining yours hopefully, AMY FOWLZIL.</p>
      </div>
