      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>NOT THE SAME THING</head>
        <p>NOT THE SAME THING</p>
        <p>At a college in Cambridge there was a naster who was extremely fond of figs.,. He watched his fig tree very closely and tenderly, 'or he held that in the existence of a fig tree Lhere was but one fit and proper time at which the ripe fruit should be eaten. To eat a fig either before or after that.supreme moment was, said the master, a neglect of an opportunity and a sad mistake.</p>
        <p>One year, for some reason, the tree produced only one good fig; and one day the master's examination of this fig led him to the conclu- aion that it would be at its best the day follow- ing. Then he did an exceedingly foolish thing, considering that there were undergraduates about. He wrapped hits precious fig in some silver paper, and labelled it, "The Master's Fig." At what he judged the right moment of the next day, the master went to the tree, anticipat- ing a brief but exquisite pleasure. Alas the fruit had vanished, and the empty branch bore a label, with these words, "A fig for the master l"</p>
        <p>ARNOLD BOLTON</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l received the box of choco- lates this morning, and thank you very much for it. I am sending you a short story, which I hope you can find space for. It is called, "A Father's Love." Now I must close. IeIARGERY HOCKLEY. A FATHER'S LOVE.</p>
        <p>A farmer, who was left with three helpless children, on his return home about midnight from the fair of Clare, found his house all in a blaze. His first exclamation was, "Where are my children? I must relieve them, or we must perish together." He ran to the yard, where, fortunately, there happened to be a lad- der, which he put against the wall. He rushed into the flames, and succeeded in penetrating into the room where the children were in bed. He had already taken two of them in his arms, when a third, the youngest, a beautiful girl, cried out, "Sure, father, you will not leave your own little Hannah in the fire !" The distracted parent took up the little child, wrapped in her night clothes, in his teeth, and, by God's good- ness, he escaped without any serious injury to himself or to his precious burden. The house, with all the furniture, fell a prey to th,:.&gt; flamcs. Li. H.</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—l should very much like to become a member of the "Children's Corner." I have been very interested in it for a long time, and in the last letter you wrote you said you had a bad cold, which I hope is better now. I am thirteen years old, and am in the Seventh Standard. I am sending you a shbrt story, called:—</p>
        <p>1. t.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>SHARP RETORT.</head>
        <p>A SHARP RETORT.</p>
        <p>A train was carrying, in a compartment of the third class a clergyman and five or six young ragamuffins, rascals who, to annoy the minister, kept scoffing at religion, and tell- ing disagreeable storie6. The good man endured it all, hearing every- thing without answering, -and without being moved. Arrived at his journey's cud, he got out, and only remarked, "We shall meet again, my children." "Why shall we meet again?" said the leader of the band. "Because lam a prison chaplain," was the reply.</p>
        <p>MILLICENT M. MASON.</p>
        <p>A JOLLY GAME. - THE BIRD DEALERS</p>
        <p>The game of the bird dealers i played as follows :—The children stand in a row, leaving two outside. These two represent the bird dealers. Each child represents a bird, one being a crow, another a crane, another a canary, and so on.</p>
        <p>One bird dealer says to the other : "I wish to buy a bird." "What kind of bird?" asks the eecond dealer.</p>
        <p>"A bird that can fly fast," saYs the first dealer.</p>
        <p>"Very welli" answers the other dealer. "Take what you wish." "Then," says the first dealer, "I will take a robin."</p>
        <p>As soon as the word is out of his mouth, the " robin " must leap from the row and run around to escape. If the dealer catches the bird he puts it into a cage, where it must stay till all the other birds are cauglit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <p>4-</p>
        <p>If a pin lies on the ground with the head to- wards you, pick it up, as it foretells good ltrck, but avoid any pins which have the points to- wards you, as this meahs b.ad luck. You know the rhyme:—</p>
        <p>"-See a pin and pick it. up, All that day you'll have good luck, See a pin, and let it lay, You!ll have bad luck all that day."</p>
        <p>..................+•.....i.</p>
        <p>WHAT WAS IT?</p>
        <p>Guess what he had in his pocket! Marbles and tops and sundry toys, Such as always belong to boys, A bitter apple, a leather ball?— Not at all. What did he have in his pocket? A bubble pipe and a rusty screw, A brassy watch-key broken in two, A fish-hook in a tangle of string?— No such thing. r , What did he have in his pocket? Gingerbread crumbs, a whistle he made, Buttons,- a knife with a broken blade, A nail or two, with a rubber gun?— Neither one.</p>
