        <p>EFFECTS OF ANSTHETICS ON PLANTS. Experiments with ether and chloroform show that as the effect of these drugs varies in differ- ent individuals of the human race, so in different species of plants different results are obtained by their action. Alfred Burgestein has experi- mented with these reagents on buds, on bulbs, and on seeds. In some cases the growth was ac- celerated, and in others, the begonia, -for example, retarded. Narcissus bulbs sprouted a week earlier under the influence of ether, but onions were retarded three days. Tulips under the same treatment were unaffected. Again, while forty-eight hours' exposure of a bulb to ether vapour had no injurious effect, eight hours' exposure to chloroform killed it. The growth of seeds was accelerated twenty-fou! hours by ether, but the proportion of seeds gor- minating was not altered. Chloroform was found to accelerate some and retard others, while the proportion of seedlings produced was generally reduced. If the seeds were first soaked in water, ether diminished their growing powers, and chloroform generally killed them. Plants of bean, pumpkin, and sunflower were almost entirely arrested in growth in an 'atmosphere containing one-two-hundred-and-fiftieth per cent, of chloroform, while the same proportions of ether promoted their growth. They even throve in an atmosphere containing three times this amount of ether.</p>
