        <p>SCIENCE NOTE-1 AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>A ITITIF TOT L MA RIME,</p>
        <p>A tew for the mall Ifactu re of r,rtificial marble as been rat ented in Berlin. Abbestos, dyeing ins feria'3, sliac. and atsh• are pounded into a stiff 3.71a0s ?.rid then subject to high pressure. The product is surprisingly firm and tough, not brittle, is very easily worked by means of tools, can be given a fine polish, and in appearance cannot be distin- guisho from genuine marble.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturda7, March 23, 1907.</p>
        <p>smiple and let -mysterious to the fayman. .i•or measuring the lighting power the most reliable results, says. Health, are obtained by means of a grea,se spot. In its inost,simple application the e-xperMlent can be tried eatily at home. A sheet of white paper with a grease spot in the centre is put into a frame and placed between two flames of different lighting power—for installer between an ordinary candle and a lamp. , When the frame is equally distant from the two unequally bright flames, the grease spot can be seen plainly on both sides. By moving the frame with the sheet of paper slowly towards the less brilliant light—that is, the candle—it will arrive finally. at a point where the grease spot has apparently diapi:.eared on both sides of the paper. Thi3 deception must always occur when on both ide o he paper an equal brigll !mess prevails at,d no side lights exist. Having ar- rived at this point, it can be asscertained how much stronger Is the light of their lamp than that of the candle. If the candle is twenty inches dis- tant from the paper and the lamp sixty inches, and yet the brightness on both sides of the paper is the same, then the light of the lamp will be as strong as that of nine candles. The calculation is based upon the distances, the figures of which are multiplied by themselves and then divided—here, for ir4anee, sixty times sixty divided by twenty muitipaect by twenty equals nine. This, of course, is the most primi- tive method for measuring light, but it is the principle for all the delicate instruments uwd the laboratories.</p>
        <p>GILCHRIST LECTURES: STEWARDS ENTERTAINED.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Ormand, hon. secretary to the local committee of the Gilchrist Lectures,last week entertained the staff of stewards, to a splendidly served knife and fork tea and supper at the Town Hall. The interval lv,tween tea and supper was most agreeably spent under the genial influence of the host, by the company giving their impressions of the lectures and lecturers and criticizing the same, also their experiences in the different work allotted to them. Mr. Ormand was thanked for his hospitality, and he, in tuill,thanked his friends for their valuable help and assistance.</p>
