      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>POWER FROM TT-IT: TIDER</head>
        <p>POWER FROM TT-IT: TIDER</p>
        <p>Attention is once more to this .iubject by the proposed sstablishment of a p-wer-plant based on the use of the tides to compress air at Rock- land, Maine, United States. At that place there exists a natural tidal basin covering an area of about one square mile. The average rise and fall of the tide is 12ft. It has been calculated by the engineers in charge of the project that 5,000 horse-power can be developed, assuming an efficiency of 70 per cent. for the air-compress- ing apparatus. It has been shown that com- pressed air can be transmitted long dis- tances through pipes with very little lose of power, and it is proposed to supply such air to stone-quarries situated within a radius of twentj, miles from the tidal basin. It is pointed out that many similar tidal baeins exist eleewhero along the seacoast.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>OUR VITAL ENERGY</head>
        <p>OUR VITAL ENERGY</p>
        <p>'A curious paper was read at a meeting of the French Academy of Sciences. It had relation to a study—really interesting in its way—made by a Roumanian physiologist, Dr. Paulescu, of the hypophysis, that tiny organ of the size of a pea seated at the base of the brain, which Descartes declared to be the seat of the soul. The result of Dr. Paulescu's researches is to show that Des- cartes had better excuse for his theorisings than is the case with some so-called philosophising&amp; Dr. Paulescu has amused himself by removing the organ in dogs and cats, but could not induce one to survive the operation more than twenty- four hours. From which it appears that in some mysterious way the hypophysls is closely bound up with our essential conditions of life. In giants the organ may attain to the size of a large chestnut, and it is also of abnormal size in per- sons having very large heads, hands, and feet.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>LIGHT AND BLOOD</head>
        <p>LIGHT AND BLOOD</p>
        <p>According to a notice published in the German medical press, Dr. Oerum, of the Finsen Insti- tute at Copenhagen, has carried out a number of experiments on the effect of light on the blood, of which a few results are given in the following: Darkness has been found to reduce the total amount of blood by 3 to 3.3 per cent., while decreasing at the same time the amount of blood contained in the heart. Red light will exert a similar action to dankness, while blue light is apt to result in an excess of blood, and an increase in the amount contained by the heart. Light baths are apt to increase the amount of blood in the course of four hours. Darkness will reduce the , amount of blood con- tained in the heart within three to four weeks, and intensive light will have the same effect in four hours. Darkness will increase, and inten- sive light will reduce the blood pressure. Ani- mals born in the dark or in red light have a greater weight but only half the amount of blood of those born under normal conditions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>PREVENTING CONSUMPTION,</head>
        <p>PREVENTING CONSUMPTION,</p>
        <p>There was formerly the same attitude towards the plague, typhus, and smallpox, diseases that then ravaged the civilised world, and which now have been so far successfully warred against that they at present hardly figure in mortality statis- tics. Proper sanitary measures may accomplish much the same result with tuberculosis which now causes more deaths than any other disense. But before such measures may be formed or en- forced, the public whose will the laws embody must first learn and believe that the thing is worth while. It must be widely known, for in- stance, that tuberculosis is caused by a living germ conveyed from man to man principally through spitting, that the disease is now know:: to be not hereditary, as for many years it was thought to be, that its cure rests as a basis upon fresh air and sufficient wholesome food, and that its prevention depends upon the proper care of the sputum of the consumptive and the prohibi- tion of those methods of life and industry which weaken the resisting power of the human bcdy to the ever-present infinitesimal tubercle bacillus, The true import of these things realised by the public and the century will see us released from the great scourge which now levies its fearful annual tribute.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>FLYING MACHINES.</head>
        <p>FLYING MACHINES.</p>
        <p>Colonel J. D. Fullerton, R.E., lecturing before the Aeronautical Society on "Wings v. Screws," expressed the opinion that practical flyers would soon be ,as cheap as bicycles. The lecture was illustrated by practical experiments with wing models designed by Major Moore. These were of the flying-fox type. By means of a clock- spring the wings were caused to flap up and down, and thb model being placed on a groove. slide, a jerking, forward movement of the con- trivance took place, shewing that an up and down movement of wings designed on this prin- ciple will produce a forward propulsion. The conclusions arrived at by Colonel Fuller- ton were that the propeller in the form of a screw, as in a ship's propeller, is quite as effi- cient as the wing type, and that the former is the safer form, as a machine fitted with bird. wing propellers only would lott highly dangerous if the motor were to stop =Mentally when the wings were at an awkward angle. M.. Jose Weiss, the well-known landscape painter, showed some successful model bird. flights with pieces of thin cardboard cut into the shape of a bird with outstretched wings, and weighted with pieces of lead to imitate the weight of the bird's body. These . gliders soared across the large hall of the Society of Arts in a series of curves, exactly imitating the flight of a drooping sea-gull. A paradox of flight also demonstrated by ex- periment is that the stronger the wind blowing against a certain form of curved wing-surface the less resistance does the bird offer to it!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>CANDLE POWER.</head>
        <p>CANDLE POWER.</p>
        <p>The question of candle power has been until recently of interest to scientists only. But since one method of lighting is competing with the.' other for superiority, the question of lighting power has seemingly become a public matter. nasun vudt Pk aunctian 11 vary</p>
      </div>
