      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>NEW THEOLOGY. MR. CAMPBELL'S. New BOOK.</head>
        <p>THE NEW THEOLOGY. MR. CAMPBELL'S. New BOOK.</p>
        <p>(REVIEWED BY THE REV. F. B. MEYER.)</p>
        <p>In this book of 264 pages Mr. Campbell sets forth a statement of ,his personal views on "The New Theology.' To employ his own words, "it is intended as a concise statement of the teaching given from the City Temple Pulpit." . There is very little that is fresh. Those who have read his later sermons and essays are familiar with nearly all the contents of the various chapters. To turn to these pages for an exhaustive and profound argument is to court disap- pointment. They bear traces of the haste with which they have been prepared. The reader is constantly reminded of the impres- sionist style which is characteristic of Mr. Campbell's preaching. For this reason the ! book will be widely read and discussed in circles that would not concern themselves with the ordinary theological treatise. Per- haps this will not be considered an objec- tionable feature, for whatever else Mr. Campbell has done he has certainly started the whole world talking theology. This is what Alexandria did in the fourth century, and we might talk of worse things! There are two or three beautiful descriptions of Cornish scenery and stories of the sea, which are a little out of keeping with the graver letterpress, but give tone and colour. The air of dogmatic certainty is slightly offensive. We have no fault to find with a. ; man who does his own thinking as the pre- face puts it. But we like to 'feel that an , author treads reverently, arid that his soul ' is penetrated with the mystery which al- , ways flings its blue haze around these lofty; themes. After all, we can only push the ' thick curtain back an inch or two. Such ' statements as "I do not think the Atone- ment is such a very great mystery after all " ; "It is the fashion to declare the pro- blem of evil insoluble, but I have the audacity to declare that it is not so"; "Mr. Blatchford's moral earnestness is a mark of his Christhood, and his work is a part of the Atonement; I know the thing when I see it "—hurt the reader. Of course, Mr. Campbell may be the Prophet of a New Dis- pensation which is to revolutionise the venerable conclusions of the past, but we could wish that he were not so sure. Let others place the crown on his brow. The issue of this book must, I think, precipitate a final and irreconcilable break between Mr. Campbell and the Evan- gelical party as represented by Dr. Parker. It is impossible to maintain the position, that his is only another way of stating the Gospel. That Jacob wore Esau's clothes did not make him Esau. However the old nomenclature may be employed, the familiar terms Sin, Atonement, Sonship, Salvation, Judgment, do not mean the same from Mr. Campbell's mouth as they did when they were published to the world by the New Testament writers. If Mr. Campbell is right we may as well put the Bible on the shelf and discuss the whole question of re- ligion afresh from the standpoint of human philosophy and reason. In our author's own words, 'the psychological must take prece- d..nce. of the historical." This is the method he pursues. The Authority of Scripture is not discussed till the eleventh chapter. He says there: "It is the Divine Self within every one of us Which enables us to discern the truth best fitted to our needs . . . . If men could only come to see this more clearly, and to trust their divine nature, they would not suffer themselves to be hampered by formal and literal statements of belief, whether in the Church, the Bible, or anywhere else." It is useless, therefore, to confront Mr. Campbell with passages of Scripture. Whenever they conflict with his theories he sets them deli- berately aside. "I question," he says, "whether we should ever have heard of the Old Testament if it had not been for Sesus, and the New is only a statement of what some good men thought about Jesus and His Gospel at ;the beginning of the Christian his- tory." At the close or the chapter on the Authority of Scripture he says—and we only quote. the sentence because it so clearly sums up his position—" Never mind what the Bible Says about this or that if you are in search of truth, but trust the voice of God within. you." It is clear, then, that there is no com- mon ground between the minister of the City Temple and the ordinary Christian apologist. It is no longer 'a question of the interpretation of Scripture, but of philosophy. He has re- moved his appeal from the Court of Scripture to that of Philosophical Discussion, and there the case must be settled Here are some of the statements which evangelical Christendom will keenly contro- vert: "Paul's opinion is simply Paul's Opinion, and not necessarily a complete and adequate statement of truth " ; "The noble 53rd chapter of Isaiah and all similar pas- sages about the prophetic conception of the suffering servant of God have, literally under- stood, nothing whatever to do with Jesus " ; "The Christ, Who is the deeper self of every human being, is saving individuals by filling them with the unselfish desire to save the race " ; "I say frankly, that there is no such thing as punishment, rio far-off Judgment Day, no great white throne, and no Judge external to ourselves" ; "Paul's theory as to the resurrection of every physical human body is just nonsense in the light of our larger knowledge of the universe and its laws, and we may as well say so." The essence of tli New Theology is un- doubtedly stated in the chapter on God and the Universe "We get two modes of God---- the infinite, perfect, unconditioned, pri- mordial Being • and the finite, imperfect, conditioned, and limited Being of which we are ourselves expressions. Yet these two are one, and the former is the guaranke that the latter shall not fail in the purpose for which it became- limited." The paragraph from which this sentence is taken is fairly puzzling. In one sentence we are told that God is infinite consciousness, and in the ;next that He needs to manifest to Himself the possibilities of His Being by limiting :it; and we fail to see how such Immanence .as Mr. Campbell pleads for, and which .amounts almost, if not altogether, to Pan- theism, is compatible with the simultaneous transcendent existence of an Infinite Being. The only distinction between our being ;and God's is that of the bay and the ocean. Free-will, in the sense of unlimited free- dom of choice, does not exist. Evil is nega- tive, the shadow where the light may be, the absence not the presence of something. Jesus was God manifest in the flesh because His Life was a consistent expression of Divine love, but not in any way that would cut Him. off from the rest of human kind. .Jesus was the child of Joseph and Mary, and had an uneventful childhood. When- ever a man sacrifices himself for others you have the Atonement. Sin is selfishness. _Such are the leading features in this treatise. The exposition has all the charm of Mr. Campbell's personality. Throughout there is a passion for righteousness. Evidently he believes in prayer, and is sincere in his desire to know the riddle of the world. We gravely question, howew?r, whether he is going to solve the question of pardon for the penitent, and power for the paralysed will. In our judgment. if this teaching were to be generally adopted, the restraint which Chris- tianity has taught and enabled men to exert, ' would be greatly relaxed, and human responsibility ler wrongdoing obscured. Mr. Campbell eulogises Canon Wilberforce, Father Tyrrell, and the Rev. T. Rhondda Willjains; has a kind word for Mr. Blatch- ford and Genera' Booth; and feels sore with the editor of the British Weekly. "James," said Charles 11. to his brother, "they're not going to assassinate me to make you King " • and surely humanity is not going to part with the Gospel as we have been taught it to install these philosophic speculations in ita vlace.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith.</p>
        <p>RADICAL.—The letter signed "Conservative "did not appear in our columns.</p>
        <p>The Congo Horrors. Sir,—May I venture to correct an error which occurs in a recent leader of yours on the Congo Reform meet- ing held in Warrington. You say that 15,000,000 human lives have been sacri- ficed in the Congo during the last 20 years through the mad lust for wealth of King Leopold and his associates. The highest estimate of the depopulation caused since 1892, when the rubber decrees came into force is 3,000,000. The entire population of the Congo territory has never exceeded 20,000,000 probably, and that figure is do doubt an exaggeration. Putting the population • down at 15,000,000 twenty years ago, the disappearance of 3,000,000 would mean a fifth of the entire race—a sufficiently ghastly record.— Yours faithfully, E. D. MOREL. (Hon. Sec. Congo Reform Association, Oldhall-street, Liverpool.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>Shop Assistant's _Grievances.</head>
        <p>Shop Assistant's _Grievances.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l regret that absence from town on Tuesday evening last prevented my attendance at the meeting of the shop assistants. I shall be glad if you will permit me in this way to express Me sympathy with their pre- sent contention. It seems to me that the agreements which the shop assistants so strongly protest against are of a character which ought not to be attempted. I am very glad that the prominent firm referred to in the , proceedings of the meeting has seen its way to retrace its steps in this direction ; and I trust that the agitation now promoted by the shop assistants will result in the whole attempt at fettering the freedom of employes being entirely defeated. I quite agree also in the remarks ot one of the speakers at the meeting about the living-in system, especially when it is carried out as it too often is. There have been, and doubtless still are, employers pursuing that system who make very admirable provision for the comfortable home-life of their servants. But these, it is to be feared, are very rare exceptions to a general rule, whereby assistants have very scanty com- fort, and very much impeded liberties. The way for instance, in which in London assistants are practically turned out in the streets on Sundays, is a scandal and a shame, and an infamous disgrace to the employers who adopt the system. I write these lines in order to let it be known by the promoters of last Tuesday's meeting that my cordial sympathies are with them in their present movement.—Yours, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>JOHN YONGE. 19, Wilson Patten-street, March 21st. 1907.</p>
        <p>Germanophobia. Sir,—l trust your readers will not be misled by the statement* of Mr. Leo Masse as to the existance of Anglophobia in Germany. During a residence of thirty- two years in Germany, and prolonged visits during the- last six years, I have never experienced or perceived any hatred for England. It does not exist. On the ' contrary, the people of Germany are far better disposed towards the English than to almost any other race on earth. There are many reasons for this. The over- whelming majority of the Germans are Cosmopolitans. The two nations are not only near akin in blood, but in thought and aspirations. England has never crossed swords with Germany, and the two countries stand in the closest possible commercial i elations with each other. In literature, in art, and in the sciences we are closely allied. English scientists are held in the greatest esteem in Germany, and Shakespeare is held in greater regard in Germany than in his own country. Shakes- pearian performances are commoner in Germany by scores of times than they are here.</p>
        <p>It is not difficult to account for Germanophobia in this country. A servile press, acting from sordid motives, has been endeavouring to rouse the passions of the people for some years. It is a trap of the meanest and basest description and one that the people of this country would never be caught in, if they could but understand that it is the monopolists in this country who are their chief enemies and who foster this hatred of foreigners in order to secure themselves in their monopolies. An enquiry will show that the reason of the rapid develovment of Germany during the last dozen years lies in her high level of education ; tha fact that, notwithstanding her protective tariffs, there exists within the Empire the purest Free Trade system of any country in the world ; and the nationalisation of the railways, ensuring to the people of Germany fares and freights at half the rate of English charges. Canals connect all the great rivers, and the freights are very low as compared with this country. The same remark applies to post-office service. An 11 lb. parcel is carried 50 miles for 3d. and to any part of the Empire and Austria-Hungary for 6d. The great advantage of this cheap method of intercommunication can be seen in the building up of the foodstuffs and clothing industries, producer and consumer being brought into direct contact. Telephones and telegraphs also belong to the Government, and the profits from these various monopolies go to the reduction of taxation.</p>
        <p>The-greatek advance of recent years is To be found in the fact that in Germany about 300 of the largest towns levy Iktax on the unimproved value of land vary- ing from 5 to 7,?,i per cent. Although therefore the principle of the single tax on land values, is still far from being realised tc the full, a great advantage has thus been gained. I again express the hope that the people of Warring- ton will not be misled by the activity of any German- °phobia agitators. Their best interests are served by fraternising with their fellow democrats on the continent. The frightful consequences of a war between this country and Germany have been vividly pictured in that remarkable hook, "The God of this World," by John B. Middleton. In my opinion an aggressive war on the part of Germany would be immediately followed by a revolution far more disas- trous than the French revolution in 1789, and this opinion is shared by the majority of people in Germany. This will be understood when I say that the over- whelming majority of the great German army consists of Socialists, who are opposed to a war of aggression. We want industrial freedom, brought about by free land, but those who endeavour to raise the angry passions of Germanophobia in this country know,or ought to know that war leads to the further enslavement of the masses and the enrichment of the few.—Yours &amp;c.,_ _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>R. OCKEL. March 21st; 1907. The Trades Council and the Board of</head>
        <p>R. OCKEL. March 21st; 1907. The Trades Council and the Board of</p>
        <p>Guardians.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Owing to an engagement which kept me at the Town Hall [was late in arriving at the last meeting of the Trades Council. When I read the report of the meeting in last Saturday's "Examiner "I was surprised to find what had been said by Messrs. Grealy and Hickinhottora (the Bakers' representatives)in reference to the candidature of Mr. James A. Heywood, the Labour candidate for Whitecross Ward. My feelings regarding the expressions of the two Bakers' delegates on trades union matters are generally feelings of admiration, but I have to confess that in regard to the incident in question I cannot compliment them upon what they said. Either they have a prejudice against the I.L.P. and Mr. Heywood, or they have been mis- informed as to the facts in regard to the candidature. Mr. Councillor Evans—against whom I have nothing to say, he and myself being' excellent friends—was not first "in the field" for the Guardians' election. The facts are as follows :—The I.L.P. have for some time been trying to secure candidates for the Guardians election with the hope of getting assistance for Mrs. Wilkinson who has been the only Labour representative during the last three years. Owing to the fact that all the meetings are held during the day,very few working- men can offer themselves as candidates for the Board of Guardians. After considerable trouble, the I.L.P. were able to prevail upon Mr. Heywood, who has the time to spare, to consent to become a candidate. When the question of selecting a ward came before the meet- ing, it was thought, for obvious reasons, that the present representative for Whitecross Ward ought to be Opposed, whereupon it was resolved, by a unapimous vote, that Mr. Heywood should be nominated for that Ward. This decision was arrived at three weeks before the meeting of the Liberal Ward Committee was held, and furthermore, Mr. Heywood's name appeared , in the "Examiner " as a candidate for White- cross Ward ten days before the date of the Liberal meeting. Of course, I am not in ' any way complaining of the action of the Liberal Ward Committee, for we recognize that either they or any , other party have as much right as ourselves to take ' what action they think best in the interests of their party. lam stating these facts to prove to Messrs. Grealy and Hickinbottom that Mr. Heywood was not selected after it was known that Mr. Councillor Evans was nominated, for no one was more surprised than ourselves to find that Mr. Councillor Evans had been selected. We were assured, however, that the object of the Liberal Party having been obtained by Mrs. Clay declining to seek re-election—that Mr. Evans would withdraw from the contest. However, no withdrawal , did take place and the result is that there is a contest for the vacancy. What would the Bakers' delegates have had to say if an employer in the Engineering or Building Trades had been in opposition to a Labour candidate and the engineers'or joiners'delegates on the Trades Council bad spoken in favour of the employerand against the Labour candidate? The I.L.P. however, is composed of the kind of men and women who are not discouraged by tactics of the kind in question. Weare working for the direct and independent representation of Labour on all our local governing bodies, and will endeavour to steer straight for that goal. We welcome honest criticism and Opposition. The term "blackleg "has frequently been used by men on the Trades Council, and with some degree of justification. I trust, however, that none of us will become "political blacklegs" a brand which may become nuite as dangerous as those of an industrial brand.—Sincerely Yours,</p>
        <p>March 21st, 1907.</p>
        <p>FRED STOTT.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, March 23, 1907.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, March 23, 1907.</p>
        <p>GUARDIANS ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>Spirited Gontests.</p>
        <p>Polling for the election of representatives on the Warrington Board of Guardians takes place on Monday, and the result will be declared from the Kendric%-street schools shortly after nine o'clock the same evening. The pollinr, booths will be open from 12 noon until ' S p.m. and as follows :—Town Hall Ward, Infant schools, Kendrick-street ; Wb.itecross Ward, St. Barnabas schools ; Bewsey Ward, 93, Hoyle-street, and St. Ann's schools ; Fairfield Ward, Fairfield schools ; St. Austin's (So. 2) Ward, Infant school, Thewlis-street ; Latchford, St. Mary's school and Christ Church school ; Great Sankey, Council school; and Rixton, Church of England school, Hollins Green. Locally several of the contests are being conducted with great spirit., this being especially so in the case of Town Hall Ward, where Mr. A. J. Jolley is opposed by Mr. Marsh. The following is a list of the candidates:</p>
        <p>ST. AUST-IN'S, NO. 2. William Throssell, 676, Knutsford-road, gentleman and Rev. M. Ryan, 7, Liverpool-road.</p>
        <p>FA IRFIEL D.</p>
        <p>* Robert Bolton, 88,-"lia-n-c-1;e-srer.-road, insurance agent and May Carruthers, 310, Manchester-road, married woman.</p>
        <p>WITITECROSS. James Andrew Heywood, 6, Arpley-street, assurance agent, and James Evans, 14 and 16, Elizabeth-street, baker and grocer.</p>
        <p>BE W SE V.</p>
        <p>Agnes Simpson, 104, Bewsey-road, married woman, and * James Payton, 65 and 67, Allen-street, beer and wine seller.</p>
        <p>TOWN HALL.</p>
        <p>Alfred Benedict Marsh, 3, Rolleston-street, estate agent and * Alfred John Jolley, 16, Arpley-street, gentleman.</p>
        <p>LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>* Constance Harvey Broadbent, The Hollies, spinster, and Edwin Plinston, 194, Wilderspool Caaseway, agent.</p>
        <p>RIXTON- WITH-GLAZEBROOK. - * Alfred Brooks, Willow Bank, Glazebrook, estate agent, and James Robinson, Rixton Hall, farmer.</p>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY. William Furness, Fern Lea, commission agent, and Thomas Halton, Vine Cottage, farmer. * Indicates retiring member. The polling booth at Stockton Heath will be in St. Thomas's School and will be open the same hours as the booths in Warrington. There is a strong probability that the representation of the district on the Runcorn Board of Guardians and District Council will undergo a change. The candidates are * Ntr. James Whitley, * Mr. John Hallows,Councillor Pemberton and Mr. J. Mynekyme.</p>
        <p>The election of Parish Councillors in Sankey and Penketh will also take place on Monday, conjointly with the election of Guardians. In Sankey seven candi- dates are nominated for six seats whilst in Penketh, where the polling booth will be in the infant schools, nineteen candidates are nominated for the six seats.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. J. Jolley's Candidature. Vigorous Reply to Critics Another meeting in support of the candidature of Mr. A. J. Jolley in Town Hall Ward was held in the Kendrick-street Schools on Thursday evening. The Rev. W. Bracecarnp presided over a small attendance. Mr. JOLLEY delivered a lengthy speech and replied to what he termed" the odious and offensive insinuations" made against him, which were as wicked as they were baseless. He challenged his opponents to produce a single instance in all his fifteen years' services as Guardian where he had been a party either to the break- ing up of a home or to the refusal of out-relief to a deserving and needy person. The cost of feeding the inmates of the Workhouse and hospital averaged 2s. 6d. a week per head. If his opponents who prated about his extravagance were going to reduce this and substitute a more economical diet he would very much like to know of what it would consist. Did they advocate pigswill for paupers? That would not be a very useful election cry. (Laughter). On the Assessment Com- mittee, continued Mr. Jolley, one met with the most impudent attempts by tricks and dodges of the most despicable kind of a certain type of property owner to evade his just responsibilities and throw the burden on to the shoulders of others. The majority of property owners were honourable and straightforward but a minority were neither and it had been his duty as chair- man of the committee on more than one occasion to severely reprimand some of the latter. When promi- nent citizens and members of what he had playfully described as a rent raising association were detscted and rebuked for these mean frauds, his hearers might be sure they bore an undying hate to the man who had brains enough to detect and courage enough to rebuke their disgraceful conduct. He would not weary them with a detailed exposure of the dirty dodges of some of the low class property owners in their efforts to evade their just share of the pecuniary responsibilities of citizens of a civilised community, but part of the opposition to him was duo to his having fought against them. (Applause.) MR. JOLLEY HECKLED. A NON-TRADING QUESTION, Iklr. W. CHRIMES said it had been reported that a Committee of the Guardians bad been negotiating for sites for new offices and it was stated that one of the sites which had been under consideration either belonged to Mr..lolley or that he was in some way personally or financially interested in it. He would like to know whether that was a fact, and if so whether Mr. Jolley had voted or taken any part whatever in the negotia- tions and discussions with reference to it ? Mr. JOLLEY: I would like to ask the gentleman if he will give me the name of his informant. Mr. CHRIMES : It has been simply given as a rumour and I ask the question so as to give Mr. Jolley an oppor- tunity of replying to it. Mr. JOLLEY : I ask you to give the name of the person who has made the statement to you. It is a slanderous statement to make and I ask in the interest of fair-play for the name of the person who has told you. Mr. COOPErt T saw it in the Papers, that at a meet- ing of the Board of Guardians Mr. Bolton said Mr. Jolley had voted and he did not think he had any right to do so. Mr.JoLLEY : Voted on what.?</p>
        <p>Mr. COOPER: I don't kno;,. it is only what Mr. Bolton said. I understand they were discussing the selection of sites.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOLLEY : I tell you it is not true, (to Mr. Chrimes) : Will you favour me with the name of your informant of this slanderous statement?</p>
        <p>Mr. CHRIMES said he did not think there was any slander. He simply gave it as a rumour that was going about the town. Personally he knew nothing about it.</p>
        <p>Cauncillor WILSON said he did not think the speaker had any right to make such insinuation without giving his name and address.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOLLEY again asked for the name of Mr. Chrimes' informant.</p>
        <p>Mr. CHRIMES refused to give it. Mr. JOLLEY then asked for Mr. Chrimes' own name and address and the information was given. A similar procedure was followed with regard to Mr. Cooper, of Buttermarket-street.</p>
        <p>Mr. COOPER said that at the 13oarl of Cuardians meeting, as far as he could gather, someone proposed that a report should be given by the Sites for Offices Committee and it was seconded. There was an amend- ment that the report be not given and Mr. Bolton was reported as saying that Mr. Jolley had no right to vote. Mr. JOLLEY : Will any man in this room get up and suggest that I have ever during the fifteen years of my public life made the slightest pecuniary benefit from my connection with public bodies ? You are making a most offensive suggestion. Mr. CooPEFt said he was only repeating what one of Mr. Jolley's colleagues had said. Mr. JoLury : Is there a single man who will suggest that I have ever during my fifteen years' services either spoken, voted or taken any part in any matter in which I am pecuniarily interested ? Mr. COOPER: Personally Tam not prepared to say so. Mr. JoLLEr: Then what do you call yourself? You are making a foul insinuation. Mr. Jolley repeated his challenge.</p>
        <p>Mr. SHEPHERD asked Mr. Jolley to give a straight- forward answer to the question. Was he interested in the property which the Guardians were negotiating for? Mr. JOLLEY said he was in a rather peculiar position because the question was not public and he was bound to keep confidence with his colleagues on the Board. As anybody who read the papers would know, some few months ago an advertisement appeared stating that the Guardians were proposing to consider the erection of central offices and they asked persons having land suitable for the purpose in any part of the town to submit tenders, and all tenders submitted would be regarded as private and confidential. Acting as he did in the capacity of trustee for others in certain property, indirectly he was interested in two sites which were submitted amongst a number of others to the Guardians. In response to a resolution of the Board a report was made by Messrs. Owen to the Guardians on Wednesday. A number of sites were submitted and he being inter- ested indirectly in two of them, took no part whatever in any way or shape, directly or indirectly,in the selec- tion which had to be made out of the numerous offers received. He wished to know, supposing his colleagues, the majority of whom were businessmen, thought one of the two sites in question was the best in the interests of the retepayers as being the cheapest, what wrong would be done to the ratepayers, if it were selected? On no single occasion, either on the Board of Guardians or any other public body, had he either voted, spoken,or used the slightest influence in further- ing his own private interests either directly or indirectly. He wished to know whether his questioners were satisfied with that statement. Mr. CHRINIES and Mr. COOPER said they were perfectly satisfied. MRS. CARRUTHER'S CANDIDATURE. A meeting in support of the candidature of Mrs. Carruthers in Fairfield Ward was held in Fairfield school on Thursday evening. Mr. George Walker presided. Mr. W. Long wrote in support of Mrs. Carruthers, and the election of Women Guardians. Miss 'MARGARET ASHTON, of the Women Guardians'</p>
        <p>777-,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>WRECK OF THE JEBBA.</head>
        <p>THE WRECK OF THE JEBBA.</p>
        <p>Penketh Passengers' Trying Experiences</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Thomas Gandy and Mrs. Jenks, o Penketh, were among the passengers on the ill-fated Elder-Dempster liner " Jebba," which went ashore a little to the eastward of Bolt Tail, Devonshire, on Monday morning during a dense fog. They were returning from the Canaries and the journey was almost completed when they were called upon to undergo a really terrifying experience. The "Jebba " struck in the small hours of the morning at the foot of a steep cliff, and at once was found to be filling. By means of flash-lights and the siren, the coastguard and the fishermen at Hope Cove were brought to the scene, and after heroic efforts they established direct communication between the ship and the shore. All the passengers, who showed splendid calmness, were brought ashore by means of the boatswain's chair, and there was no approach to panic. The work of rescue was exceedingly difficult owing to the great height to which the cliffs rise at this point, and it is a matter for gratitude that not a single life was lost. A "Morning Leader" correspondent says : Amongst the rescued whom I have spoken with are Mr. and Mrs. Gandy, Mr. and Mrs. Percival, and Mrs, Jenks. All were covered from top to toe in red clay. Every member of the party was hatless. Their sodden clothing was concealed beneath heavy blankets. Torn and discoloured garments told of what these survivers had been through. Mrs. Percival spoke for the party. "When the ship struck," she said, "we were all aroused. The lights were promptly turned on all through the ship, and we were told by the officers to put on the warmest clothes we possess3d, we might have to wait about for some time.</p>
        <p>" Everybody kept quite calm, and there was no shrieking amongst the women. "The thing that surprised us most was the prompt- ness with which our signals to the shore were answered. The sailors worked splendidly, and the first man to go ashore over the rope came back to show us that it was safe for us to trust ourselves on it.</p>
        <p>"It was a terrifying experience," exclaimed the ladies, "and just look at what we suffered being dragged up the cliffs." In their hurry the ladies had evidently put on their overnight dinner gowns, and the discreet p eep beneath the salvage blankets that Vfa4 permissible revealed torn and clay-besmirched skirts beyond the powers of any cleaners to restore. Mr. Gandy was seen by an " Examiner " reporter on Wednesday, but he was still suffering from the effects of his ardu ills experience, and had no desire to recall the incidents of the terrible night. Our repre- sentative gathered from a brief conversation with him, that the passengers had passed through a very trying ordeal, as for hours they were huddled together on the vessel which was rolling heavily, and after being conveyed ashore by means of the rocket apparatus they had to be hauled up the face of the steep cliff. As Mr. Gandy remarked, it was a mercy that all were saved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>SEQUEL TO A SUICIDE. -- Claim for insurance at Warrington.</head>
        <p>SEQUEL TO A SUICIDE. -- Claim for insurance at Warrington.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before his Honour Deputy Judge Strachan, K.C., and a jury, Edward Plins ton, Broomfields Cottage, Appleton,b rought a claim against the Wesleyan and General Assurance Society, to recover £22 Is., the amount due under a policy of assurance on the life of Frederick Plinston (deceased). Mr. W. Madden (instructed by Mr. T. S. Steel) appeared for the plaintiff, Mr. A. Browae repre- senting defendants. Mr. Madden' at the outset, produced the policy, premium book, certificate of death, and letters of administration, and having done this contended that he had established a prima facie case. Mr. Browne pointed out that death was certified to be due to hemorrhag3 of the brain caused by a rifle shot. A coroner's jury returned a verdict of" Febo de se." There was no provision for suicide in the policy. Ar. Madden urged that the verdict of the coroner's jury was not conclusive and the question of the deceased's sanity must be decided by that jury. As Mr. Browne had raised the plea it was for hini. to support it. Mr. Browne said Mr. Madden had put in the docu- ment and it was for him to show that the verdict was wrong,_</p>
        <p>Hi;El.cnour ruled that the facts must be placed before the jury. Mr. Browne then proceeded to call witnesses who related the story of the tragedy previously reported and which, briefly re-started was that on the 24th January the deceased, who was a member of the Lymm Company of Volunteers, went to the Drill Hall at that village and obtained his rifle on a plea that he wanted to practice firing. The same night his dead body was I found in a second-class carriage at Lyme-street Station, Liverpool. The deceased's had the rifle between his legs and the lace of one of his boots was tied to the trigger. His mouth was shattered and there was a small hole in the top of his head. In his possession he had two undischarged cartridges. Mrs. Charlotte Clegge, Lymm, spoke to seeing the deceased at the Plough Hotel on the 24th January. Deceased was restless. He had previously told her sister-in-law that life was not worth living. William Mclntyre, Golborne-street, Warrington, said that on the 24th January, when at Lymrn Post Office, deceased came to him and asked him to arrange with his (deceased's) brother to meet him at Arpley Station, Warrington, with a cab. They then went to the Spread Eagle Inn, where the deceased wrote a note addressed to Mr. Maginn. The note (produced) read : "Please send a cab up to our house, Broomfields, and bring Ted to Arpley Station to meet the 9.43 train from Lymm due Arpley 9.58. Do not be late. Most important.—E, PLINsTox." Witness carried out these instructiots, but could not get an answer at Broomfields. This concluded the defendant's case. Edward Plinston said that the deceased had for two years been out of work and witness kept him and lent him £65, part of which was to enable dcceisod to start in business for himself. Deceased kept company with a lot of young men in a better class of life than himself with the result that he got into debt. Deceased gave up business after about two months trial and his busi- ness and other debts amounted to between £BO and £lOO Witness and his brothers tried to induce him to settle, down but he could not do so. The deceased had under- gone an operation for hernia from which he had suffered several years. Just before Christmas deceased was upset by the bailiff's of the County Court, being after him. Deceased could not sleep at night and many times had said he was tired of life. For a month before his death ae suffered from neuralgia. On the night before his death deceased slept very little. On the 24th deceased in the afternoon said "I am going to Chester to try and enlist." They went to the Patten Arms and returning to the workshnp in Arpley-street, deceased started crying, rushed at witness and kissed him, and before witness could do anything he had gone. Some two years ago witness's mother committed suicide. His father's brother died about three years ago in Rainhill Asylum and some years previously his father's cousin died in the same institution. Witness took out the policy on his brother'sl ifs to cover his (witness's) loans.</p>
        <p>Cross-examined: He had his brother insured in three societiel for a total amount of about £5O. He did not disclose the fact that the deceased was suffering from hernia in the proposal form. Mr. Browne put in a proposal form. Witness denied that the signature on the proposal was his brother's. His brother did not know that he was being insured. Mr. Clayton confessed that he proposed Mr. Browne : I have not asked about hat.</p>
        <p>Mr. sladden : The witness swears that nr. Clayton admitted that he had forged his brother's name. Mr. Browne: And yet they are trying to claim on a forged document..</p>
        <p>His Honour: They are claiming on the policy. If the witness is right you mast remember that it is your agent who is the forger. Witness : I signed a blank proposal form and gave it to Mr. Clayton. Continuing he said Mr. Clayton was aware of the insanity in the family. John William Plinston said he had often given money to the deceased. Deceased was in financial difficulties and was greatly distressed. Mr. Browne: At the inquest was a verdict of " Folo_de se" returned?</p>
        <p>His Honour : I have already ruled INIr. Browne that that question must not be put. We cannot have the Court trifled with.</p>
        <p>Elizabeth Plinston said that on the 24th January deceased was very much distressed. His Honour pointed out that unless there was on the policy of insurance aclause protecting the company from suicide, they would be bound by their undertaking to pay on death. David Clayton, the agent, gave evidence, and denied that he told the plaintiff on Friday last that the signa- ture of Frederick Plinston was a forgery. His Honour in addressing the jury, said that in a case of suicide sanity or insanity did not come into question because, whether sane or insane the company were bound to pay, not having protected themselves by stat- ing that a policy should be void in case of suicide. The jury, without leaving the box, returned a verdict for the plaintiff for the amount claimed with costs. Mr. Browne applied for a stay of execution, which was granted for twenty-one days.</p>
        <p>Almost the last words of an old Stepney man who died from heart disease accelerated by mental excite- ment were: " I don't want to die just yet ; I am coming into some money." Worry over the expected legacy had contributed to his death, said th 9 doctor at the inquest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S FIRST COUNCIL SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S FIRST COUNCIL SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>A MODEL BUILDIN G.</p>
        <p>Opening by Alderman Bennett.</p>
        <p>WArrington's first Council School—The Beamont School, Orford-avenue—was formally opened on Thursday afternoon. Considerable interest was centred in the event which marks an important advance in our local educational life. The building is a distinct ornament to the town architecturally, while it is replete with the most modern equipment. There are spacious and well-lighted rooms, bright with pictures, and everything possible has been done to ensure the comfort of scholars and teachers. The large playgrounds are a striking feature. To quote from Alderman Bennett's interesting description the school occupies a site of close upon two acres and a half. It is divided into three departments boys, girls and infants, each capable of accommodating 3GO scholars, making a total of 1,080. In addition to this the Committee have provided a school for manual instruction, capable of accommodating twenty students, and a cookery centre which will easily accommodate a further seventy-two. There are three spare plots of land reserved for nature study, one for each department; and in each of the two singularly spacious playgrounds there is a covered shed for use in wet weather; whilst the whole of the premises will be under the supervision of the caretaker, who, resides upon the spot. Every effort has been made to arrange the school upon the best and most convenient lines. Each of the three departments boasts of a central hall. Facilities for proper supervision have been carefully provided. There is a room for each head teacher looking out across the playground and tht approaches to the school and similar rooms for the assistants, and each of them will have a separate classroom, making twenty-one in all. The rooms are bright and cheerful in appearance; and are well warmed and ventilated. Special attention has been paid, indeed, to ventilation, and the committee think they have approached perfection under this parti- cular head. The floors are fireproof, the cloakrooms are extremely well arranged, and the sanitary arrange- ments the best that could be devised.</p>
        <p>Lord Stanley of Alckrley had promised toperform the opening ceremony, and it was a matter for regret that ill-health prevented his attendance. At the request of the managers Alderman Bennett, Chairman of the Education Committee, stepped into the breach and dis- charged the duties admirably. The proceedings took place in the boys' central hall, in the presence of a numerous and representative assembly. The Mayor (Alderman Smethurst), who was accompanied by the Mayoress, presided and was supported on the platform. by Alderman Bennett. the Rev. Canon Morley Steven- son, Alderman Bolton, Councillors Fulton Smith, Ball, and Wilkinson, Mr. S. P. Silcock (architect), Mr. J. Nfoore Murray (director of education), and Mr. R. B. Roberts (assistant director). The MAarott said it was with great regret that he had to apologise on behalf of the managers for the absence of Lord Stanley of Alderley. His Lordship had written to say that the doctor had forbidden him to leave his house and therefore he could not be with them. The managers had arranged for Alderman Bennett, who had been Chairman of the Committee for two and a half years, to open the school. (Applause). The Beamont school was the first Council school to be completed in the town. Tney would have noticed the large area on which it stood. This would enable future extensions to be made if required and would avoid the nececessity of purchasing land. Provision had been made for a garden, in which it was hoped the children might be taught to love and respect the flowers and plants, and at the same time it would add to the beauty of the school. A manual instruction centre—the first of its kind in the town—was included, and in this it was hoped the boys of the district would receive training of hand and eye, which would be useful to them in whatever trade or profession they took up. Further accommodation was provided for cookery pur- poses—one of the most useful subjects at present taught in the schools to girls. (Applause). Ph, curriculum of the schools would be similar to that of the other schools. A house was provided for the caretaker, giving him supervision over the buildings and enabling him to pro- tect the property. Ha believed that when those present had had an opportunity of seeing the schools they would agree that the committee were to be congratu- lated upon the result of their work and that every care had been taken to provide a useful building without nnnecessory elaborate work. Before declaring the school open, Alderman BENNETT delivered an eloquent address, which space permits us from printing. He described the buildings and paid a high compliment to the architect (Mr. S. P. Silcock), the builder (Mr. C. W. Davenport), the Clerk of Work. (Mr. Hallard), and the Director for their valuable help The land had cost £2,718 or £2 Gs. 4d. per head; the buildings £13,781 103., or £ll 15s. per head ; the fittings about 2G50, or lls, per head, a total cost per head of £l4 12s. 4d., a cost which would compare most favour- ably, conside ring the accommodation, the appearance and the various a dvantages attaching to the school with that of similar schools elsewhere. (Applause.) Councillor 310REEY STEVENSON, moved a vote of thanks to Alderman Bennett. Councillor FULTON SMITEI seconded. The vote was heartily accorded. Councillor BALI, moved a vote of thanks to the I.‘rayor. Councillor WILKINSON seconded and the vote was enthusiastically passed. This concluded the proceedings and the visitors then inspected the schools. In the evening the buildings were open for public inspection.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>EASTER HOLIDAYS</head>
        <p>EASTER HOLIDAYS</p>
        <p>Where to Spend Them.</p>
        <p>We would refer our readers to our advertisement columns in which will be found the announcement of the special cheap trips arranged by the London and North-Western Railway Company. As is their usual custom they have again arranged a large and varied list of cheap trips to suit all, and to some of the principal ones we would draw attention. On Thursday March 28th, through traias will run to Glasgow and. Edinborough by West Coast Royal Mail Route, in con- nection with which bookings at cheap rates are given 'to many of the principal stations in Scotland. To the West of England on March 28th, by the short route via. the Severn Tunnel. To York,Durhatn and Newcastle and other stations in the N.E. district. On March 28th and 30th to Dublin and numerous stations in the interior of Ireland, by the short sea route via. Holyhead. To London (Easton) :larch 28th, 30th and April let in con- nection with which cheap tickets will be issued to the Continent. Many other popular trips have been arranged to Blackpool, Lancaster, North Wales Coast stations, Birmingham, Liverpool, Manchester. In addition to the spahial facilities given, we would draw attention to the faht that the week-end tickets usually issued on Fridays and Saturdays, will be issued on Thursday March 25th, Good Friday, March 29th (where train service permits) and Saturday March 30th. Messrs. Dean and DAWSOII have arranged for excep- tional excursion facilities by the Great Central Ril- way. On Thursday, March 23. there will be special trains to London, South and West of England resorts, Midlands, and the East Coast. On Good Friday excur- sions will be run to ShelliAd, Gainsborough, Brigg, Grimsby, Hull and Cleetborpes. On Saturday, March 30th, cheap tickets will again be issued to London, South of England, East Coast and other resorts, and on Easter Monday to London. Nottingham, Sheffield,Work- sop, Lincoln, Grimsby, Sch. Farther particulars are advertised.</p>
        <p>The Great-Western Railway announce a, cheap express excursion to the West of England by direct route, for 5,8, 10 or 15 days, leaving IVarrington on Thursday next at 10.30 a.m. For particulars of fares, &amp;c., see advt. By the Cheshire Lines Railway cheap excursion tickets will be issued during the holidays to Douglas, Llandudno, Bangor, Beaumaris, Menai Bridge. Dublin, Belfast, Cork, Liverpool, Brighton, Eastham, Southport, Manchester, Knutsford, Northwich, Delamere, &amp;c. The London and North-Western and Great-Western Joint Railways have arranged excursions on Good Friday and Easter Monday to Halton, Frodsham, Helsby and Chester. Many attractions are offered to holiday makers this Easter but none will appeal with greater force than a short sojourn in Sunny Devon the County of Cider and Cream, or in Cornwall the modern Riviera. In either of these Western Counties, both equally noted for their lovely scenery, a holiday, whether short or long, is pro- ductive of a quiet yet luxurious pleasure seldom experienced. Not only are their beauties unique in character and the mild climate unequalled, but remain- ing so long more or less unexplored, pictsresque valleys and old-world towns possess the charm of fresh- ness so much sought after. For the Easter holidays there are exceptional facilities for visiting the West, and particular attention is directed to the announce- ment made by the Great-Western Railway in another column of the cheap fares to various resorts on their system—the longest in the United Kingdom.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>[By " Ovalist."] Warrington are once more moving in the direction of the Northern Union Cup. So far they have been exceptionally well favoured by the draw and should reach the third round without much difficulty. Luck is a sine qua non in cup competitions, and Warrington will need all they can get in order to repeat the success of 1905. •</p>
        <p>Saturday's match in the first round witnessed a welcome return to form by the Wirepullen. Pro- bably they touched the low-water mark the previous week-end at Swinton and Halifax and have now com- menced to rise again. Their old foes from Batley were completely vanished and the huge score of 34 points to 9 repays Warrington for many disappoint- ments suffered at the hands of the men from Mount Pleasant. The latter made their first cup-tie visit to Warrington and are not likely to forget it in a hurry. A year ago they put Warrington out of the first round by a solitary point margin, so that the order of the knock on Saturday was merited. At no time did Batley show any of their old-time cup-tie prowess, and very soon admitted themselves a beaten team,</p>
        <p>A feature of the home-team's display was the fine generalship and the success which attended it. In the first half Warrington had the benefit of a strong wind and by judicious punting they prevented the Batley men making more than an occasional excursion beyond the half-way line. The attack was mean- while pushed home cleverly and when the interval arrived they were leading by the useful margin of 13 points to 2. It was expected that Batley would make a fight of it on resuming with the wind, but War- rington actually put on 21 more points and had more of the game than before. This was a remarkable achievement and was due to tactics which seemed to have been neglected since the early part of the season. Warrington abandoned all punting and instead devoted themselves to the passing game. With the forwards getting possession, Batley scarcely ever saw the ball and had no opportunity of driving Warrington to the other end. They were quite out- classed by ,the brilliant combination of the home backs who showed all the speed :and cleverness which distinguished their play at the opening of the season; and which have been so wanting of late. A mainten- ance of the same form will carry the club a long way , on the road to the final.</p>
        <p>Individually the palm must go to Jenkins, who played a brilliant game throughout and made nearly all the openings from which tries and goals came so frequently. He has never been seen to better advan- tage and is, to-day, an undoubted treasure to Warrington. Hockenhall shaped better at the base of the scrum than was expected, and I think the position suits him better than the outside one. His feat in scoring three tries was one for which he deserves high praise. Fish also showed evidence of the general rejuvenation and was very•nearly his old irrepressible self. The cleverness and resource are all there, and the speed is coming. There is no doubt his presence has a big influence on the play of the team, and especially when he is a trier as on Saturday. Taylor filled Dickenson's place admirably and showed that understanding with his wing that is so essential. Dickenson's greatest fault is individualism and a tendency to wander away from Brooks. The forwards were splendid, and under the clever leadership of Thomas and Heath, quite out- rnanceuvred the Batley six. The latter have a formid- able reputation, but they met their masters on Saturday. In fact, Wattie Davies and the full back seemed the only class men on the visiting side, and they had little opportunity of stemming the tide. The draw for the second round took place in Man- chester on Monday night with the following result : Broughton Rangers v. Leeds. Salford v. Wigan. Halifax v. Oldham. York v. Swinton. Warrington v. Hull. Whitehaven v. Keighley. Huddersfield v. Hull Kingston Rovers. Wakefield v. Dewsbury. The matches will take place to-day on the grounds of the first-named club.</p>
        <p>Warrington people are delighted that the draw for the second round has left Warrington at home again, with Hull as visitors. The match Is not only an attractive one, but it should result in a substantial victory, for Warrington have already beaten the Third Porters twice twice this season by 33-7 and 0-5. Nevertheless Hull will probably prove harder to beat than Batley, for they are a speedier and cleverer team. Their weakness on the defence has been their undoing during the present season.</p>
        <p>After Saturday's fine display there could not be much change in the Warrington team, and in fact there is only one alteration, Lees taking up his old place in preference to Hockenhall. The latter has been too erratic this season or his fine performance against Batley might have secured him his place. Lees is less .speedy but more resourceful and could hardly have been dropped if perfectly fit. The selected side is therefore :</p>
        <p>Tilley, full back; Fish, Isherwood, Taylor and Brooks, threequarters ; Lees and Jenkins, half-backs: Thomas, Boardman, Shugars, Heath, Belton and Harmer, forwards.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>NOTES FROlf HULL.-</head>
        <p>NOTES FROlf HULL.-</p>
        <p>A Hull corr-soonclent sends me the following: Though the Hull directors are unable to place their full strength in the field against Warrington, they will have a very serviceable side on duty. They are look- ing forward to making this third meeting with the " Wirepullers " this season pay for all, for the Third Porters have not got over the humiliation they experi- enced at Wilderspool on September Bth, and they are still smarting from the effects of the reverse Warring- ton served out to them in front of their own supporters on January 12th. Owing to his indiscre- tion against Liverpool City last Saturday Cook, the ex-Gloucester centre, has been suspended over the match, and in addition Burchell, the county half, is stil horg 1e combat. The former' position will be filled by Willie Hall, the old Lancaster three-quarter, whose inclusion is expected to strengthen the defence. Larkins, a local youth and a certain tackler, will partner Wallace, the ex-Durham County man, who is a box of tricks and a very daring man on the attack.</p>
        <p>The forwards are a good sound lot who can gPt the ball out quickly, and also travel well in the loose. Harry Taylor, whose presence generally means so much to his side, will he in his old position under the Posts, and the Visitors' defence will be anything but the weak affair its record in the League table makes it out to be. Taylor is confident that he has a side under his command capable of upsetting the calculations of their rivals if the latter imagine that the seaporters are the team of a few months back. It is not gener- ally known that P Carvill, the black-polled Hull for- ward, holds the record for scoring by a front rank man in the Northern Union. This season o far he has obtained 20 tries and placed 17 goals (91 points).</p>
        <p>Hull have not played a Northern Union cun-ti away from the Boulevard since they were defeated 1)7 Halifax in the semi-final of 1903. This hnd induced them to take thingi'eaqily in the past and suffer several unlooked for defeats. Now that they have at length been called upon to leave the banks of the Humber they will take nothing for granted. The players have trained hard and are very enthusiastic about their chances.</p>
        <p>The teams will be :—H. Taylor (captain), back ; T. Jenkins, W. Hall. E. Jonf s, and E. Roger, three- quarter barks H. Wallace and Larking, half backs ; J. Major, VT. Carroll, W. Banks. H. Parks, P. Carvill, and J. Kilburn, forwards.</p>
        <p>Warrington have a very important mach on Monday when they go to Runcorn to fulfill the fixture which was really dosvn for to-day. Points will, of course count both in the Northern League and the South- West Lancashire League. If Warrington • win or draw they will secure the West Lancashire Cull, so they have every inducement to try. The kick-off has been fixed for 5.30 p.m.</p>
        <p>The South-West Lancashire League up-to-date is: Pts.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. For Agst. Pts. W'gton.. 9 ... 7 ... 1. ... 1 ... 134 69 ... 15 Runcorn 8 6 2 ... 0 ... 117 70 ... 12 Wigan... 8 ... 5 ... 3 ... 0 ... 158 ... 72 ... 10 Leigh ... 10 ... 3 ... 6 ... 1 ... 114 ... 115 ... 7 Widnes.. 9 ... 3 ... 6 ... 0 ... 80 ... 214 ... 6 S.Helens 9 ... 2 ... 7 ... 0 ... 62 ... 125 ... 4</p>
        <p>Other important matches on lionday are Swinton Wigan and Leigh v. Oldham. It has been decided in case of a drawn game in the semi-final of the League Cup that the club which had not choice of ground for the first match shall have choioe of ground for the second.</p>
        <p>• DON'T DELAY IF YOU FEEL "OUT OF SORTS Or if you have a friend whose constitution has _been weakened, whether by overwork of brain or body by exposure to the weather, by working long hours in ill-ventilated rooms, by recent sickness, or by any cause tending to weaken the bodily powers; in a word, if he is one who suffers from any form of complaint which may be the result of any of the above or similar causes; if, for instance, you can see that he is troubled with Indigestion, the bane of our Country and of modern civilization; or, if you think he has Low Spirits, that sure sign of some Nervous Disorder, bringing in its train a host of other complaints such as Melancholy, Lassitude ezc., or, perhaps, Neuralgia, and frequently, Sleeplessness, which of itself is sufficient to weaken the strongest constitution, and to make life a burden; or, if being free from these, he is regarded by his friends or himself as being of a delicate constitution, subject to Bronchitis, Coughs, Colds, a tendency to Consumption, or other Chest Affections, and if he, like thousands of others, failed to get relief from other remedies,tell him he need not be disheartened, that there is one Remedy whose virtues are so great, that it has succeeded where others have failed, and that there is hope he may be cured,as thousands of others have been cured beforehira by making a sensible and timely use of Gwilym Evans Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic. If your friend then gives it a fair trial, he will be fully convinced, SS others have been, that this is the Best Remedy of The Age. You or your friend may ask "What is Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters ?" It is a Preparation now extensively taken throughout the country by patients suffering from Debility, Nervousness, and General Exhaustion, and if any value be attached to human testimony, the efficacy of this medicine has been suc- cessfully established. Its claims have been tested and proved by the medical profession and others, and corro- borated by the written testimonials of eminent men. • Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters contain not only a suitable quantity of Quinine in each dose, but the active principles of the following well-known herbs— Sarsaparilla, Saffron, Gentian, Lavender, Burdock, - and Dandelion. The use of Quinine is well known but it has never been satisfactorily combined with these preparations until after overcoming considerable difficulties, the proprietor was able to secure a perfectly uniform preparation, combining all the essential properties of the above plants in their greatest purity and concentration. It is now estab- lished as a family medicine,and is increasing in popular favour the more it is known and tested. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is a tonic " Pick-me-up " scientifically mixed in happy proportions. Sold everywhere in bottles, 2s. 9d. and 4s. 6d. each. Should any difficulty be experienced in procuring Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, it will be forwarded carriage free for the above prices by the Sole Pro- ' prietors : The Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company-, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales. We particularly caution our readers against imitators, and impress upon them the importance of always seeing that the name 1" Gwilym Evans" is on the Label, Stamp,and Bottle without which none is genuine,</p>
