      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>"A SOCIALIST RELIGION."</head>
        <p>"A SOCIALIST RELIGION."</p>
        <p>Vigorous Exposition at Warrington.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Independent Labour Party were fortunate in securing for Thursday the services of Mr. Victor Grayson, a well-known Socialist lecturer, to deliver an address in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey- street. Mr. E. Osborne presided over a large and representative gathering and at the outset briefly ex- plained that the I.L.P. stood for an industrial common- wealth founded upon the socialization of land and capital. The subject of Mr. GRAYSON'S address was "A Socialist religion" and for nearly an hour and a half ho commanded the undivided attention of his audience. Displaying a scholarly command of language he delivered a carefully reasoned address illumin- ated by flashes of humour and effective personal touches. Socialists, he said, were met by two classes of people. The one said" You Socialists are idealists, dreamers of dreams, poets; you cannot come down to the concrete facts of existence," whilst the other said "If only you had a vision. You are</p>
        <p>always grubbing amonethe swill tubs. It-Cs-bre-ad-and- butter with you. The municipalisation of this and the municipalisation of that." Whilst they were writhing on the horns of a dilemma created by those two charges they realized that both were correct, that the true Socialist had the capacity to scale the heights of vision and see the vistas of the future,ant, also to grapple with the hard tangible facts of life. They were often told not to mix business and religion, but the Rev. R. J. Campbell came along with a new theology—a new theology which was old, and which they had long preached—that religion and life were inextric- ably bound together, and that when a religion became a thing for private exercise out of the workshops it stood self-condemned and must die. "The whole of the functions of life" continued the speaker, "are bound up together in a most remarkable manner and we cannot fulfil one social or private function without putting our hands into the deadly' work which is making the lives of so many of our brethren a hell on earth. If I buy a coat, all things being equal, I buy the cheaper article. The cost of pro- duction is the law which regulates the cheapening of commodities and the most important element in this iS the cost of labour, so that if an article is cheapened it is cheapened at the expense of the earnings of labour." Continuing he gave some personal observations of the operatioms of the sweating home work system in Man- chester which brought about the reduction in the price of articles. " People " he said "are being practically murdered by the conditions of industry imposed upon them and you cannot buy a cheap coat without playing a part in the degredation of your fellow men. How are you going to harmonise cheap labour with the doctrine of the brotherhood of man which so long as it remains inde- finite and undefined all humanity will proclaim? Old age is cast on the scrap heap and feels itself a curse and an incubus on unwilling shoulders because the country stands idly by and says it can do nothing. Old age Pensions are not strong enough. Starving,hungry, ill- clad children do not tear the heart strings sufficiently. The money for these cannot be found but if to-morrow we wanted to open a new market in Thibet and had to indulge in an expensive war for the purpose the money would be found. There never was a time in the history of the world that the organized capital- istic governments have failed to find the money for a war to open up new markets. Before we develop our missionary enterprise and create a surplus morality which we wish to export would it not he better to look at our own country and try and feed the little children and give the workers a greater part in the system to which they are supposed to subscribe." Continning, be said that modern society was such that _ _</p>
        <p>-it was impossible to do good at one end withoutcausing ill in some other part. The local Councils could not give employment to the unemployed without creating unemployed and the horror of the whole system was that it was impossible to move two steps in the direc- tion of social reform without taking two steps in the direction of social retrogression. "The mocking Jew standing at the Cross upon which the Great Personality was crucified who cried 'He saved others. Himself He cannot save' expressed the root factor of the Socialist religion. If you would save others you must be willing to sacrifice yourselves. If you would enjoy life you must abolish selfish pleasure, selfish indulgencies and see that your salvation depends upon the salvation of others. Drop your petty little isms and petty little ologies realise that in-this religion there is some- thing which will save our suffering men and women. Libertv,Equality and Fraternity. Those are the guiding faiths that the Socialist would make concrete facts,' (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1907-03-23_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
