        <p>Pat Mach-Travelled Princess.— Princess Victoria atrieia of Connaught, who has just celebrated her tw T-first birthday, is one of the most travelled of our Princesses. Last year she accompanied the Duke and Duchess of Connaught on a tour of South Africa, and bee spent the recent winter with her Royal parents all the visit to Ceylon, India, and the Far East. The r/incess is the youngest of three children of the Duke Duchess of Connaught, and was born on St. Patrick's ;--413', a fact happily commemorated at her baptism. The !Jountess of Crewe is also named after a coincidence of her birth, and bears the quaint name of Etrenne. It comes from the French " etrennes," the gifts which ~everybody in France gives to everybody else at the New ear, and Lady Crewe was born on New Year's Day. I_Globe Trotters.—Among the fortunate people who 'l,t.ve been spending the winter months in summer cluzes, are the Marquis and Marchioness of Hertford, 'Who have been a visit to India and Ceylon, and fife not e/Pected back until the end of April. It was the fourth karquis who left the family treasures away from the tle, and which now form the famous Wallace Collec- tion at Hertford House. The present Lord Hertfor is th. e sixth Marquis, and his principal seat is at Bagley ark, near Alcester, in Warwickshire. His eldest son, o Earl of Yarmouth, also resides in the neighbour- ...ood, where Lady Yarmouth has many friends. The -tad has been visiting the Riviera during his wife's ,tbsence in America, where she has been undergoing the 'mg ordeal of frequently being present at the Thaw trial. 44uTehe Queen of Spain and her Court.—The youthful lell of Spain—not yet out of her teens—naturally ilde the excessive formalities of Spanish Court Inlquette lather trying. She is always surrounded by .".leinordinate number of Ladies-in-Waiting, of whom i re are about thirty or forty, and they are so imbued a sense of lofty dignity, that it is at all times cult for the Queen to follow the natural inclinations Z3I nae girl reared in the freedom of English homelife. appro°,f, the things the Ladies-in-Waiting mostly dis- reportle,,°S is the Queen's love of fresh air, and it was the dO-4 the other day that no fewer that sixteen of her AT 9rt attendants were laid up with colds owing to open aJ th rreltT's habit of requiring all e windows to be ref o - ."-La" Alfonso has essayed the part of a palace Pl _ rmer, bbut the regime of the Grandees is tenacious, at the task is not an easy one. He has not succeeded .1 greatly relieving the Queen of the burden of exoes- awe pompand ceremony,and the presence of multitudin- pus attendants, but apparently Queen Victoria Euenie's love of fresh air is having more effect. The Irish Industries Sale.—Many of the leading 'women in Irish society, assisted at the Irish Industries Sale at the Ritz Hotel in London this week. Lady Londonderry specially asked for the help of all who 'desire to promote the cottage industries of Ireland, and there is reason to believe that the sale was one of the testever held. There have been in all thirty-three sales during the past twenty ' years and over £240,000 has been realised by the Association for the workers in the 'various industries. Unfortunately there are still num- bers of fashionable women who prefer to go to Paris for their materials, and articles of clothing, which could he equally well supplied from home manufacture. The Irish lace is as beautiful as any made on the Continent, and so also are the crochet, embroideries, fine lingerie, and needlework which constituted some of the attrac- tions at the Irish Sale. If fashionable women would "online their custom to goods of home manufacture, Instead of going to Paris and Vienna for them, much %ore might be done to benefit the home workers in elle various industries. Ribbons.—Sever have ribbons been'as fascinating 111-4,:",e5' are this spring. Shot effects in which green is always present, lead, then come the beautiful erenlntry ribbons, which at the moment, are being for'n„led for sashes, as well as millinery. Plaid ribbons tarte plain hats intended for wear with severe he ;`,T blades "are also very modish, and the same may of the thin, soft,pliant satin ribbons. Pumpadour tieu;aB are still in high favour, and promise to con- -80 throughout the summer. Modes.—ln the matter of headgear,and wraps, 14e apparently a. little mad this spring. The latest -'8 in the milliAery world, are with few exceptions, rlded in worn hanging off the back of the head, and e feathers are so arranged as to give one the impres- lisua that the worker had just shut her eyes and fixed 1 Val at the spot she touched first. The effect afforded these waving plumes in the high March winds, is .fgell ludicrous to a degree. Then the new ooatees are ' atyallently more curious than pretty. The favourites moment are doubtless inspired by the hanging Vete worn by some of the officers in Montenegro, ial other mid-European States of that still half-wild e• For the woman who seldom wears a gown, wrap ar. "at, more than a few times, these eccentric notions CI all very well, moreover she is able in every parti- te-am, to dress up to them, but those among us who have to d" such purchases more times than we should care °taint. will do better to keep to plainer and neater nlodea, Night-Caps.—When the modern night-cap first „I)Peared, some two' or three season's back, few people ' '.73Pected the idea to last, but it has, and to-day no 2rousseau is considered complete that does not include liiset of night-caps. These are usually of the 1:41:4 type, tr, frills of lace falling well over the hair in front, and ftyd_Vmay down the neck behind. Some tie, and some are ih-eu by invisible means, and all prove eminently becom- e-g- Fine ringed or spotted net is the fabric usually ,steploYed for them, real Valenciennes, or any other lace I,eferred, being used for the frills. Needless to say, l'abons find a place on all these dainty trifles. Collars Fashionable.—lt is impossible to lament the 12444 of the collarless gown, our necks, like our arms, 7PPearing to greater advantage covered, during the day- 'ne• We have returned to the moderately high collar, either transparent or semi-transparent, and well-boned " the sides a 13,dbae k. o theT majority of women the collar is most becoming, and in very much better "ate than the rows of pearl beads, that for some con- siderable time did duty for it. Is Trying on Boots and Shoes.—When fitting on a pair of 'Tots or shoes, it is best always to stand up and walk aoout, as the boot or shoe that feels comfortable enough when sitting down after pinches or feels un- comfortable when the wearer throws his or her weight it. Happily our own boot and shoe makers now copy ltiie excellent American notion of supplying many gradua- 'ons of size, so that it is possible for almost every ,pot to find just what it requires, viz., a boot neither a -uttle too large, nor that intensely miserable size, one tliat is a little too small. ' Improving" the natural beauty of Flowers.- -Minting the lily is apparently not such a futile art as we have always supposed, and it seems that many of the out flowers we buy in the spring are dyed, or " faked " thestalks being placed in a solution of dye, which is absorbed into the blossom. Lilies of the valley, carna- tions and daffodils, which figure so plentifully, among</p>
        <p>INFLUENZA'S original foe. Is still repelling INFLUENZA</p>
        <p>our Easter decorations, are some of the varieties corn- monly treated in this way. The popular demand for out flowers ha 's introduced much that is artificial in their production. Folklore and legend are alike defied. There is the old saying that " flowers out of season" mean "sorrow out of reason " but we buy spring blossoms at Christmas, and can apparently have them of any shade of colouring we prefer, just the same as we buy. the artificial substitutes for our hats. MADGE. THE BEGGAR'S COUGH. At the Warrington County Sessions, on Wednesday Michel Mee and his wife, Julia Mee, who for the hat fortnight have been living at Earlestown, were charged with neglecting their four children, and further with causing their eldest daughter to beg. For the prose- cution Mr. .J.W.Knowles stated that for some months the defendants had been tramping the country, and the children were badly neglected. Their eldest child aged ten, was sent out on the streets at night to solicit alms, and so as to excite sympathy when asking for money she was told by her father to cough. The male prisoner said that his children had only gone to " rack "since he got out of regular employment. The magistrates sent him to gaol for three months and the woman was ordered to be detained in the workhouse.</p>
